{"parentUuid":"3a1ac540-5ad1-4220-86da-690047f63486","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"1fbb447d-9642-4c7b-9881-20ad5bd7f8d0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Looking at the dispatch_frame function, the issue is:\n\n```rust\nlet frame_type = line\n    .split('\"')\n    .skip(1)\n    .next()\n    .unwrap_or(\"\")\n    .to_string();\n```\n\nFor a frame like `{\"type\":\"register\",\"kind\":\"Agent\",\"name\":\"...\",\"pid\":1234}`:\n- `.split('\"')` splits on `\"`\n- `.skip(1)` skips the first element (the part before the first `\"`, which is `{` — wait, actually `split('\"')` on `{\"type\":\"register\",...}` gives:\n  - `{` (before first `\"`)\n  - `,type` (between `\"` and `\"` of `\"type\"`) — no wait...\n  \n  Let me think more carefully. `{\"type\":\"register\"}` split by `\"`:\n  - `{` (before `\"type\"`)\n  - `type` (between `\"type\"`'s opening and closing `\"`)\n  - `:` (between closing `\"` of `type` and opening `\"` of `register`)\n  - `register` (the value)\n  - `}` (after closing `\"` of `register`)\n  \n  So `.skip(1).next()` would give `type` — which is the field NAME, not the value!\n\nThis is the bug described in the summary. For `{\"type\":\"register\",\"kind\":\"Agent\",\"pid\":1234}`:\n- `.split('\"')` gives: `{`, `type`, `:`, `register`, `,kind:`, `Agent`, `,pid:1234}`\n- `.skip(1).next()` gives `type` (the field name), NOT `register` (the value)\n\nWait, let me re-examine. `\"type\"` appears as the first quoted string. Let me re-count indices:\n\n```\n{\"type\":\"register\",\"kind\":\"Agent\",\"name\":\"...\",\"pid\":1234}\n```\n\nSplit by `\"`:\n1. `{` (before first `\"`)\n2. `type` (between opening and closing `\"` of field name)\n3. `:` (after closing `\"` of `type`, before opening `\"` of `register`)\n4. `register` (the value of type)\n5. `,kind:` (after closing `\"` of `register`, before `\"kind\"`)\n6. `Agent` (value of kind)\n7. `,name:` (etc.)\n8. `...` (value of name)\n9. `,pid:1234}`\n10. (if there are more quotes...)\n\nSo `.skip(1)` = skip `{`, `.next()` gives `type` (the field name).\n\nThis IS the bug! The dispatch_frame gets `\"type\"` (the JSON key name) instead of `\"register\"` (the value).\n\nActually wait, I need to re-read. Let me trace `split` more carefully.\n\n`\"{\\\"type\\\":\\\"register\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"Agent\\\"}\".split('\"')` \n\nIn Rust string terms, the string is `{\"type\":\"register\",\"kind\":\"Agent\"}`.\n\nSplit by `\"`:\n- Before first `\"`: `{`\n- Between first `\"` and second `\"`: `type`\n- Between second `\"` and third `\"`: `:`\n- Between third `\"` and fourth `\"`: `register`\n- Between fourth `\"` and fifth `\"`: `,kind:`\n- Between fifth `\"` and sixth `\"`: `Agent`\n- After sixth `\"`: `}`\n\nIteration through `.split('\"')`:\n1. `{`\n2. `type`\n3. `:`\n4. `register`\n5. `,kind:`\n6. `Agent`\n7. `}`\n\nSo `.skip(1)` skips `{`, `.next()` gives `type`.\n\nThis confirms the bug. The frame_type will be `\"type\"` (the key name), not `\"register\"` (the value). So it hits the `_` case and returns \"unknown frame type: type\".\n\nBut wait, the summary says \"it extracts 'type' instead of the command value\" which is confirmed by my analysis above.\n\nNow the question is: what about `line.contains(\"Agent\")` for the kind? That works because it just checks if \"Agent\" appears anywhere in the JSON, not relying on parsing the first field.\n\nSo the dispatch frame parsing is completely broken. The `frame_type` will always be `\"type\"` (the key), and it will never match any of the expected commands.\n\nFix: I need to either:\n1. Use serde_json to parse the frame properly\n2. Change the split logic to grab the value (e.g., skip 3 elements to get to the value: `.skip(1)` skips `{`, `.next()` gets `type`, `.skip(1)` gets `:`, `.next()` gets `register` — so skip 3 from start)\n\nActually simpler: `line.split('\"').nth(3)` would give `register` (the value after `{\"type\":\"`):\n- `{\"type\":\"register\",...}` split by `\"`:\n  - Index 0: `{`\n  - Index 1: `type`\n  - Index 2: `:`\n  - Index 3: `register`\n\nSo `.nth(3)` would get the value. Let me fix this.\n\nActually wait, looking more carefully, `.skip(1).next()` is equivalent to `.nth(1)`. The bug is that they used `skip(1)` instead of `skip(3)`. The simplest fix is `line.split('\"').nth(3)`.\n\nHmm, but could this be fragile? In valid JSON, the `\"type\"` field might not always be the first field. Let me check what frames are being sent:\n\nFrom dsx-agent/main.rs:\n- `{\"type\":\"register\",\"kind\":\"Agent\",\"name\":\"dsx-agent\",\"pid\":123}` — `type` is first\n- `{\"type\":\"unregister\",\"pid\":123}` — `type` is first\n\nFrom HP itself:\n- `{\"type\":\"register\",\"kind\":\"Tui\",\"name\":\"dsx-hp\",\"pid\":123}` — `type` is first\n\nSo in practice, `type` is always the first field in our codebase. But fixing via `nth(3)` is fragile. Better to just use serde for this.\n\nBut the user said \"不动底层逻辑\" (don't touch underlying logic). So a minimal fix that works is better.\n\nLet me think about the minimal fix:\n1. Use serde_json::from_str to parse `{\"type\":\"register\"}` pattern — but that requires defining structs or using Value. That's more code.\n2. Use `nth(3)` — one character change, but fragile if field order changes.\n3. Change the frame format so `type` is `command` — but that's a bigger change.\n\nActually, the simplest fix is to use `serde_json::Value`. Something like:\n\n```rust\nfn dispatch_frame(line: &str, service: &Mutex<HealthService>) -> String {\n    let frame_type: String = serde_json::from_str::<serde_json::Value>(line)\n        .ok()\n        .and_then(|v| v.get(\"type\").and_then(|v| v.as_str().map(String::from)))\n        .unwrap_or_default();\n    // ... rest stays the same\n}\n```\n\nThis is a one-function change with minimal footprint. Let me do this.\n\nActually, let me think about what else needs to be done. The user said \"收尾确保今天可以正常使用\" (wrap up and ensure it can be us