{"parentUuid":"876a4489-9c05-495a-8d30-e062ed75e096","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"5b472eae-41f4-4a5b-9eaa-c7a85adeb7a5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so we see \"dsx-tui: agent started\" and \"dsx-agent starting\" but NOT \"dsx-agent: tools → ...\" or \"dsx-agent: HP not running\" etc. This means the agent started but got stuck somewhere.\n\nTwo possibilities:\n1. The agent couldn't find the `dsx-tools` binary next to `dsx-agent` and crashed\n2. The agent is stuck waiting for something\n\nLet me look at agent's startup:\n```rust\n// 2. Spawn dsx-tools\nlet exe = std::env::current_exe().unwrap();\nlet tools_path = exe.with_file_name(\"dsx-tools\");\nlet mut tools_child = Command::new(&tools_path)\n    .stdin(Stdio::piped())\n    .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n    .spawn()\n    .expect(\"dsx-agent: failed to spawn dsx-tools\");\n```\n\nThe agent tries to find `dsx-tools` in the same directory as itself. When running via `./target/release/dsx-tui`, the agent is at `./target/release/dsx-agent`, so tools would be at `./target/release/dsx-tools`. That should exist since we built with `--release`.\n\nWait, but the agent stderr doesn't show \"tools → ...\" which would appear after reading the ToolsReady frame:\n```rust\nlet ready = read_frame(&mut tools_reader).unwrap_or_default();\neprintln!(\"dsx-agent: tools → {ready}\");\n```\n\nSo either:\n1. Tools didn't start (tools binary not found?)\n2. Tools started but didn't send Ready frame\n3. Tools started but agent's read of the Ready frame timed out or blocked\n\nAlso, we don't see \"dsx-agent: cannot connect HP\" message, which would have appeared if the port file couldn't be read or connection failed. So either the agent couldn't reach the HP connection code, or it connected but didn't print the expected message.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The agent startup sequence is:\n1. `eprintln!(\"dsx-agent starting\");` — YES, we see this\n2. Read hp.port to get port — would get 41533\n3. Connect to HP at that port — `hp_port()` returned the port, `TcpStream::connect` should succeed\n4. `eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: cannot connect HP: ...\")` only if connect fails\n5. If connected, register with HP via the `register` frame\n6. Spawn dsx-tools\n7. Read ToolsReady frame\n\nWait, let me re-read the agent code more carefully.\n\nActually, I notice: the agent registers with HP, THEN spawns tools. But `stdin` in the agent is locked:\n\n```rust\nlet stdin = std::io::stdin();\nlet mut tui_reader = BufReader::new(stdin.lock());\n```\n\nThis line locks stdin. If the TUI -> agent pipe is set up, the agent's stdin IS connected to the TUI's child stdin. When the TUI sends the `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"hello\"}`, the agent reads it.\n\nBut wait - the agent is spawned by the TUI. The agent's stdout is connected to the TUI's agent_reader. The agent's stdin is from the TUI's agent_writer. The agent's stderr inherits from the TUI (which inherits from the terminal).\n\nSo the flow should be:\n1. TUI spawns agent (stdin piped, stdout piped, stderr inherit)\n2. Agent starts, prints \"dsx-agent starting\" (stderr)\n3. Agent reads hp.port -> gets 41533\n4. Agent connects to HP at 127.0.0.1:41533\n5. Agent registers with HP -> frame sent\n6. Agent spawns tools as child with stdin/stdout piped\n7. Agent reads ToolsReady from tools\n8. Agent then enters main loop, waiting for TUI stdin\n9. TUI writes \"hello\" frame to agent's stdin\n10. Agent reads it, echoes back\n11. TUI reads the echo, tries to loop forever\n\nBut we're not seeing step 7 (\"tools → ...\"). WHY?\n\nPossibilities:\n- dsx-tools binary not found at the expected path\n- dsx-tools binary crashes on start\n- dsx-tools binary starts but doesn't send Ready frame\n\nLet me check if the dsx-tools binary exists and can run.","signature":"5b472eae-41f4-4a5b-9eaa-c7a85adeb7a5"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":183,"cache_cr