{"parentUuid":"4324d716-b932-494e-b914-0d12b4dd52a5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"e9b34fa6-6444-4ff5-bbc8-bef0b581ca81","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `timeout` command doesn't exist on this system. The previous test worked because `/tmp/dsx/tui.log` showed output, but maybe that was from the first attempt. Let me check if there's a different way to run it — maybe use `gtimeout` or just run without timeout.\n\nWait, but earlier the `timeout` command worked: `echo \"hello\" | XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp timeout 5 ./target/release/dsx-tui 2>/tmp/dsx/tui.log; echo \"exit=$?\"` returned exit=124 (which IS the timeout return code). So `timeout` IS available but `timeout` as a separate command might not be — let me check the PATH.\n\nActually, looking more carefully:\n- Previous command: `echo \"hello\" | XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp timeout 5 ./target/release/dsx-tui` — this worked (exit 124)\n- Current command: `echo '...' | timeout 5 XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp ./target/release/dsx-agent` — timeout command not found\n\nHmm, the difference is the placement of the env var. In the first one, `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp timeout 5 ./target/release/dsx-tui`, the env var is set BEFORE `timeout`. In the second, `timeout 5 XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp ./target/release/dsx-agent`, the env var is AFTER `timeout` and `timeout` doesn't recognize env vars in that position.\n\nActually no, that's not how bash works. In bash, `VAR=val cmd` sets an env var for `cmd`. So `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp timeout 5 ./target/release/dsx-agent` sets XDG_RUNTIME_DIR for the timeout command, which passes it to the agent.\n\nBut `timeout 5 XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp ./target/release/dsx-agent` — here `timeout` receives `5` as the duration, then `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/tmp` is treated as a COMMAND, which doesn't exist.\n\nSo the fix is: always put env vars BEFORE the command name. Let me fix this.","signature":"e9b34fa6-6444-4ff5-bbc8-bef0b581ca81"}],"stop_reason":"t