   input.clear();\n    print!(\"> \");\n    let _ = std::io::stdout().flush();\n    if stdin.read_line(&mut input).is_err() || input.is_empty() {\n        break;\n    }\n    let msg = input.trim();\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, this would break on the first empty line too. I need to differentiate between \"empty input\" (just a newline) and \"EOF\" (no data at all).\n\nActually, when read_line returns Ok(0), the buffer is unchanged (still empty after clear). When it returns Ok(1), it has read \"\\n\" (a blank line). So I can check if the buffer is empty vs just whitespace:\n\n```rust\nif stdin.read_line(&mut input).is_err() {\n    break;\n}\nif input.trim().is_empty() {\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nThe issue is: `input.is_empty()` would be true when read_line returned Ok(0) (EOF), but `input.trim().is_empty()` would also be true for a blank line (input = \"\\n\"). Both should `continue` or `break`? \n\n- EOF (pipe closed) → break the loop\n- Blank line (user pressed Enter with no text) → continue\n\nThe cleanest fix: check `is_err()` → break. Check `input.trim().is_empty()` → if also `input.is_empty()` (EOF), break; else continue.\n\nActually, the simplest approach is just to use the number of bytes read:\n\n```rust\nmatch stdin.read_line(&mut input) {\n    Err(_) | Ok(0) => break,\n    Ok(_) => {\n        let msg = input.trim();\n        if msg.is_empty() { continue; }\n        // process msg\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis handles all cases:\n- Error → break\n- EOF (Ok(0)) → break  \n- Empty line (Ok(1), input = \"\\n\") → continue\n- Real input → process","signature":"4b58bcc7-64b4-415c-93a4-e72888b1c682"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":308,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":65152,"output_tokens":1110,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standa