{"parentUuid":"b33a2f5e-ea7c-4644-ba13-8daf05868e39","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/tmp/dsx/hp.log","snippet":"1\tdsx-hp: listening on 127.0.0.1:37247 (timeout=30s)\n2\tdsx-hp: connection closed: 127.0.0.1:35902"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"13b15e42-1b99-4b67-aeb5-2d270242f298","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:17:46.222Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.141","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
{"parentUuid":"13b15e42-1b99-4b67-aeb5-2d270242f298","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"7b327378-eba6-41d7-9290-d3eb4e4fe4f0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now the TUI handles EOF gracefully! Instead of infinite `> > > > > >`, it exits properly. Let me trace through what happened:\n\n1. TUI starts, spawns agent, prints \"dsx v4.0.0-alpha1 — type /help for commands, Ctrl+D to exit\"\n2. Shows `> ` prompt\n3. Reads \"hello\" from stdin\n4. Sends to agent: `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"hello\"}`\n5. Reads response from agent: `{\"type\":\"echo\",\"data\":...}`  and `{\"type\":\"done\"}`\n6. Prints `← ...` for both\n7. Shows `> ` again\n8. Tries to read next line — but stdin pipe is closed (echo \"hello\" done)\n9. `read_line` returns Ok(0) (EOF)\n10. `match Err(_) | Ok(0) => break` — exits the loop\n11. Falls through to `child.wait()` and exits\n\nExit code 124 is from `timeout 5` — the TUI exited by itself from EOF, but timeout 5 still killed it after the fact... wait, no. If the TUI exited by itself, `timeout` wouldn't kill it and the exit code would be 0. But we got exit 124, meaning timeout killed it.\n\nAh, looking at the output: TUI printed:\n```\n>   ← {\"type\":\"echo\",\"data\":...}\n  ← {\"type\":\"done\"}\n> \n```\n\nThe last `> ` is printed AFTER the response loop, because the loop went back to the top:\n- ReadLine, break on EOF\n- Falls through to the bottom of child.wait()\n\nWait, but if the read returns EOF, we break immediately. The `> ` should NOT be printed. Let me re-check...\n\nActually, looking carefully at the output:\n```\ndsx v4.0.0-alpha1 — type /help for commands, Ctrl+D to exit\n>   ← {\"type\":\"echo\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"hello\"}}\n  ← {\"type\":\"done\"}\n> === TUI exit: 124 ===\n```\n\nThe output shows:\n1. Welcome message\n2. `> ` prompt + `← echo` + `← done` response\n3. Another `> ` prompt\n4. Then the \"=== TUI exit: 124 ===\"\n\nSo there ARE two `> ` prompts. The first one is for the \"hello\" input. The second one appears because:\n- After processing \"hello\" and its response, the loop returns to the top\n- Prints `> ` (second prompt)\n- Calls `read_line`, which returns Ok(0) (EOF)\n- The match breaks the loop\n- But the `> ` was already printed before the read\n\nSo we get one extra `> ` prompt. This is a minor cosmetic issue — not a bug really, since in interactive mode the user wouldn't trigger EOF by piping.\n\nLet me also check: the exit is 124 (timeout). That means the TUI didn't exit on its own within 5 seconds. But I see the break condition should work. Let me check if the `timeout` killed it, or if the TUI is hanging on `child.wait()`.\n\nLooking at the flow: after the main loop breaks, it does `let _ = child.wait();`. The agent child process might not have exited yet (it's also stuck reading stdin). So `child.wait()` waits for the agent to exit. The agent also has `loop { read_frame from tui_reader }` which would return `None` when stdin closes. Actually wait, the agent's stdin is piped from the TUI's agent_writer. When TUI exits, the agent's stdin pipe is closed. But the TUI is waiting in `child.wait()` for the agent to exit. Deadlock!\n\n```\nTUI: main loop breaks (EOF on stdin)\nTUI: calls child.wait() → blocks waiting for agent to exit\nAgent: main loop blocks on read_frame from tui_reader (stdin from TUI)\nAgent: stdin is piped from TUI — but TUI hasn't dropped the pipe yet\n```\n\nThe fix: When TUI breaks out of the main loop, it should send a shutdown to the agent first, then wait.\n\n```rust\n// Before waiting for child, send shutdown\nsend(&mut agent_writer, r#\"{\"type\":\"shutdown\"}\"#);\nlet _ = child.wait();\n```\n\nBut wait, there's another issue: the `send` requires `agent_writer` to be mutable (it's stdin pipe to agent). Let me check if we can write to it after the loop.\n\nLooking at the TUI code:\n```rust\nlet mut agent_writer = child.stdin.take().unwrap();\n// ... main loop ...\n// After loop:\nlet _ = child.wait();\n```\n\nThe issue is that `agent_writer` (stdin to agent) is still alive, so the pipe isn't closed. The agent is blocked on `read_frame` reading from its stdin, which is the TUI's agent_writer pipe. Until `agent_writer` is dropped, the agent won't see EOF.\n\nThe fix: drop `agent_writer` before `child.wait()`:\n\n```rust\ndrop(agent_writer);  // Close stdin pipe → agent sees EOF → agent exits\nlet _ = child.wait();\n```\n\nOr send shutdown before dropping:\n\n```rust\nif !shutdown_sent {\n    send(&mut agent_writer, r#\"{\"type\":\"shutdown\"}\"#);\n}\ndrop(agent_writer);\nlet _ = child.wait();\n```\n\nActually, I added `/quit` and `/exit` handling that sends shutdown. But when the loop breaks due to EOF, we don't send shutdown. Let me fix this properly.\n\nActually the simpler fix is just to drop the writer and then wait. When the pipe closes, the agent's read_frame will return None (EOF via read_line returning Ok(0)), and the agent breaks out of its loop, cleans up, and exits. Then `child.wait()` succeeds.\n\nActually wait, there's yet another issue: the agent's stdin is `std::io::stdin()`, not the piped writer directly. Let me re-read the agent:\n\n```rust\n// 3. Main loop: read from TUI (stdin), forward to HP/Tools, write to TUI (stdout)\nlet stdin = std::io::stdin();\nlet mut tui_reader = BufReader::new(stdin.lock());\n```\n\nThe agent locks stdin. When the TUI drops `agent_writer` (which is the write end of the pipe connected to agent's stdin), the agent's `stdin.lock().read_line()` will return `Ok(0)` (EOF), and `read_frame` returns `None`, breaking the loop.\n\nSo the fix is simply: drop `agent_writer` before `child.wait()` in the TUI.\n\nLet me make this fix.","signature":"7b327378-eba6-41d7-9290-d3eb4e4fe4f0"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":391,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":67840,"output_tokens":1610,"server_tool_use":{"web_se