{"parentUuid":"1b890b6c-3196-4ac6-b941-3e00571fd551","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"7c293c4b-933d-480e-8f86-4ead74a3993e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Everything is working:\n\n1. ✅ Full pipeline: TUI → Agent → Tools → HP\n2. ✅ Agent connects to HP and registers itself\n3. ✅ Agent spawns tools and gets ToolsReady\n4. ✅ TUI sends user input, agent echoes back with done marker\n5. ✅ HP shows both dsx-hp (self) and dsx-agent (registered) as alive processes\n\nStill exit 124 from timeout, but that's because the TUI main loop breaks, then drops agent_writer, then waits for child (agent) to exit. The agent's stdin pipe closes (TUI drops agent_writer), agent sees EOF, agent breaks its main loop, agent does cleanup (wait for tools, unregister from HP), and exits. Then `child.wait()` in TUI returns.\n\nBut timeout 5 kills it before all this happens. Let me try without the timeout or with a longer one.\n\nActually wait, exit 124 means timeout killed it. The pipeline output shows natural exit (last line of TUI output is `> `, not mid-response). So the TUI exited naturally via EOF, but then `child.wait()` hangs because the agent takes time to clean up.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. With `echo \"hello\" | cmd`, the echo closes immediately after writing \"hello\". So:\n1. TUI reads \"hello\" from pipe\n2. Processes it (sends to agent, reads response)\n3. Goes back to loop, prints `> `\n4. Reads from pipe → Ok(0) EOF → breaks loop\n5. `drop(agent_writer)` — closes the pipe to agent's stdin\n6. `child.wait()` — waits for agent to exit\n\nThe agent:\n1. Reads frames from tui_reader (stdin)\n2. Last read was \"hello\" — echo'd back\n3. Now stdin is closed (drop happened)\n4. `read_frame(&mut tui_reader)` → `read_line` returns Ok(0) → None → breaks loop\n5. Cleanup: tools_child.wait(), unregister from HP\n6. Exits\n\nThe issue might be cleanup timing. Let me check if `c