{"parentUuid":"5a1095f5-e36a-460e-9b84-c6190e68cd81","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"9d6e8ff8-f5c0-4786-84be-fc2b674e5897","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"HP has tokio with full features. Now let me plan the implementation:\n\n1. Add an `api_chat` handler in HP's dispatch_frame\n2. Create a synchronous wrapper that runs the async chat_stream, collects the response\n3. Add the IPC frame protocol between Agent↔HP for API requests\n\nBut wait, this approach has a problem: the API call might take 10-30 seconds, and the HP's dispatch_frame runs inside a per-connection thread that holds the `service` mutex lock. Blocking HP APIs for 30 seconds would prevent other processes from registering heartbeats etc.\n\nActually, looking at the code again:\n\n```rust\nfn handle_connection(stream: TcpStream, service: Arc<Mutex<HealthService>>) {\n    let mut reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap());\n    loop {\n        buf.clear();\n        match reader.read_line(&mut buf) {\n            Ok(_) => {\n                let response = dispatch_frame(trimmed, &service);\n                // write response\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd dispatch_frame:\n```rust\nfn dispatch_frame(line: &str, service: &Mutex<HealthService>) -> String {\n    let mut svc = service.lock().unwrap();\n    // health operations...\n}\n```\n\nThe `service.lock().unwrap()` holds the lock for the entire dispatch. If we call the API inside dispatch_frame, the lock is held for 10+ seconds, blocking all other HP operations (heartbeats, etc.).\n\nThe fix: release the lock before the API call, or use a separate connection for API calls.\n\nA better approach: create a new request type `api_chat` that the HP processes separately, outside the registry lock.\n\nActually, the simplest approach that doesn't require restructuring the HP main loop:\n\nCreate a SEPARATE HTTP API endpoint in the same process. Instead of routing