l client::chat_stream\n            // Collect response\n            // Return as JSON\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nActually, this approach has a problem: the `service` mutex is locked at the top of the old dispatch_frame. But I already changed it to not lock early. Let me re-read the current code.\n\nLooking at my current dispatch_frame:\n```rust\nfn dispatch_frame(line: &str, service: &Mutex<HealthService>) -> String {\n    let frame_type = serde_json::from_str::<serde_json::Value>(line)\n        .ok()\n        .and_then(|v| v.get(\"type\").and_then(|v| v.as_str().map(String::from)))\n        .unwrap_or_default();\n\n    let mut svc = service.lock().unwrap();  // ← Locked here\n\n    match frame_type.as_str() {\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe lock is at the top of dispatch_frame. For `api_chat`, I don't need the lock. Let me restructure: move the lock inside each handler, not at the top.\n\nActually, let me just move the lock into each existing handler and leave `api_chat` without the lock.\n\nLet me do this step by step. But first, let me think about whether this is the right approach or if there's something simpler.\n\nActually, the USER just said \"继续\" (continue). They want me to continue with my plan. Let me just do it — add the api_chat handler to HP, wire up the agent to send API requests, and make the whole thing work.\n\nBut wait — for the user to \"正常使用\" today, they need more than just a one-shot API call. They need:\n1. Conversation management (remembering history)\n2. System prompt management\n3. Tool calling\n4. Error handling\n\nThis is a LOT of work. And the user said \"不动底层逻辑\" (don't touch underlying logic).\n\nLet me re-think the MINIMUM viable thing for today:\n\n**Absolute minimum: The echo pipeline works, binaries compile, cleanup passes**\n\nActually, maybe \"正常使用\" for the user just means \"I can compile and run the binaries\" — not \"the AI responds to my messages\". They might want to verify the architecture works and then continue building on top of it.\n\nLet me just ask rather than assuming. But first, let me present what's done and what's remaining, and ask what they want.\n\nWait, actually — the user's exact words were \"收一下尾确保今天可以正常使用\". This means \"tie up loose ends so it can be used normally today\". The key question is what \"正常使用\" means.\n\nGiven the context of building DSX (an AI coding assistant similar to Claude Code), \"正常使用\" most likely means: type a prompt and get an AI response.\n\nTo achieve this, I need:\n1. Conversation context management (ContextAssembler in dsx-agent library exists but not wired)\n2. API call path to LLM provider (HP has client.rs but no IPC protocol for API calls)\n3. TUI can display results\n\nThe shortest path: Add `api_chat` IPC frame to HP, wire agent to use it.\n\nLet me estimate the work:\n- HP: Add `api_chat` handler ~30 lines\n- Agent: Wire api_chat handler in main loop ~20 lines\n- Agent: Conversation context (minimal: just append user msg, get response) ~20 lines\n- TUI: Already works, no changes needed\n\nTotal: ~70 lines of code. This is minimal enough to qualify as \"收尾\".\n\nLet me do it. The plan:\n1. Move the `service.lock()` into each HP handler, not at the top of dispatch_frame\n2. Add `api_chat` case in dispatch_frame that parses the request, calls client::chat_stream via tokio runtime, collects response\n3. In agent main.rs, when receiving `user_input`, build a simple conversation and send `api_chat` to HP\n4. Forward response back to TUI\n\nLet me start implementing.","signature":"9d6e8ff8-f5c0-4786-84be-fc2b674e5897"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":714,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":89472,"output_tokens":1911,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assista