ongside the api_chat implementation. The fix: drain stale data before sending a new request.\n\nThe simplest approach: After sending the register frame, read and discard the response. Or, make the HP NOT send a response to the register frame (or any frame that the agent doesn't need to read).\n\nActually, the simplest fix is to just read the register response:\n\n```rust\nif let Some(ref hp) = hp {\n    let frame = format!(...register...);\n    let mut hp_write = hp.try_clone().unwrap();\n    write_frame(&mut hp_write, &frame);\n    // Drain the register response\n    let mut hp_r = BufReader::new(hp.try_clone().unwrap());\n    let _ = read_frame(&mut hp_r);\n}\n```\n\nThen later, when we want to use the HP connection for api_chat, we create a new reader which won't have stale data.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The `hp` variable is `let hp = ...` (not mutable). When we create `hp_r` from `hp.try_clone()`, we're cloning the TcpStream (which creates a new handle to the same TCP connection). Reading from one handle reads from the same socket buffer. So if we read and discard one line, the next reader on a different handle will get the next line.\n\nActually, `try_clone()` creates a new file descriptor referencing the same socket. The socket's receive buffer is shared. Any read from any handle reads from the shared buffer. So if I read the register response from one handle, the next read from another handle won't see it.\n\nActually wait, `try_clone` on `TcpStream` uses `dup()` or similar, creating a new fd pointing to the same socket. So they share the same receive buffer. If I read a line from hp_r (cloned handle), the data is consumed from the shared buffer. Then creating a new BufReader later would start from the next data.\n\nSo the fix is:\n1. After sending register, read and discard the response from a cloned reader\n2. Then when sending api_chat, create a new reader that starts from the api_chat response\n\nLet me implement this. Also, the same issue exists