{"parentUuid":"6eabf355-0452-4212-8c53-2825e8a513ad","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"8064e249-ae39-4fcf-a685-32e87cee4ffd","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants to:\n1. Static link the binaries (so they don't need dynamic libraries at runtime)\n2. Combine into a single binary (instead of 4 separate binaries: dsx-hp, dsx-agent, dsx-tools, dsx-tui)\n\nTwo separate questions:\n\n**Static linking**: Rust uses `musl` target for fully static binaries on Linux. This means building with `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` target triple instead of the default `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`.\n\n**Single binary**: This is more architectural. The current architecture has 4 processes that communicate via IPC. To make a single binary, I'd need to either:\n1. Use `std::process::Command` to spawn the sub-processes from within one binary (like the TUI currently spawns agent)\n2. Use threads instead of processes for the internal components\n3. Use an \"orchestrator\" binary that spawns all components\n\nBut the whole point of the 5-process architecture IS process isolation (the memory says \"DSX surpasses Claude Code through architecture - process isolation, daemon, sandbox\"). So making it a single binary goes against the architecture.\n\nWait, but the user might mean something simpler: a single binary that spawns the sub-processes from embedded binaries. Like `cargo bundle` or using `include_bytes!` to embed the other binaries and extract them at runtime.\n\nOr they might mean: can we use `rustix` or `musl` to make the binaries statically linked so they can be copied to another machine without dependencies.\n\nLet me think about both requests:\n\n### Static linking\nUse musl target: `rustup target add x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` then `cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`\n\nThis produces fully static binaries that don't depend on glibc. They'll run on any Linux regardless of libc version