{"parentUuid":"4ded0dc4-1de5-42a4-b1ca-0ce63ae2dd6c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"296bc61e-6d1b-4919-9463-e28b971e1cd0","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`reqwest` already uses `rustls`, not `native-tls`. This means there's no dependency on OpenSSL/libssl. The only native dependency would be musl's libc. This should make musl compilation much easier.\n\nLet me check what other native dependencies might exist:\n- `crossterm` → uses termios (should work with musl)\n- `ratatui` → pure Rust\n- `tokio` → pure Rust\n- `pty-process` → might use fork/exec (should work with musl)\n\nLet me also check if `rustls` has any native dependencies... Rustls is pure Rust, so it should be fine.\n\nLet me try installing the musl target and building.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about the single-binary approach first, since that addresses the user's real concern (deployment simplicity). \n\nFor the single binary, I have a few options:\n\n**Option A: One binary, multiple roles (like busybox)**\n- A single `dsx` binary that accepts subcommands: `dsx hp`, `dsx agent`, `dsx tools`, `dsx tui`\n- When `dsx tui` runs, it spawns `dsx hp` (if not running), `dsx agent`, and `dsx tools` as subprocesses\n- The tricky part: spawning itself recursively with different subcommand args\n\n**Option B: TUI spawns everything directly (no agent binary, no tools binary)**\n- The TUI directly uses the agent library and tools library (in-process)\n- This eliminates the agent and tools processes\n- HP still runs as a separate process (daemon) for API key isolation\n- But this defeats the process isolation architecture\n\n**Option C: Workspace builds all binaries, launcher script**\n- Keep separate binaries but provide a `dsx` wrapper binary that manages them\n- `dsx start` → starts HP, then TUI\n- `dsx hp` → run HP daemon\n- `dsx tui` → run TUI (which spawns agent+tools)\n\nI think Option A is the best approach. Let me implement it:\n\n1. Create a new \"umbrella\" crate (dsx-cli or just modify dsx-tui to be the main entry)\n2. Use clap subcommands\n3. Each subcommand delegates to the corresponding binary's main function\n\nActually, the cleanest implementation: **make the TUI binary the umbrella**. It already has clap as a dependency. I can add subcommands to it.\n\nWait, the TUI's Cargo.toml has `clap` with the `derive` feature. So I can use clap subcommands.\n\nBut there's an architectural issue: the functions in dsx-hp/src/main.rs, dsx-agent/src/main.rs, etc. are in different crates. I can't call `dsx_hp::main()` because main() is in the binary target, not the library.\n\nOptions:\n1. Move each binary's main logic into its library as a public function (e.g., `dsx_hp::run()`)\n2. Have the umbrella binary exec() itself with different args\n3. Use `include!` to share the code\n\nOption 1 is the cleanest. I'd:\n1. In dsx-hp/src/lib.rs, add `pub fn run() { ... }` with the main logic\n2. In dsx-hp/src/main.rs, just call `dsx_hp::run()`\n3. Same for dsx-agent, dsx-tools\n4. Create a new umbrella binary that dispatches to the right run() function\n\nBut this is a significant refactor. The user said \"不动底层逻辑\" (don't touch underlying logic).\n\nActually, option 2 is simpler and doesn't refactor anything:\n1. Create a new `dsx-cli` crate with a single binary target\n2. When run as `dsx hp`, it does `exec ./dsx-hp` (finds the real binary next to itself)\n3. When run as `dsx tui`, it does `exec ./dsx-tui`\n4. Build all 5 binaries as before + the wrapper\n\nBut this is just a symlink/launcher, not a TRUE single binary.\n\nOption 3: use `include_bytes!` to embed binaries and extract at runtime:\n1. Build dsx-hp, dsx-agent, dsx-tools, dsx-tui as usual\n2. In a new `dsx` binary, `include_bytes!(\"../../target/release/dsx-hp\")` and write to temp dir, then exec\n3. This is hacky and fragile\n\nLet me think about what the user REALLY wants. They asked \"可以编译成静态链接和单一二进制吗\". This means:\n- Static link: yes\n- Single binary: combine everything into one file\n\nThe simplest approach that preserves architecture:\n\n**Create an umbrella binary that uses symlinks or embedded exec:**\n\nNew crate `dsx` at workspace root:\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();\n    if args.len() < 2 {\n        // Default: start TUI\n        start_tui();\n    } else {\n        match args[1].as_str() {\n            \"hp\" => dsx_hp::run(),\n            \"agent\" => dsx_agent::run(),\n            \"tools\" => dsx_tools::run(),\n            \"tui\" => start_tui(),\n            _ => eprintln!(\"Usage: dsx [hp|agent|tools|tui]\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nFor this to work, I need to:\n1. Extract the main logic from each binary into a publicly accessible function in each library\n2. The umbrella binary depends on all 4 crates\n\nThis is the cleanest approach but requires moving the `main()` logic into library functions. For dsx-hp, dsx-agent, dsx-tools — their main functions are relatively simple and self-contained. For dsx-tui, the main.rs is already using the library.\n\nLet me check how much work this would be:\n\n**dsx-hp**: The main function has ~170 lines (TCP listener, dispatch, etc.). Moving into `dsx_hp::run()` would make it accessible.\n\n**dsx-agent**: The main function has ~130 lines (HP connect, spawn tools, main loop). Moving into `dsx_agent::run()`.\n\n**dsx-tools**: Already calls `dsx_tools::ipc::ipc_main_loop()`. Just need a `dsx_tools::run()` wrapper.\n\n**dsx-tui**: Already uses `clap::Parser`. The main function has ~90 lines. Moving into `dsx_tui::run()`.\n\nThen create a new crate that depends on all 4 and dispatches.\n\nActually, I realize there might be a simpler approach that the user might prefer:\n\n**Just use `exec` to re-exec the same binary with the right subprocess name.**\n\nCreate a single `dsx` binary that:\n1. If invoked as `dsx` (no args) or `dsx tui`: run TUI mode\n2. If invoked as `dsx hp` or symlinked as `dsx-hp`: run