::from(\"/tmp\"));\n    base.join(\"dsx\").join(\"hp.port\")\n}\n```\n\nThis should work. If `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is not set, it defaults to `/tmp/dsx/hp.port`. The `mkdir -p /tmp/dsx` was done, so the directory exists.\n\nBut `mkdir -p /tmp/dsx` was run BEFORE starting HP. The `create_dir_all` in `write_port_file` should also handle this.\n\nLet me check: when I ran `$STATIC_BIN hp > /tmp/dsx/hp.log 2>&1 &`, the shell might have started the process but the working directory might be different, or the process might not have had time to write the port file before the next command ran.\n\nLet me add a sleep and try again. Or check more carefully.\n\nActually, looking at the HP log output, it says `listening on 127.0.0.1:35115` and then the `cat /tmp/dsx/hp.port` found nothing. This must be a timing issue — the HP writes the port AFTER binding, but maybe the `sleep 2` wasn't long enough? No, the HP has `sleep 2` in the script.\n\nWait, actually let me re-read the test script:\n\n```bash\n$STATIC_BIN hp > /tmp/dsx/hp.log 2>&1 &\nsleep 2\necho \"=== HP: $(cat /tmp/dsx/hp.port) ===\"\n```\n\nOh I see: the `cat /tmp/dsx/hp.port` returned \"No such file or directory\" which is shown as an EMPTY string after \"HP: \". The `$()` in the echo swallowed the error. This must mean the port file was not written.\n\nBut the HP log says it's listening. Let me check if the write_port_file actually works. Maybe the `create_dir_all` silently fails.\n\nActually wait, maybe the issue is simpler. When I ran:\n```bash\nrm -f /tmp/dsx/hp.port; mkdir -p /tmp/dsx\n```\n\nThe `mkdir -p /tmp/dsx` creates `/tmp/dsx`. Then `$STATIC_BIN hp > ...` starts the HP, which tries to write to `/tmp/dsx/hp.port`. The `create_dir_all` in write_port_file might be redundant if the dir exists.\n\nLet me check: does the static binary have filesystem permissions issues? It shouldn't — it's running as the same user.\n\nLet me try again, more carefully.","signature":"fc87fae3-8e64-4ee5-9408-3141b1a3a230"}],"stop_reaso