{"parentUuid":"f6aab496-0abc-4988-88c3-dcd3fd89c5f7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"d15bef79-c6f5-412a-82d6-87b53d506dfd","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so the memory issue is fixed (thanks to removing the blocking read, the event loop now drains agent frames properly). But CPU is still 100%.\n\nThe CPU 100% issue means either:\n1. `crossterm::event::poll()` always returns immediately (spurious wakeup), so the loop never sleeps\n2. The throttle `sleep(16ms - elapsed)` is working but something else burns CPU\n\nWait, if `poll()` always returns immediately, `elapsed` would be very small (< 1ms), and the throttle would sleep for ~15ms. So the loop would run at ~60fps anyway. Unless the throttle code isn't executing properly.\n\nLet me re-examine the throttle code:\n\n```rust\nlet elapsed = frame_start.elapsed();\nlet min_frame = std::time::Duration::from_millis(16);\nif elapsed < min_frame {\n    std::thread::sleep(min_frame - elapsed);\n}\n```\n\nThis should work. 60fps with ratatui rendering shouldn't cause 100% CPU.\n\nWait - could the issue be the `render_all` function itself? If it's doing a LOT of work each frame (e.g., wrapping all messages with complex text rendering), it could take 100+ms per frame and consume 100% CPU on one core. But that would make the loop run at <10fps, not cause 100% CPU.\n\nUnless... ratatui's rendering is very expensive. Let me check what `render_all` does.\n\nActually, let me check if the issue is that `terminal.draw()` takes a LOT of CPU. In some ratatui applications, each frame renders all content from scratch, including complex operations like text wrapping, syntax highlighting, etc. If the message history grows, each frame becomes more expensive.\n\nBut the user said CPU is 100% even with no messages. So it's not the message rendering.\n\nMaybe the issue is with the render_all_vertical function? Let me check.\n\nActually, let me think about what happens when the user runs the TUI in a narrow terminal (which is common for terminal apps). The `render_all` function checks `if area.width < 100` and uses `render_all_vertical`. If the vertical layout is buggy or expensive, it could cause issues.\n\nWait, let me check what happens on the first draw. The terminal is cleared, then rendered for the first time. The initial state has:\n- No messages\n- Empty input\n- Mode::Chat\n\nThe render function should quickly draw an empty conversation area + input box + status line. This should be fast (<1ms).\n\nUnless the `render_all` function is hanging or looping. Let me check if there's an infinite loop in any of the render functions.\n\nActually, I think the most practical approach is to add a simple diagnostic: print how long each frame takes. But I can't do that without modifying the code and rebuilding.\n\nLet me try another approach: just make the loop simpler and more robust. Instead of depending on `poll()` which might have platform-specific behavior, use a fixed-frame-rate approach:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    // Check for keyboard events (non-blocking)\n    while crossterm::event::poll(Duration::from_millis(0))? {\n        if let Event::Key(key) = crossterm::event::read()? {\n            // handle key\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Drain agent frames\n    for frame in conn.drain_frames() { ... }\n    \n    // Render\n    terminal.draw(...)?;\n    \n    // Fixed-rate sleep\n    std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(33)); // ~30fps\n}\n```\n\nUsing `poll(0)` (non-blocking) is simpler and more predictable than `poll(10ms)`. And the explicit sleep guarantees the loop doesn't spin.\n\nBut wait, `poll(0)` might also have issues in some terminals. Let me use `poll(Duration::ZERO)` or just `poll_ms(0)`.\n\nActually, the simplest approach that definitely works: skip `poll()` entirely and use `read()` with a timeout approach:\n\nNo, `read()` also blocks. Let me just use the pattern I described above with `poll(0)` + explicit sleep.\n\nActually, maybe the real issue is simpler. Let me check if `std::thread::sleep` is actually being called. The condition is:\n\n```rust\nif elapsed < min_frame {\n    std::thread::sleep(min_frame - elapsed);\n}\n```\n\nIf `elapsed` is ALWAYS greater than 16ms (e.g., because rendering takes >16ms), then the sleep is never called, and the loop runs at the pace of rendering. But if rendering takes ~5ms, the sleep adds 11ms, giving ~60fps.\n\n60fps with complex rendering could still cause 100% CPU on a single core. That's normal for any GUI application running at 60fps.\n\nHmm, but the user explicitly says the CPU is \"满\" (full). Let me fix this by reducing the frame rate significantly. In a TUI, even 10fps is fine for most use cases (text doesn't need 60fps to look smooth).\n\nLet me reduce to 33ms (~30fps):\n\nActually wait, let me think about this more carefully. If the render is very expensive (e.g., 50ms per frame), then the loop runs at ~20fps, and each frame uses 50ms of CPU. That's 100% CPU usage. The solution would be to make the render less expensive, or cache things.\n\nBut I should first check what the actual render time is. Let me add timing info.\n\nActually, for a quick fix, let me just switch to a simpler rendering approach. Instead of using ratatui's full rendering, let me use a simpler renderer. Or better yet, let me add a dirty flag - only render when something changed.\n\nLet me modify the event loop to only render when state changes:","signature":"d15bef79-c6f5-412a-82d6-87b53d506dfd"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":98,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":254720,"output_tokens":2939,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"9aa9111e-0de9-47a9-aa51-0729