{"parentUuid":"7c9e1601-19be-4911-90ba-7db291f9b886","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"ae8853d3-3786-4dff-a5b6-a3c7efa33046","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is saying CPU and memory both spike immediately. This suggests it's not just the TUI rendering - something else is going on. Let me think about what happens when `./dsx` runs:\n\n1. The umbrella binary dispatches to `dsx_tui::run()`\n2. `ensure_hp_running()` is called - spawns HP daemon if not running\n3. `AgentConnection::spawn()` is called - spawns agent subprocess\n4. Terminal setup (raw mode, alternate screen)\n5. Event loop starts\n\nThe HP daemon is a separate process that listens on TCP. It should be nearly idle.\n\nThe agent subprocess is also a child process. It connects to HP, spawns tools, and waits for input. When idle, it blocks on `read_line()` waiting for TUI input. It should use zero CPU.\n\nThe TUI event loop should be idle most of the time, only waking for keyboard events or agent frames.\n\nBut if CPU and memory spike immediately, something is very wrong. Let me think about what could cause this:\n\n1. **AgentConnection background thread**: The `spawn()` method creates a background thread that reads from the agent's stdout and sends frames to an mpsc channel. If the agent is sending a LOT of data (or infinite data), this thread could be busy and the channel could fill up.\n\n2. **The agent itself**: What if the agent process is stuck in a tight loop? The agent's `runner::run()` function:\n   - Connects to HP\n   - Spawns tools\n   - Enters main loop: reads from stdin (TUI), processes frames\n   \n   The agent should block on `read_frame()` waiting for TUI input. But if something causes it to loop without blocking...\n\n3. **Tools subprocess**: Similar - should block waiting for stdin.\n\nWait, let me look at the agent's main loop more carefully:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    match read_frame(&mut tui_reader) {\n        Some(frame) => {\n            // process frame\n        }\n        None => break,\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`read_frame` uses `BufReader::read_line()` which blocks until a line is available. So the agent should block here. But what if the TUI closes its stdin pipe? Then `read_line` returns `Ok(0)`, `read_frame` returns `None`, and the agent breaks out of the loop and exits.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. When the user runs `./dsx`:\n1. The umbrella binary starts\n2. It calls `dsx_tui::run()`\n3. `ensure_hp_running()` spawns `dsx hp &` as a background process\n4. `AgentConnection::spawn()` spawns `dsx agent` as a child process\n5. The agent spawns `dsx tools` as a child process\n\nSo we have 4 processes: dsx (TUI), dsx hp, dsx agent, dsx tools.\n\nEach process should block on I/O:\n- HP: blocks on `accept()` or `read_line()` on TCP connections\n- Agent: blocks on `read_line()` from stdin (TUI pipe)\n- Tools: blocks on `read_line()` from stdin (agent pipe)\n- TUI: blocks on `crossterm::event::poll()` or sleeps\n\nNone of these should cause CPU spikes. Unless one of them has a tight loop.\n\nWait... let me look at the agent's `connect_hp()` function:\n\n```rust\nfn connect_hp() -> (Option<BufReader<Box<dyn std::io::Read + Send>>>, Option<Box<dyn std::io::Write + Send>>) {\n    let port = hp_port();\n    if port == 0 {\n        eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: HP not running (no hp.port) — continuing without AI\");\n        return (None, None);\n    }\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nAnd in `run()`:\n```rust\nlet (mut hp_reader, mut hp_writer) = connect_hp();\n// Drain the register response\nif let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n    let _ = read_frame(r);\n}\n```\n\nThis drains the HP register response. Then later, when a `user_input` frame arrives from TUI:\n\n```rust\nhandle_user_input(&frame, &mut hp_reader, &mut hp_writer, &mut conversation, &mut tui_writer);\n```\n\nInside `handle_user_input`:\n```rust\nfn handle_user_input(\n    frame: &str,\n    hp_reader: &mut Option<BufReader<Box<dyn std::io::Read + Send>>>,\n    hp_writer: &mut Option<Box<dyn std::io::Write + Send>>,\n    conversation: &mut Vec<serde_json::Value>,\n    tui_writer: &mut impl Write,\n) {\n    // ...\n    let reader = match hp_reader {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => { write_frame(tui_writer, &format!(...)); return; }\n    };\n    let writer = match hp_writer {\n        Some(w) => w,\n        None => { write_frame(tui_writer, &format!(...)); return; }\n    };\n    \n    let chat_req = serde_json::json!({...});\n    write_frame(writer, &chat_req.to_string());\n    \n    if let Some(resp) = read_frame(reader) {\n        // process response\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSo when the agent gets a `user_input` frame, it sends an `api_chat` request to HP and blocks waiting for the response. HP processes the request (makes an HTTP call), and returns the response. This is a synchronous request-response cycle.\n\nBut what if HP takes a long time? The agent blocks on `read_frame(reader)` waiting for HP's response. During this time, the agent can't process any other frames. The TUI is waiting for the agent's response. This is fine - it's a synchronous pipeline.\n\nHmm, but issues could arise if the agent sends an api_chat request and the response never comes (HP crashed, connection issue). The agent would block forever. But that wouldn't cause CPU/memory spikes - just a hang.\n\nLet me think about the tools subprocess more carefully:\n\nThe agent spawns tools with:\n```rust\nwrite_frame(&mut tools_writer, r#\"{\"type\":\"tools_init\",...}\"#);\nlet ready = read_frame(&mut tools_reader).unwrap_or_default();\n```\n\nThis sends an Init frame and waits for Ready. After that, the agent enters the main loop and only forwards `tool_call` frames to tools. But in our current flow, the user just types messages — no tool calls are made. So tools sits idle waiting for input.\n\nWait, I just realized something important. The agent's `handle_user_input` function sends the entire `conversation` to HP with each request. The conversation grows with each turn. If the conversation gets very long, the JSON serialization and the HP response could be large. But this shouldn't cause immediate CPU/memory spikes.\n\nLet me look at this from a different angle. The user says CPU AND memory spike immediately. This means something is wrong from the very start.\n\nLet me check: does `AgentConnection::spawn()` work correctly? It starts a background reader thread:\n\n```rust\nlet handle = thread::Builder::new()\n    .name(\"agent-reader\".into())\n    .spawn(move || {\n        let reader = BufReader::new(stdout);\n        for line in reader.lines() {\n            match line {\n                Ok(line) => {\n                    if line.trim().is_empty() { continue; }\n                    match serde_json::from_str::<AgentFrame>(&line) {\n                        Ok(frame) => {\n                            if frame_tx.send(frame).is_err() { break; }\n                        }\n                        Err(e) => {\n                            log::warn!(\"agent: invalid frame: {}  line={}\", e, line);\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                Err(e) => {\n                    log::error!(\"agent: stdout read error: {}\", e);\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    });\n```\n\nThis thread reads from agent's stdout. When the agent starts, it doesn't print anything to stdout until it processes a `user_input`. So the thread should block on `reader.lines()`.\n\nBut wait - does the agent print anything to stdout that's not a valid AgentFrame? Let me check:\n\nAgent's main loop:\n```rust\nhandle_user_input(&frame, &mut hp_reader, &mut hp_writer, &mut conversation, &mut tui_writer);\nwrite_frame(&mut tui_writer, r#\"{\"type\":\"done\"}\"#);\n```\n\n`handle_user_input` calls `write_frame(tui_writer, ...)`. `tui_writer` is `std::io::stdout()`. So when the agent sends a response, it writes to stdout (which is read by the TUI's AgentConnection reader thread).\n\nThe `write_frame` writes: `{\"type\":\"api_response\",\"content\":\"Hello\"}` and `{\"type\":\"done\"}`.\n\nThese ARE valid AgentFrame format. So the reader thread should deserialize them correctly.\n\nBut what if the agent writes something that's NOT a valid AgentFrame? The reader thread would hit the `Err` branch and log a warning. The `log::warn!()` call... does it go to stderr? Yes, `log::warn!` goes to stderr by default. So it shouldn't interfere with the AgentFrame deserialization.\n\nHmm, let me think about this even more. Could the issue be that the agent is crashing repeatedly? Like, it starts, crashes, gets restarted, crashes again? No, there's no restart mechanism.\n\nWait, let me look at the agent's `runner.rs` more carefully. When the TUI sends the FIRST message, the agent calls `handle_user_input`. This function:\n\n1. Parses the `user_input` frame\n2. Adds user message to `conversation`\n3. Sends `api_chat` to HP\n4. Reads HP's response\n5. Adds assistant response to `conversation`\n6. Writes `api_response` frame to TUI\n\nAfter this, control returns to the main loop. The main loop then writes `{\"type\":\"done\"}` to TUI.\n\nAll of this should be fine. No tight loops, no memory leaks.\n\nWait, but I just realized something. The `conversation` Vec grows without bound. Each turn adds 2 entries (user + assistant). Over many turns, this could grow large. But it wouldn't cause IMMEDIATE CPU/memory spikes.\n\nLet me look at the HP runner. The HP's dispatch_frame for `api_chat` calls `handle_api_chat(line)`:\n\n```rust\nfn handle_api_chat(line: &str) -> String {\n    let config = match HP_CONFIG.get() {\n        Some(c) => c.clone(),\n        None => return json_response(\"error\", \"HP not configured (no API key)\"),\n    };\n    \n    let rt = match tokio::runtime::Runtime::new() {\n        Ok(r) => r,\n        Err(e) => return json_response(\"error\", &format!(\"runtime error: {e}\")),\n    };\n    \n    let (content, error): (String, Option<String>) = rt.block_on(async {\n        // ... tokio::spawn, rx.recv() ...\n    });\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nEach api_chat call creates a NEW tokio runtime. This creates a new thread pool (tokio creates worker threads). Over multiple api_chat calls, runtimes are created and dropped. Creating a runtime allocates threads and memory. But they should be dropped after the call completes.\n\nWait... `tokio::runtime::Runtime::new()` creates a multi-thread runtime by default (one worker thread per CPU core). On an 8-core machine, that's 8 threads per call. These threads are destroyed when the runtime is dropped. But if the runtime isn't dropped (e.g., due to Rust's async cleanup being tricky), the threads would leak.\n\nBut this is only triggered when api_chat is called (i.e., when the user sends a message). Not on startup.\n\nOK, I'm going in circles. Let me take a completely different approach. Let me add more diagnostic output to understand what's happening. Or better yet, let me check if the issue is specifically with the HP daemon or the agent.\n\nActually, I just thought of something. When the TUI spawns the agent with `AgentConnection::spawn()`, the TUI and agent communicate via stdin/stdout pipe. The TUI writes frames to the agent's stdin, and reads frames from the agent's stdout.\n\nBut what if the TUI is sending frames that the agent doesn't understand? The TUI sends `TuiFrame::UserInput` which serializes to `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"...\"}`. The agent's main loop checks:\n```rust\nif frame.contains(\"\\\"type\\\":\\\"user_input\\\"\") {\n    handle_user_input(&frame, ...);\n}\n```\n\nThis should work - the serialized TuiFrame contains `\"type\":\"user_input\"`.\n\nBut wait, does the TUI send any OTHER frames? Let me check what happens with `AgentConnection::send_frame`:\n\n```rust\npub fn send_frame(&mut self, frame: TuiFrame) {\n    if let Some(ref mut stdin) = self.stdin {\n        let mut line = serde_json::to_string(&frame)\n            .unwrap_or_else(|_| r#\"{\"t\":\"error\",\"message\":\"serialize failed\"}\"#.into());\n        line.push('\\n');\n        if let Err(e) = stdin.write_all(line.as_bytes()) {\n            log::warn!(\"agent: stdin write error: {}\", e);\n        }\n        if let Err(e) = stdin.flush() {\n            log::warn!(\"agent: stdin flush error: {}\", e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd `AgentConnection::shutdown()` sends `TuiFrame::Shutdown` which serializes to `{\"type\":\"shutdown\"}`. The agent handles this correctly.\n\nOK, I'm going to take a practical approach. Let me create a minimal TUI test to isolate the issue. But first, let me check: could the problem be in the `render_all` function? Let me look at what it does on the first render with an empty state.\n\nActually, I just thought of something. Let me check if `render_all` is being called from the correct scope. In my event loop:\n\n```rust\nterminal.draw(|f| {\n    crate::render::render_all(f, state, conn, stream);\n})?;\n```\n\n`render_all` is `pub(super)` in the `render` module. `pub(super)` means accessible from the parent module of `render`, which is the crate root (`lib.rs`). So `crate::render::render_all` should work.\n\nBut wait, let me trace through the render_all code to see if it could cause issues:\n\n```rust\npub(super) fn render_all(\n    frame: &mut ratatui::Frame,\n    app_state: &AppState,\n    agent_conn: &AgentConnection,\n    stream_buf: &StreamBuffer,\n) {\n    let ctx = RenderContext::from_parts(app_state, stream_buf, agent_conn);\n    let area = frame.area();\n\n    if area.width < 100 {\n        return render_all_vertical(frame, &ctx);\n    }\n    \n    // horizontal layout...\n}\n```\n\n`RenderContext::from_parts` borrows from `app_state`, `stream_buf`, `agent_conn`. No allocations. Should be fine.\n\nThen for a narrow terminal, it calls `render_all_vertical(frame, &ctx)`. Let me check that function.\n\nLet me look for `render_all_vertical` in the render module.\n\nActually, let me look at this from yet another angle. Maybe the issue is that `crossterm::event::poll(Duration::ZERO)` always returns `true` in this terminal, and the inner `while` loop spins processing events. But `poll(Duration::ZERO)` should return immediately with `true` or `false`. If something keeps generating events (like the terminal sending cursor position reports), the inner loop would process them. The `_ => dirty = true` catch-all marks dirty and continues. After the inner loop, it renders and sleeps 33ms. With 33ms sleep, max ~30fps, which shouldn't cause 100% CPU on a modern machine.\n\nUnless... the `terminal.draw()` call is very expensive and takes >33ms. If draw takes 50ms, the effective framerate drops to 20fps, but each frame uses 50ms of CPU = 100% of one core. This IS possible if the render function does complex operations.\n\nBut the initial render (empty state) should be very fast. Just a banner, empty message area, and input box. No text wrapping, no message formatting.\n\nWait, let me check: does the banner render use ANSI art that requires complex operations?\n\nLooking at the render module, it imports `DS_BANNER` which is a large ASCII art string. Rendering this each frame could be expensive if it's being wrapped and styled. But even so, for a terminal-sized window, rendering a banner should be sub-millisecond.\n\nOK, I'm going to take a different approach entirely. Let me create a MINIMAL ratatui test that just draws a simple screen without all the complex render modules. This will isolate whether the issue is in the rendering or elsewhere.\n\nActually, let me just strip down the render to the absolute minimum: a blank screen with just a text input at the bottom. No banner, no chat history formatting, no status bar.\n\nActually, the simplest approach: let me add some timing diagnostics to find where the CPU is being spent. I'll add eprintln! calls to log frame times.\n\nOr even simpler: let me just run the TUI with `perf` to see where CPU is being spent.\n\nWait, I'm overthinking this. Let me try a completely minimal test: run the umbrella binary with `dsx hp &` first, then run a simple program that just reads from stdin and prints to confirm the terminal works. But that's not helpful.\n\nLet me try yet another approach: make the event loop simpler - instead of using crossterm's event system with poll/read, use a simpler stdin reading approach.\n\nActually, I just realized something important. The `crossterm::event::read()` function has been known to cause issues in some configurations. When you enter raw mode and call `read()`, crossterm initializes the event reader. If the event reader initialization fails silently, `poll()` might behave incorrectly.\n\nLet me try a different approach entirely: sidestep crossterm's event system and use raw stdin reading directly with `termion` or manual ANSI parsing.\n\nActually, for a quick fix, let me just use the `crossterm::event::EventStream` API instead of `poll()` + `read()`. Or better yet, let me use `std::io::stdin().read_line()` in a simple loop like the original readline version, but with ratatui rendering.\n\nWait, I have another idea. The issue might be that we're NOT in a real terminal. If the binary is run without a proper terminal (e.g., piped), `enable_raw_mode()` might fail or behave unexpectedly. But the binary is launched directly by the user.\n\nOK let me just take the most pragmatic approach: \n1. Add frame time diagnostics\n2. Find the bottleneck\n3. Fix it\n\nActually, I just thought of the most likely cause. The `crossterm::event::poll()` and `read()` functions use the `std::io::stdin()` handle. When `enable_raw_mode()` is called, crossterm puts the terminal into raw mode, which changes how stdin reads data. But the `std::io::stdin()` handle is lazily initialized and might have buffered data from before raw mode was enabled.\n\nMore importantly, in some terminals, raw mode causes escape sequences to be sent by the terminal (e.g.