{"parentUuid":"01fadc04-a4f2-46b1-8a57-1692407ef1c0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"49fb7acc-0af1-4209-b0e4-b7e43373a510","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The HP port file has a stale port. The HP process from earlier was killed (probably by the timeout test), but the port file wasn't cleaned up. So `ensure_hp_running()` sees the port file exists and returns immediately, but the HP is actually dead.\n\nThen `AgentConnection::spawn()` spawns the agent, which tries to connect to HP on port 33635. The connection fails, so the agent prints \"cannot connect HP: Connection refused\" and continues without HP.\n\nBut... does the agent actually start and run? Let me check. The agent should:\n1. Try to connect to HP → fails → continues without HP\n2. Spawn tools\n3. Wait for ToolsReady\n4. Enter main loop (block on read from stdin)\n\nIf the agent starts correctly, AgentConnection::spawn() should return. Then `run_impl()` should proceed to terminal setup.\n\nBut the user said CPU and memory spike immediately. Maybe the issue is that the HP port file is stale, and the agent repeatedly tries to connect to it?\n\nWait, looking at the agent code:\n```rust\nfn connect_hp() -> (...) {\n    let port = hp_port();\n    if port == 0 {\n        return (None, None);\n    }\n    let stream = match TcpStream::connect(format!(\"127.0.0.1:{port}\")) {\n        Ok(s) => s,\n        Err(e) => {\n            eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: cannot connect HP: {e}\");\n            return (None, None);\n        }\n    };\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe agent tries to connect ONCE and returns None on failure. It doesn't retry. So if the HP is dead, the agent connects HP → fails → continues without HP. This should be fast.\n\nBut then the agent spawns tools:\n```rust\nlet mut tools_cmd = Command::new(\n    if std::env::var(\"DSX_SINGLE_BINARY\").is_ok() {\n        exe.to_path_buf()\n    } else {\n        exe.with_file_name(\"dsx-tools\