{"parentUuid":"be4ec899-8ab1-4c3b-a45b-c5fb38da56d9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"1258a55f-1e12-4138-bdc2-32626efbd5a2","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Wait, but `cargo build --release -p dsx` would create the binary at `./target/release/dsx` (the default output path). The `--target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` flag puts it at `./target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/dsx`.\n\nIf the user runs `./target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/dsx`, they get the static build. If they run just `./dsx` from the project root, that file doesn't exist.\n\nBut the user might have been running the wrong binary. Let me make sure the right one is used and accessible.\n\nActually, let me focus on what I can reproduce. My test shows 0% CPU and 5MB RAM. The user gets 100% CPU and infinite memory. The difference must be environmental.\n\nLet me look at this from the user's perspective. They run:\n```bash\n./target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/dsx\n```\n\nThis starts the TUI. The TUI:\n1. Calls `ensure_hp_running()` - finds HP already running (from previous test), returns immediately\n2. Spawns agent process\n3. Sets up terminal\n4. Enters event loop\n\nIn my test (without a real TTY), the TUI starts and waits. In the user's terminal, the TUI also starts. But they get 100% CPU.\n\nCould the issue be that the agent spawns `dsx tools`, which spawns more processes? Let me check: does the agent's tools spawning work correctly?\n\nThe agent's runner.rs:\n```rust\nif std::env::var(\"DSX_SINGLE_BINARY\").is_ok() {\n    exe.to_path_buf()\n} else {\n    exe.with_file_name(\"dsx-tools\")\n}\n```\n\nWhen running inside the umbrella, `DSX_SINGLE_BINARY` is \"1\". So it spawns `exe.to_path_buf()` which is the SAME binary. Then `.arg(\"tools\")` is added.\n\nSo the chain is:\n```\ndsx (TUI) → spawn dsx agent → spawn dsx tools\n```\n\nWhen `dsx agent` runs, it has `DSX_SINGLE_BINARY=1`. So it spawns `Command::new(&exe).arg(\"tools\")`. The `exe` is `std::env::current_exe()` which returns the path to `dsx`.\n\nSo `dsx agent` spawns `Command::new(\"/path/to/dsx\").arg(\"tools\")`. This runs `dsx tools`, which calls `dsx_tools::run()`, which enters the IPC loop.\n\nBut wait — when `dsx tools` starts, does IT also spawn something? Let me check: `dsx_tools::run()` calls `ipc::ipc_main_loop(&mut build_tool_manager())`. `build_tool_manager()` registers tools but doesn't spawn processes. The IPC loop reads stdin and processes frames. So no recursive spawning. Good.\n\nWhat about when `dsx hp` runs (started by `ensure_hp_running()`)? It listens on TCP and forks threads per connection. Threads sleep on `accept()` and `read_line()`. No tight loops.\n\nOK, I genuinely can't reproduce the issue. The binary runs fine in my test environment. Let me take a completely different approach: instead of the ratatui TUI, let me provide a fallback mode that uses the simple readline loop. If the ratatui has environmental issues, the user can fall back.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check one more thing. The user said \"不显示tui且内存占用还是会无限增长\". \"The TUI doesn't display AND memory still grows unboundedly.\"\n\nFor the memory to grow, something must be allocating and not freeing. In the event loop:\n- `conn.drain_frames()` returns a `Vec<AgentFrame>` — allocated each iteration, but dropped at end of loop\n- `terminal.draw(...)` — ratatui renders to an internal buffer, which should be reused\n- `RenderContext::from_parts(...)` — creates references, no heap allocations\n- `handle_chat_key(...)` — might modify `input_state` but doesn't grow unboundedly\n\nUnless the agent is constantly producing frames that fill the mpsc channel. But `drain_frames()` drains ALL of them each iteration.\n\nWait, but what if the loop iteration takes very long (e.g., render takes seconds)? Then frames accumulate in the channel between drain calls. But `drain_frames()` should still drain all accumulated frames once rendering completes.\n\nHmm, what if the agent crashes and restarts in a loop? No, there's no restart mechanism.\n\nOK, I'm going to take a different approach. Let me add a MAX_CHANNEL cap to the AgentConnection, so if frames accumulate beyond a threshold, old frames are dropped. This prevents the memory growth even if the event loop can't keep up.\n\nAlso, let me add a fallback: if the TUI mode fails (e.g., terminal setup), fall back to readline mode. This ensures the user can always use DSX.\n\nActually, let me do something even better. Let me check if the issue is with `crossterm::event::poll()` specifically. Let me modify the event loop to use `stdin.read_line()` like the readline version, but still use ratatui for display. This avoids crossterm's event system entirely.\n\nNo, that won't work with ratatui because ratatui needs raw mode.\n\nOK, let me try the simplest possible fix: add a maximum channel size to AgentConnection.","signature":"1258a55f-1e12-4138-bdc2-32626efbd5a2"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":48,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":274816,"output_tokens":1403,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"fde35716-da92-4ab6-808b-210cf2bfc6ae","timestamp":"2026-05-15T04:21:43.072Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
