{"parentUuid":"308efe8f-831d-4dc3-bda5-0bff9a9aae87","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"12c5770d-aa18-4cf8-914f-62a81bc3aa25","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the issue. The channel `std_mpsc::channel()` creates an unbounded channel. If the background reader thread reads frames faster than the main thread drains them, the channel fills up with frames, consuming memory.\n\nBut in normal operation, the agent only sends frames in response to user messages. So the channel should be nearly empty most of the time. Unless there's a situation where the agent sends infinite frames (a bug in the agent).\n\nWait — could the agent be in a tight loop sending frames? Let me check the agent's behavior when `HP_CONFIG` has the API key but the HP connection fails.\n\nIn the agent's `runner.rs`:\n1. `connect_hp()` tries to connect to HP → if it fails, returns `(None, None)`\n2. The agent's main loop handles user_input by calling `handle_user_input`\n3. `handle_user_input` checks `hp_reader` and `hp_writer` — if None, falls back to echo\n4. Echo just writes `{\"type\":\"echo\",\"data\":...}` once\n\nThat's one frame per user input. Not an infinite loop.\n\nBut what if `reader.lines()` in the background thread is reading garbage from stdout? If the agent writes something unexpected to stdout (not valid JSON), the reader thread would log a warning and continue. But it wouldn't send frames to the channel.\n\nActually, wait — what if the `log::warn!()` output goes to stdout instead of stderr? That would corrupt the JSON-LP stream. Let me check what logger is configured.\n\nLooking at the agent code, there's no logger initialization in `runner.rs`. The `dsc_log` module is not called. So `log::warn!()` would go to... by default, the `log` crate doesn't have an output. It needs a logger implementation like `env_logger` or `simplelog`. Since none is configured, `log::warn!()` is silently swallowed.\n\nSo the reader thread should work correctly. Invalid frames are skipped silently.\n\nOK, I'm going to make a practical fix:\n\n1. Use a `sync_channel` with a bounded buffer (100 frames max) instead of an unbounded channel\n2. Add a `--readline` flag for fallback mode\n3. This prevents memory growth regardless of the root cause\n\nFor the sync_channel approach:\n```rust\nlet (frame_tx, frame_rx) = std::sync::mpsc::sync_channel(100);\n```\n\nWith `sync_channel(100)`, if the channel has 100 frames and the sender tries to send another, it blocks until the receiver drains one. This prevents the channel from growing.\n\nBut there's a problem: blocking the reader thread would also block the agent's stdout, which could cause the agent to hang. So I need to use `try_send()` instead of `send()`:\n\n```rust\nif frame_tx.try_send(frame).is_err() {\n    // Channel full — discard oldest frames or just skip\n    break;\n}\n```\n\nWait, `try_send` on a `SyncSender` returns `Err(TrySendError::Full)` if the channel is full. I should handle this by discarding the frame (which means the TUI loses a frame, but that's better than OOM).\n\nActually, with `sync_channel`, the sender blocks on `send()` when the buffer is full. The background thread would block, which means the agent's stdout pipe fills up, which means the agent blocks on `write()`. This creates backpressure all the way back to the agent. That's actually good behavior!\n\nSo the fix:\n```rust\nlet (frame_tx, frame_rx) = std::sync::mpsc::sync_channel(100);\n// ...\nmatch frame_tx.send(frame) {  // blocks if channel is full\n    Ok(_) => {}\n    Err(_) => break, // receiver dropped\n}\n```\n\nThis creates natural backpressure: if the TUI can't keep up, the agent will be blocked from sending more frames. The agent will block on writing to stdout, which means it can't process more input until the TUI drains the channel.\n\nThis is exactly the right behavior for preventing memory growth.\n\nLet me implement this fix. I'll also keep `try_recv`