{"parentUuid":"aea304cc-7756-4dd1-acb0-a3e2d03e09ff","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"647192b4-a719-4e9a-8ae7-50feb3147346","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking me to think about the root cause of the CPU 100% + memory filling up issue WITHOUT reading any more code. Let me think about this from first principles.\n\nThe system architecture:\n```\ndsx (TUI) → spawn → dsx agent → spawn → dsx tools\n                                → connect → dsx hp (TCP daemon)\n```\n\nThe TUI event loop:\n```\nloop {\n    poll keyboard\n    drain agent frames  \n    render\n    throttle sleep\n}\n```\n\nThe user says:\n1. CPU 100% immediately\n2. Memory fills up immediately\n3. No TUI display\n\nThe key insight: I couldn't reproduce this in my test environment (0% CPU, 5MB RAM). The difference must be environmental.\n\nWait — the user said \"不显示tui\". If the TUI doesn't display at all, maybe the issue is BEFORE the event loop. Maybe the terminal setup (`enable_raw_mode`, `EnterAlternateScreen`) is silently failing or crashing, and then... what? The program either exits or continues without rendering.\n\nBut the user also says CPU and memory spike. If the program exited, there'd be no CPU/memory spike. So something is running.\n\nLet me think about what could cause:\n1. No visible output\n2. 100% CPU\n3. Infinite memory growth\n\n**Hypothesis: The issue might not be in the TUI at all.**\n\nWhat if the problem is in the **agent** or **HP** processes? When `dsx` starts:\n1. It spawns HP (if not running)\n2. It spawns agent\n3. Agent spawns tools\n4. Agent connects to HP\n\nIf any of these processes goes into a tight loop, it would consume CPU. And if it's generating output (e.g., writing to stdout in a loop), the TUI's background reader thread would read it and fill up the channel.\n\nBut the agent blocks on `read_line()` from stdin. The tools block on `read_line()`. The HP blocks on `accept()` or