pl`:\n```rust\nif cli.readline {\n    return run_readline(&mut conn);\n}\n```\n\nSo `run_readline` borrows `conn` and runs. It doesn't create a new `AgentConnection`.\n\nLet me trace more carefully:\n\nWhen `--readline` is used:\n1. `run_impl()` calls `ensure_hp_running()` — starts HP if needed\n2. Creates `config` and `conn = AgentConnection::spawn(...)` — spawns agent\n3. Calls `run_readline(&mut conn)` — enters readline loop\n4. Loop reads stdin, sends to agent, drains frames\n\nIn step 2, `AgentConnection::spawn()` spawns the agent and starts the reader thread. The reader thread reads from the agent's stdout. While no user input is sent, the agent hasn't written anything to stdout, so the reader thread blocks on `reader.lines()`.\n\nBut wait — does the agent write to stdout during startup? Let me re-check the agent's runner:\n\n```rust\npub fn run() {\n    eprintln!(\"dsx-agent starting\");\n    // ...\n    let (mut hp_reader, mut hp_writer) = connect_hp();\n    // Drain register response\n    if let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n        let _ = read_frame(r);\n    }\n    // ... spawn tools ...\n    write_frame(&mut tools_writer, r#\"{\"type\":\"tools_init\",...}\"#);\n    let ready = read_frame(&mut tools_reader).unwrap_or_default();\n    eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: tools → {ready}\");\n    // Main loop\n    loop {\n        match read_frame(&mut tui_reader) {\n            Some(frame) => {\n                // ...\n            }\n            None => break,\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe agent sends `{\"type\":\"tools_init\"}` to the tools subprocess (NOT to stdout). The agent reads `{\"type\":\"tools_ready\",\"tools\":[]}` from tools' stdout (NOT its own stdout). The agent only writes to its own stdout in `handle_user_input()` via `write_frame(&mut tui_writer, ...)` where `tui_writer` is `std::io::stdout()`.\n\nSo the agent's stdout should be empty until a user_input is processed. The reader thread should block.\n\nUnless... the agent's `handle_user_input` is calle