stdout?\n\nLet me check `registration::build_tool_manager()`.\n\nActually, I think there might be a MUCH simpler explanation. Let me look at the umbrella binary's main.rs:\n\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let _ = std::env::set_var(\"DSX_SINGLE_BINARY\", \"1\");\n    let role = std::env::args().nth(1).unwrap_or_default();\n    match role.as_str() {\n        \"hp\" => dsx_hp::runner::run(),\n        \"agent\" => dsx_agent::runner::run(),\n        \"tools\" => dsx_tools::run(),\n        _ => {\n            if let Err(e) = dsx_tui::run() {\n                eprintln!(\"dsx: TUI error: {e}\");\n                std::process::exit(1);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhen the user runs `./dsx` (no arg), it calls `dsx_tui::run()`. The TUI then spawns `Command::new(&exe).arg(\"agent\")`. Since `DSX_SINGLE_BINARY=1` is set, this works.\n\nBut wait — the TUI starts the HP daemon via `ensure_hp_running()`. It spawns `Command::new(&exe).arg(\"hp\")`. This means the umbrella is called as `dsx hp`, which calls `dsx_hp::runner::run()`. The HP daemon then runs as a separate process. This is correct.\n\nThen the TUI spawns the agent via `AgentConnection::spawn()`. This runs `Command::new(&exe).arg(\"agent\")`. This calls the umbrella as `dsx agent`, which calls `dsx_agent::runner::run()`. The agent then spawns tools.\n\nBut here's the thing: when the umbrella calls `dsx_agent::runner::run()`, it sets `DSX_SINGLE_BINARY=1`. The agent checks this and spawns tools as `Command::new(&exe).arg(\"tools\")`. This calls the umbrella as `dsx tools`, which calls `dsx_tools::run()`.\n\nBut `dsx_tools::run()` calls `ipc::ipc_main_loop(&mut build_tool_manager())`. The `ipc_main_loop()` function:\n\n```rust\npub fn ipc_main_loop(manager: &mut ToolManager) {\n    let stdin = io::stdin();\n    let stdout = io::stdout();\n    let mut reader = io::BufReader::new(stdin.lock());\n    let mut writer = stdout.lock();\n    \n    loop {\n        let frame = match read_frame(&mut reader) {\n            Ok(Some(f)) => f,\n            Ok(None) => break,\n            Err(e) => { ... }\n        };\n        match frame {\n            InboundFrame::Init { ... } => {\n                manager.apply_init(...);\n                let tools = manager.filtered_defs();\n                if write_frame(&mut writer, &OutboundFrame::Ready { tools }).is_err() { break; }\n            }\n            ...\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe tools process reads stdin (from the agent) and writes to stdout (to the agent). It blocks on `read_line()`. When it receives Init, it sends Ready. Then it goes back to blocking.\n\nSo there's no tight loop in the tools process.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The user says \"内存爆涨\" (memory explodes). What grows?\n\nIn `--readline` mode:\n1. Main thread blocks on `stdin.read_line()`\n2. Reader thread blocks on agent stdout's `reader.lines()`\n3. Agent blocks on `read_frame()` from TUI stdin\n\nIf everyone is blocked, nothing grows. But the user says memory DOES grow even before typing.\n\nThis means either:\n1. Someone is NOT blocked\n2. Or some buffer is filling up\n\nCould the `stdin.lock()` in the agent or tools be causing issues? Or the `BufReader` internal buffer?\n\nActually, I just realized something. When the reader thread in `AgentConnection` reads from the agent's stdout via `BufReader::new(stdout)` — this is a buffered reader. If the reader thread buffers data and then blocks, the buffered data is in the reader's internal buffer. This is usually 8KB and doesn't grow.\n\nBut what about `stdout.lock()` in the tools process? `io::stdout().lock()` acquires a lock on stdout. If the tools process holds stdout's lock, the agent can't write to it. But the agent's `tui_writer` is `std::io::stdout()`, which is a different handle... wait, no. Tools and agent are separate processes. They have their own stdout. There's no conflict.\n\nOK, I think I need to check something else. The user said \"initr。。什么什么的一直弹出来\". This sounds like the `eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: tools → ...\")` message being printed repeatedly. But that message only prints once per agent startup.\n\nUnless the agent is restarting. Or unless the agent's `run()` function is being called in a loop.\n\nWait — could the issue be that `dsx agent` is NOT blocking in the main loop? If the TUI closes the pipe to the agent's stdin, the agent would read EOF from `read_frame()`, break out of the loop, and exit. Then the `AgentConnection` background reader thread sees EOF and breaks. But the TUI can then detect the child exit...\n\nBut in `--readline` mode, we DON'T close the pipe. The pipe stays open. So the agent blocks on `read_frame()`. No issue.\n\nHmm, let me re-examine the `--readline` code:\n\n```rust\nfn run_readline(conn: &mut AgentConnection) -> anyhow::Result<()> {\n    let stdin = std::io::stdin();\n    let mut input = String::new();\n    println!(\"dsx v{} -- type /quit to exit\", ...);\n    loop {\n        input.clear();\n        print!(\"> \");\n        std::io::stdout().flush()?;\n        match stdin.read_line(&mut input) {\n            Err(_) | Ok(0) => break,\n            Ok(_) => {}\n        }\n        let msg = input.trim();\n        if msg.is_empty() { continue; }\n        if msg == \"/quit\" || msg == \"/exit\" {\n            conn.shutdown();\n            break;\n        }\n        conn.send_frame(TuiFrame::UserInput { text: msg.to_string() });\n        for frame in conn.drain_frames() {\n            println!(\"  ← {frame:?}\");\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\nWait! There's a bug! After the user types a message and we call `drain_frames()`, we might NOT get all the frames because the agent might not have sent the response yet! The agent sends the response asynchronously. So `drain_frames()` might return an empty vec on the first call. But subsequent calls (on the next input) would get the old response.\n\nActually, no. The agent processes frames synchronously:\n1. Reads user_input\n2. Sends api_chat to HP (blocking)\n3. Receives