{"parentUuid":"a7806f5f-4507-44a9-b798-c52d9552ab52","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"bb37f4da-04a4-4f88-989d-e7272bcfd8fc","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user says \"dsx-tui: initlized\" (which is likely \"dsx-tui: terminal initialized\" from my earlier debug code) keeps scrolling. This means the message is being printed repeatedly. \n\nThis message is only printed once in the event loop startup. If it's scrolling, it means either:\n1. The `run_impl` function is being called in a loop (TUI restarting)\n2. Or the event loop is somehow printing this repeatedly - but it's before the loop\n\nWait, let me re-check my code. I added:\n```rust\neprintln!(\"dsx-tui: terminal initialized\");\n```\n\nThis is in `run_impl()`, right before the `event_loop` call. It should print once. Unless `run_impl()` is being called multiple times.\n\nBut `run_impl()` is called once from `run()`. Unless the program is being restarted.\n\nActually wait - the user said with `--readline` the CPU slowly rises and memory explodes. And they see \"dsx-tui: initlized\" scrolling. But `--readline` mode calls `run_readline()`, NOT the event loop. And `run_readline()` doesn't print \"terminal initialized\".\n\nSo \"dsx-tui: initlized\" must be coming from the NON-readline mode (the ratatui mode). The user might be running WITHOUT `--readline`, and the ratatui mode is broken.\n\nBut the user said \"内存不输入readline就爆长\" - memory explodes without readline, and \"输入是cpu缓慢涨，内存爆涨\" with readline.\n\nSo BOTH modes have the memory issue. And \"initlized\" scrolling in non-readline mode.\n\nWait, could it be that the `dsx-tui: terminal initialized` message is in the agent's stderr? Let me check: the agent's runner has no such message. But `run_impl()` has it.\n\nThe message is from `run_impl()` before entering the event loop. If it scrolls, something is calling `run_impl()` repeatedly.\n\nCould the umbrella binary be calling `dsx_tui::run()` repeatedly? Let me check:\n\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let _ = std::env::set_var(\"DSX_SINGLE_BINARY\", \"1\");\n    let role = std::env::args().nth(1).unwrap_or_default();\n    match role.as_str() {\n        \"hp\" => dsx_hp::runner::run(),\n        \"agent\" => dsx_agent::runner::run(),\n        \"tools\" => dsx_tools::run(),\n        _ => {\n            if let Err(e) = dsx_tui::run() {\n                eprintln!(\"dsx: TUI error: {e}\");\n                std::process::exit(1);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`dsx_tui::run()` is called ONCE. If it returns Ok, the program exits. If it returns Err, prints error and exits. No loop.\n\nBut wait — what if the umbrella is invoked as `dsx agent` or `dsx hp` or `dsx tools` from within the TUI? Let me trace:\n\n1. User runs `./dsx`\n2. Umbrella calls `dsx_tui::run()`\n3. TUI's `ensure_hp_running()` spawns `./dsx hp` (umbrella with role=hp)\n4. TUI's `AgentConnection::spawn()` spawns `./dsx agent` (umbrella with role=agent)\n5. Agent spawns `./dsx tools` (umbrella with role=tools)\n\nThese are all CHILD processes. They don't cause the PARENT (TUI) to restart.\n\nUnless... there's a fork bomb! What if spawning `./dsx hp` indirectly spawns another `./dsx` which spawns another `./dsx hp`?\n\nWhen `ensure_hp_running()` spawns `./dsx hp`, the HP process starts. It calls `dsx_hp::runner::run()` which listens on TCP and accepts connections. The HP process does NOT spawn any other processes. No fork bomb.\n\nWhen `AgentConnection::spawn()` spawns `./dsx agent`, the agent process starts. It calls `dsx_agent::runner::run()` which:\n1. Connects to HP\n2. Spawns `./dsx tools` (subprocess)\n3. Enters main loop\n\nBut `spawns ./dsx tools` IS a subprocess of the agent. And `dsx tools` calls `dsx_tools::run()` which enters the IPC loop. NO further spawning.\n\nSo there's no recursive process creation.\n\nHmm, but what if `dsx_tui::run()` is called and fails immediately, then the umbrella exits, but the terminal is in