ges could scroll.\n\nWAIT. That's it! The diagnostic `eprintln!` in the event loop!\n\n```rust\nlet r1 = r0.elapsed();\nif r1.as_micros() > 5000 {\n    eprintln!(\"dsx: draw took {}ms\", r1.as_millis());\n}\n```\n\nAnd:\n```rust\nlet frame_ms = t0.elapsed().as_millis();\nif frame_ms > 100 {\n    eprintln!(\"dsx: slow frame {}ms\", frame_ms);\n}\n```\n\nThese would keep printing if the render is slow or frames are slow! Not \"initlized\" though.\n\nHmm, but in the readline mode, the event loop isn't used. So these diagnostics shouldn't fire.\n\nOK actually, I think the root cause might be something I've been completely overlooking. Let me look at the `AgentConnection` code one more time. When `run_impl` creates an `AgentConnection` and then calls `run_readline`, the `AgentConnection` is created with a background reader thread. But `run_readline` doesn't use the AgentConnection! It creates its OWN agent pipes.\n\nSo we have:\n1. `AgentConnection::spawn()` is called → spawns agent + background reader thread\n2. `run_readline(&mut conn)` is called → spawns ANOTHER agent (direct pipe)\n3. Two agent processes running!\n4. The first agent (from AgentConnection) has its stdout being read by the background thread\n5. The second agent (from run_readline) has its stdout being read directly\n6. The background thread from step 4 keeps reading frames from the first agent\n7. These frames go into the sync_channel(100)\n8. When the channel fills up, the background thread blocks on send()\n9. The first agent's stdout pipe fills up, blocking the first agent\n\nBut wait, the first agent also spawns `dsx tools`, which spawns another process. So we'd have:\n- TUI process\n- HP process (daemon)\n- First agent + its tools\n- Second agent + its tools\n\nThat's up to 6 processes! This could certainly cause CPU and memory issues.\n\nBut more importantly: the `run_readline` function I just wrote does NOT use `conn` at all! It creates its own agent. So the `AgentConnection conn` created in `run_impl` is wasted but its background thread is still running.\n\nTHIS IS THE BUG! In `run_impl`:\n```rust\nlet mut conn = AgentConnection::spawn(agent_bin.as_deref(), config)?;\n// ...\nif cli.readline {\n    return run_readline(&mut conn);\n}\n```\n\n`AgentConnection::spawn()` starts the agent AND the reader thread. Then `run_readline` spawns ANOTHER agent. The first agent's reader thread keeps running, reading from agent #1's stdout. Even though nobody reads from the channel, the reader thread blocks the agent (backpressure via sync_channel(100) → pipe buffer → agent stdout).\n\nBut wait, the agent #1 doesn't write to stdout until it receives a user_input. So the reader thread blocks on reading from stdout. No issue.\n\nThe problem is that `run_impl` creates AgentConnection BEFORE checking if `readline` mode. The agent #1 is spawned unnecessarily. When `run_readline` returns, `conn` is dropped, which calls `AgentConnection::shutdown()`, which sends shutdown to agent #1. Agent #1 exits, reader thread stops.\n\nBut during the `run_readline` execution, there are TWO agents running. Agent #2 is the one actually being used. Agent #1 is idle.\n\nNow, would this cause memory explosion? Only if agent #1 is somehow doing work. But it's blocked on stdin, waiting for frames from the TUI (which never come since `run_readline` uses a different pipe).\n\nActually wait, agent #1's stdin IS connected to `AgentConnection::stdin`. But `run_readline` creates its own pipe to agent #2. So data going to agent #2 doesn't affect agent #1.\n\nThe TUI's main stdin (terminal input) is read by `run_readline`'s `std::io::stdin().read_line()`. This data is written to agent #2's stdin. Agent #1 never sees it.\n\nSo agent #1 truly is idle. No CPU, no memory growth.\n\nUnless... agent #1's TOOLS process is doing something. Agent #1 spawns `dsx tools` as a subprocess. The tools process enters `ipc_main_loop()` and waits for Init on stdin. Since agent #1 never sends Init (it's waiting for user_input), tools just blocks. Tools process: one thread, a few KB of memory. Fine.\n\nSo two agents doesn't explain the issue. But it's still wasteful.\n\nOK, let me just focus on fixing the immediate problem. Let me revert `run_impl` to NOT create AgentConnection when using `--readline`. This eliminates the background thread and extra agent entirely.\n\nActually, the simpler fix: check `cli.readline` BEFORE creating AgentConnection.","signature":"bb37f4da-04a4-4f88-989d-e7272bcfd8fc"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":148,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":294656,"output_tokens":2789,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"f2551928-cd6b-4b1c-b19e-5ffcabbd0aad","timestamp":"2026-05-15T04:34:02.664Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
