{"parentUuid":"c774e25a-cd08-429a-8ae7-7f8fc707067f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"7c6cf6db-7a68-4778-a31e-c269a5f8d7e5","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The agent can't connect to HP. This means the HP daemon isn't running. And `ensure_hp_running()` was called from `run_impl()`, but NOT from `run_readline_direct()`. \n\nWait, `run_readline_direct()` doesn't call `ensure_hp_running()` because it bypasses `run_impl()`. The user ran `--readline` which calls `run_readline_direct()` directly without ensuring HP is running.\n\nBut also, the user might have had a stale HP from a previous session that got killed. Let me fix `run_readline_direct()` to also ensure HP is running.\n\nBut also: this explains why the TUI mode had issues. If HP is not running, the agent can't connect to HP, so the agent falls back to \"echo\" mode. But in echo mode, the agent echoes user input back — which could cause constant frame sending if something is wrong.\n\nActually wait — the agent falls back gracefully:\n```rust\nfn connect_hp() -> (Option<...>, Option<...>) {\n    // ...\n    let stream = match TcpStream::connect(...) {\n        Ok(s) => s,\n        Err(e) => {\n            eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: cannot connect HP: {e}\");\n            return (None, None);\n        }\n    };\n```\n\nSo the agent runs without HP and echoes user input. That means the agent would echo back TUI messages. In --readline mode, user types, agent echoes. That should work fine (just a chat echo test).\n\nBut in ratatui mode without HP, the agent echoes. The TUI sends user_input, the agent processes it and echoes back. This should work normally.\n\nBut the user said \"dsx-agent: cannot connect HP: Connection refused (os error 111)\". This means `ensure_hp_running()` didn't start the HP (or the HP failed to start, or the previous HP died).\n\nLet me fix `run_readline_direct()` to call `ensure_hp_running()` first. And also, let me check if the HP daemon is actually getting started properly.\n\nActually, looking at the error: \"Connection refused\" means the HP port file exists but the HP process is dead (stale port file). This confirms what I saw earlier — the port file from a previous test session was stale.\n\nThe fix is: `ensure_hp_running()` should check if HP is ACTUALLY running (try connecting to the port), not just check if the port file exists.\n\nLet me also add `ensure_hp_running()` to `run_readline_direct()`.","signature":"7c6cf6db-7a68-4778-a31e-c269a5f8d7e5"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":125,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":299904,"output_tokens":855,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"db305f9e-ea1a-4126-8719-ef7350265670","timestamp":"2026-05-15T04:36:27.414Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
