{"parentUuid":"d3b1c289-14db-4c0f-bd43-7e2ba6721cbf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"8a165f31-ef00-4684-8b9c-f049a3f4aadd","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user confirmed:\n1. 180KB/s network traffic only happens with the ratatui TUI mode, not with `--readline`\n2. `--readline` works fine\n\nThis is actually very informative. The 180KB/s network traffic is likely from crossterm's event system in raw mode. When the terminal enters raw mode, crossterm might be sending terminal interrogation sequences or the terminal itself is sending periodic updates.\n\nActually, 180KB/s of network traffic is NOT from crossterm events - those are local terminal interactions, not network. Network traffic means actual data going over the network.\n\nWait - but DSX makes API calls to DeepSeek's API (over HTTPS). If the ratatui TUI is making repeated API calls, that would generate 180KB/s of traffic. But the user isn't typing anything - who's making API calls?\n\nUnless the agent is stuck in a loop sending api_chat requests to HP. Let me think...\n\nIn the ratatui mode, when the user types a message:\n1. TUI sends user_input to agent\n2. Agent sends api_chat to HP\n3. HP makes HTTP call to DeepSeek API\n4. Response flows back\n\nBut if the ratatui event loop is triggering `SendMessage` actions repeatedly (e.g., from some keyboard event being mistaken for Enter), the agent would keep making API calls, generating network traffic.\n\nOr more likely: the `InputAction::SendMessage` is being triggered by something other than Enter - perhaps a terminal escape sequence that crossterm parses as an Enter key event. The agent processes it, sends to HP, HP calls the API, gets a response, sends it back. The TUI renders it. Then the event is re-triggered...\n\nWait, but I changed the event loop. Now keyboard reading is in a separate thread. But the user said it still has issues.\n\nActually, the 180KB/s network traffic +