per second to DeepSeek. Each call is ~10KB round-trip.\n\nFor this to happen, the TUI must be sending `user_input` frames to the agent at a rate of ~10-20 per second. Each one triggers the agent → HP → API → response flow.\n\nThis means `crossterm::event::read()` is returning key events that trigger `SendMessage` roughly 10 times per second.\n\nOr maybe it's not key events but the agent itself that's stuck in a loop. If the agent receives a `user_input` that's empty or triggers an error, it might send a response but not consume the input. Then the next... no, the agent processes one frame per loop iteration.\n\nActually, I bet the issue is this: in the agent's `handle_user_input`, when there's no HP, it echoes back. But what does it echo back?\n\n```rust\nfn handle_user_input(\n    frame: &str,\n    hp_reader, hp_writer,\n    conversation,\n    tui_writer,\n) {\n    let v: serde_json::Value = match serde_json::from_str(frame) {\n        Ok(v) => v,\n        Err(_) => { write_frame(tui_writer, error); return; }\n    };\n    let text = match v.get(\"text\").and_then(|t| t.as_str()) {\n        Some(t) if !t.is_empty() => t,\n        _ => return,\n    };\n    conversation.push(json!({\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":text}));\n    \n    let reader = match hp_reader { Some(r) => r, None => { echo; return; } };\n    let writer = match hp_writer { Some(w) => w, None => { echo; return; } };\n    \n    // Send to HP\n    let chat_req = json!({...});\n    write_frame(writer, &chat_req.to_string());\n    if let Some(resp) = read_frame(reader) {\n        // process response\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd in the main loop, after `handle_user_input`:\n```rust\nhandle_user_input(&frame, &mut hp_reader, &mut hp_writer, &mut conversation, &mut tui_writer);\nwrite_frame(&mut tui_writer, r#\"{\"type\":\"done\"}\"#);\n```\n\nSo each `user_input` sends ONE request to HP and waits for ONE response. Then writes done. No loop.\n\nUnless something in the TUI is sending `user_input` frames in a loop without the user pressing keys. Let me reconsider.\n\nOh wait - I just realized something about the keyboard thread. `crossterm::event::read()` in a raw mode terminal... In some konsole versions, entering raw mode triggers terminal responses like `\\033[?6c` (DA - Device Attributes). crossterm might parse this as a key event.\n\nBut this is a one-time response, not continuous.\n\nUnless the terminal is sending `\\033[1;nR` (Cursor Position Report) continuously. Some terminals do this in raw mode.\n\nOR... 180KB/s of network traffic could be the the HTTP/2 or TLS keepalive. If reqwest keeps the connection alive and there's some keepalive traffic. But 180KB/s is way too much for keepalive.\n\nOK, I think the most likely explanation is that the TUI is repeatedly calling `conn.send_frame(TuiFrame::UserInput { text: trimmed })` in a loop. The trimmed text comes from the chat buffer which might not be cleared properly, or `SendMessage` is triggered repeatedly.\n\nBut looking at the code, after `SendMessage` is handled, the loop continues. The keyboard buffer IS cleared by `handle_chat_key` (which sets `state.chat.buffer.clear()` after returning `SendMessage`). Wait, actually it doesn't! Let me check:\n\n```rust\nKeyCode::Enter => {\n    if key.modifiers == KeyModifiers::CONTROL {\n        // Ctrl+Enter = insert newline\n    } else {\n        InputAction::SendMessage(state.chat.buffer.clone())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`handle_chat_key` returns `SendMessage(state.chat.buffer.clone())` but does NOT clear the buffer. The event loop handles the action:\n\n```rust\nInputAction::SendMessage(text) => {\n    let trimmed = text.trim().to_string();\n    if !trimmed.is_empty() {\n        // ... send to agent\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe buffer is NOT cleared after sending! So if the same key event triggers `SendMessage` again (e.g., from a repeat key or another Enter event before the buffer is cleared), the same message gets sent again.\n\nBut this wouldn't cause a loop because `SendMessage` is only triggered by pressing Enter, and there's only one Enter event per keypress.\n\nUnless... the `handle_chat_key` function is returning `SendMessage` for a key other than Enter. Let me check: is there another key that triggers `SendMessage`?\n\nLooking at the handler: only `KeyCode::Enter` (without Ctrl) triggers `SendMessage`. So this seems fine.\n\nOK, I'm going down rabbit holes. The 180KB/s network traffic is the most concrete clue. Let me just look at what's happening at the network level. But the user already confirmed `--readline` doesn't have this traffic. So the issue is specific to the ratatui event loop.\n\nAnd the user said to give up on the ratatui fix for now. So let me accept `--readline` as the working mode and move on.\n\nActually, maybe the user just wants me to acknowledge the situation and move forward. Let me do that.","signature":"8a165f31-ef00-4684-8b9c-f049a3f4aadd"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":327,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":324864,"output_tokens":2207,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"7ac71765-351a-437f-a619-60b65af55023","timestamp":"2026-05-15T04:50:50.402Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
