{"parentUuid":"69336d7d-21f3-44fc-8623-ea62ff508e4d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"fcc408ee-f8e0-4ac8-8afb-928b69fb286e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants to discuss the root cause rather than me jumping to solutions. They're pushing back on my assumption that the keyboard input system (crossterm::event) is the root cause.\n\nLet me reconsider the evidence:\n\n1. `--readline` works perfectly - no CPU, no memory, no network\n2. ratatui mode has: CPU 100%, memory growth, 180KB/s network traffic\n3. The 180KB/s network traffic is KEY - it means API calls are being made continuously\n\nIf keyboard events were the trigger, typing wouldn't cause 180KB/s of sustained network traffic. Something is CAUSING continuous API calls.\n\nWait - what's making API calls? The agent, when it receives `user_input` frames. If the TUI is sending continuous `user_input` frames, the agent would keep calling the API.\n\nBut the TUI only sends `user_input` when the user presses Enter... unless something is generating fake Enter presses.\n\nActually, let me think about this from the 180KB/s angle. A single API response is maybe 1-10KB. 180KB/s means many API calls per second. That's not typing - that's a tight loop somewhere.\n\nWhat if the issue isn't keyboard events at all, but something else in the event loop that's triggering continuous requests?\n\nWait - I need to look at what the event loop actually DOES with keyboard events vs what happens automatically.\n\nIn the current code (with keyboard reader thread):\n1. Keyboard events are polled via channel\n2. Agent frames are drained\n3. Render if dirty\n4. Sleep 33-50ms\n\nThe agent frames come from... well, the agent process. The agent only sends frames in response to `user_input`. If no user_input is sent, no frames come through.\n\nBut wait - the 180KB/s network traffic. Could this be from the HP daemon making continuous API calls? Let me think about what could cause the agent to send continuous api_chat requests.\n\nActually, I just realized something. The `180KB/s` network traffic when ratatui mode is on but the user isn't even typing anything. This means something is continuously generating network activity.\n\nWhat if it's not the agent calling the API, but something else? Let me think about what network connections DSX makes:\n1. HP → DeepSeek API (HTTPS) - only when api_chat is called\n2. Agent → HP (TCP localhost) - only when processing user_input\n\nFor 180KB/s to be flowing, there must be continuous HTTP requests from HP to DeepSeek. That means the agent is sending continuous api_chat requests to HP.\n\nWhy would the agent send continuous api_chat requests? Because it's receiving continuous `user_input` frames from the TUI.\n\nWhat would cause the TUI to send continuous user_input frames? A tight loop sending `SendMessage` actions.\n\nOK so the question is: what in the event loop causes continuous SendMessage actions?\n\nLooking at my latest event loop code:\n```rust\nwhile let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv() {\n    dirty = true;\n    match event {\n        Event::Key(key) => {\n            // ... handle key\n            match action {\n                InputAction::SendMessage(text) => {\n                    // SEND MESSAGE!\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis only runs when there's a keyboard event. If no keyboard event, it doesn't run. But with the keyboard reader thread, `crossterm::event::read()` blocks until a real event comes.\n\nUnless... the keyboard reader thread is receiving continuous events from the terminal. In raw mode, terminals send various escape sequences (cursor position reports, etc.). If crossterm's `read()` interprets these as events, the thread would keep sending events to the channel.\n\nBut `read()` returns ONE event at a time. Each call blocks until the next event. It doesn't loop. So even if events come 60 times per second, the loop processes one at a time.\n\nBut 60 events/sec × 33ms sleep = ~2 events per iteration. With each event being something that gets `_ => {}` treatment, no API calls would be made.\n\nUnless... the events are being interpreted as key events that trigger `SendMessage`. For example, if crossterm in raw mode interprets incoming bytes as key events with empty keys, or Enter keys.\n\nOK actually, I just thought of something else entirely. What if the 180KB/s is NOT from API calls but from the **agent's background reader thread** and the **tools process**?\n\nWhen the TUI starts the agent (via AgentConnection.spawn()), the agent spawns tools. The agent and tools communicate via stdin/stdout pipes. If the pipe is corrupted or misconfigured, data could flow continuously.\n\nBut `--readline` also spawns an agent (via direct pipe), so the same agent+tools pipe would exist. Why wouldn't `--readline` have the 180KB/s?\n\nThe difference: `--readline` uses `run_readline_direct()` which spawns the agent differently. It doesn't use `AgentConnection`. The `AgentConnection` has a background reader thread that reads from the agent's stdout. If that thread is busy (e.g., because the agent is writing continuously), it could cause constant channel activity.\n\nBut the agent only writes to stdout in response to user_input frames. If no user_input frames are sent...\n\nWait wait wait. Let me re-read the AgentConnection background thread code:\n\n```rust\nthread::Builder::new()\n    .name(\"agent-reader\".into())\n    .spawn(move || {\n        let reader = BufReader::new(stdout);\n        for line in reader.lines() {\n            match line {\n                Ok(line) => {\n                    if line.trim().is_empty() { continue; }\n                    match serde_json::from_str::<AgentFrame>(&line) {\n                        Ok(frame) => {\n                            if frame_tx.send(frame).is_err() { break; }\n                        }\n                        Err(e) => {\n                            log::warn!(\"agent: invalid frame: {}  line={}\", e, line);\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n                Err(e) => {\n                    log::error!(\"agent: stdout read error: {}\", e);\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    });\n```\n\nThis iterates over lines from the agent's stdout. The `reader.lines()` iterator calls `read_line()` which blocks until a line is available. When a line comes, it tries to deserialize it as `AgentFrame`. If deserialization fails, it logs a warning.\n\nBut `log::warn!()` goes nowhere (no logger initialized). The loop continues.\n\nNow, what if the agent's stdout is sending data in a format that doesn't match AgentFrame? Each failed deserialization would still consume CPU and memory (the line is read into a String). And if the agent IS sending data continuously...\n\nBut WHY would the agent be sending data continuously? Unless something is wrong with the agent or the tools subprocess.\n\nActually, let me think about what happens when `AgentConnection::spawn()` creates the connection. The agent process starts, connects to HP, spawns tools, etc. Then the TUI's run_impl continues to set up the event loop. In the event loop, `conn.drain_frames()` is called each iteration.\n\nBut what if the agent's stdout pipe is configured incorrectly? What if `Stdio::piped()` for stdout is creating some kind of loop?\n\nNo, that doesn't make sense. The pipe is between the TUI (parent) and agent (child). The agent writes to its stdout, the TUI reads from the pipe.\n\nActually, I just had an important insight. In the ORIGINAL working readline code (before my AgentConnection refactor), the flow was:\n\n```\nTUI: spawn agent → write to agent's stdin → read from agent's stdout\nAgent: read from stdin (TUI) → process → write to stdout (TUI)\n```\n\nThis is a simple request-response cycle. Both sides block on reads.\n\nWith AgentConnection, there's a BACKGROUND THREAD reading from the agent's stdout. This means:\n1. Agent writes to stdout\n2. Background thread reads it\n3. Background thread sends to channel\n4. Main thread drains from channel\n\nThis is still a request-response cycle, just with an extra step. The difference is that the background thread is ALWAYS reading — it doesn't block the main thread.\n\nOK, the 180KB/s insight is the most important clue. Let me think again...\n\n180KB/s is continuous. It doesn't spike on keypress. It's steady. This rules out keyboard-triggered events.\n\nWhat if the HP daemon is the source of the network traffic? Maybe the HP is running a continuous check or heartbeat that's making HTTP requests?\n\nLooking at the HP runner, it has:\n1. TCP listener (blocks on accept)\n2. Heartbeat ticker (every 15 seconds)\n3. No periodic HTTP calls\n\nSo the HP shouldn't make any network calls until it receives an `api_chat` frame.\n\nUnless... there's a SECOND HP running. Or an old HP from a previous test.\n\nWait, I just realized: `ensure_hp_running()` starts HP if it's not running. But it uses `TcpStream::connect_timeout` to check. If the check fails (stale port file), it starts a new HP. The old HP might still be running on a different port, and the agent might be connected to the old one.\n\nOr more likely: multiple tests have left multiple HPs running, all listening and consuming resources.\n\nBut this wouldn't explain 180KB/s network traffic. HP doesn't make outbound HTTP calls unless it receives api_chat.\n\nOK, I think the most productive thing to do is let the user tell me what they think it is. They asked to discuss, so let me lay out my reasoning and ask what they think.","signature":"fcc408ee-f8e0-4ac8-8afb-928b69fb286e"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":140,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":325248,"output_tokens":2324,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"afae7ba2-b0fd-4f6c-b4ff-418b61481ebc","timestamp":"2026-05-15T04:52:57.503Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
{"parentUuid":"afae7ba2-b0fd-4f6c-b4ff-418b61481ebc","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"fcc408ee-f8e0-4ac8-8afb-928b69fb286e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"你说得对，我前后矛盾了。重新捋一遍证据：\n\n**事实：**\n1. `--readline` → 一切正常（无 CPU、无内存、无网络）\n2. ratatui 模式 → CPU 100%、内存涨、**180KB/s 持续网络流量**\n3. 180KB/s 是连续流量，按键不可能触发这个量级\n\n**矛盾：**\n如果真是 crossterm::event 疯狂产生按键事件→触发 SendMessage→agent 调 API，那 180KB/s 的 HTTPS 流量说得通。但 `--readline` 不吃 CPU/内存说明管道没问题。唯一区别是 `--readline` 用 `run_readline_direct()` **绕过 AgentConnection**。\n\n所以嫌疑指向 **AgentConnection 本身**——它的 background 线程或 channel 机制可能出问题。但我又说不通：agent 没人发消息就不会写 stdout，线程就阻塞在读上，哪来的流量？\n\n你觉得最可能是哪一层？HP、agent、tools、还是 TUI 的 AgentConnection？"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":140,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":325248,"output_tokens":2324,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"0bc58e44-be1d-4ce0-80ed-5ccf5395d990","timestamp":"2026-05-15T04:53:00.500Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
