{"parentUuid":"79237061-ad55-4df6-a582-cb2dde093b7c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"149b3956-b64e-4764-95fa-2b52f884fbfc","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So `--demo` mode works perfectly - no CPU, no memory issues, keyboard input works. The user says \"typing works too\" which means the keyboard reader thread + event loop works fine.\n\nThis means the problem is NOT in:\n- ratatui rendering\n- crossterm terminal setup\n- keyboard event handling\n- the event loop structure\n\nThe problem IS in:\n- AgentConnection background thread + channel\n- The agent subprocess itself\n- Or the interaction between them\n\nWhen real processes are spawned (agent + tools + HP), something goes wrong. The most likely culprit is the `AgentConnection` background reader thread. When it reads from the agent's stdout, something happens that causes the issue.\n\nLet me think about what could be different:\n1. AgentConnection spawns the agent subprocess\n2. The agent spawns tools subprocess\n3. The reader thread reads agent's stdout\n4. The channel buffers frames\n\nWait - could the issue be that when the user types in `--demo` mode, keyboard works because `AgentConnection::spawn(None, ...)` creates a stub with no child process. When `send_frame()` is called, it silently does nothing because stdin is None.\n\nIn normal mode, when the user types characters, `handle_chat_key()` modifies the input buffer and returns `InputAction::None`. On Enter, `SendMessage(text)` is returned, which calls `conn.send_frame(...)` and `state.push_message(...)`.\n\nBut wait - the event loop sets `dirty = true` for ALL key events in the `_ => dirty = true` catch-all. Every keystroke triggers a render. That's fine.\n\nThe issue must be related to the agent subprocess. Let me think about what happens when `conn.send_frame()` is called:\n1. It writes to the agent's stdin\n2. The agent processes it\n3. The agent writes to its stdout\n4. The reader thread reads it\n5. The reader thread sends to the channel\n6. The main thread drains the channel\n\nIf any of these steps has a tight loop or memory allocation issue, it could cause the problems we see.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the original `--readline` mode. It works perfectly! And it spawns the agent directly, communicates via raw pipes. No background thread, no channel.\n\nThe `--readline` mode works because:\n1. It spawns agent directly (same as AgentConnection)\n2. It reads from agent stdout directly (same thread)\n3. No background thread, no channel\n4. When waiting for agent response, it blocks on `read_line()` — no CPU usage\n\nWhat if the issue is simply that the reader thread always has something to read? When the agent is running but idle (no user input), it blocks on `read_frame()`. No output to stdout. Reader thread blocks on `read_line()`. No CPU.\n\nBut when the agent sends a response, the reader thread reads it, sends to channel, and goes back to blocking. This should be fine.\n\nUnless... the agent is constantly producing output! What if the agent or tools processes are writing to stdout continuously?\n\nWait - I had an idea. The `dsx agent` command runs the umbrella binary with arg \"agent\". The umbrella sets `DSX_SINGLE_BINARY=1`. The agent then spawns `dsx tools` — the umbrella again with arg \"tools\". \n\nBut what if `dsx tools` somehow produces output on the agent's stdout? No, tools' stdout is piped to the agent. The agent's stdout goes to the TUI.\n\nActually, could there be a pipe issue? What if the agent spawns tools and the pipes are somehow crossed?\n\nLet me look at the spawn code more carefully:\n\nAgent's runner.rs:\n```rust\nlet mut tools_cmd = Command::new(exe.to_path_buf())\n    .stdin(Stdio::piped())\n    .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n    .spawn()\n    .expect(\"...\");\nlet tools_stdin = tools_child.stdin.take().unwrap();\nlet tools_stdout = tools_child.stdout.take().unwrap();\nlet mut tools_reader = BufReader::new(tools_stdout);\n```\n\nThis spawns tools with piped stdin/stdout. The agent reads tools' stdout and writes to tools' stdin. Tools' stderr is inherited from the agent.\n\nWait - what about the agent's stdin/stdout? The agent was spawned by AgentConnection in the TUI:\n```rust\nlet mut child = Command::new(bin)\n    .arg(\"agent\")\n    .stdin(Stdio::piped())\n    .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n    .stderr(Stdio::inherit())\n    .spawn()?;\n```\n\nAgent's stdin → TUI's AgentConnection writes TuiFrame\nAgent's stdout → TUI's reader thread reads AgentFrame\nAgent's stderr → goes to TUI's stderr (terminal)\n\nThis is correct. No crossed pipes.\n\nOK, let me try a different approach. Let me check if the issue is in `AgentConnection` by making the normal mode use AgentConnection but with NO background thread (like readline). If that works, the issue is in the background thread + channel.\n\nActually, I can just have a flag that makes `AgentConnection::spawn()` not start a reader thread but use synchronous reads instead. But that would require significant restructuring.\n\nOr I can create a \"stub with process\" mode: spawn the agent but use the readline-style direct pipe instead of AgentConnection's threaded approach. This would isolate whether the issue is in the threading or in the agent itself.\n\nLet me try something simpler first. What if in `run_demo()`, I actually spawn the agent but use the same keyboard thread approach? If it still works, the issue is with something else.\n\nActually, you know what? Let me just create a `run_impl()` variant that spawns the agent using `run_readline_direct`'s approach but renders with ratatui. This eliminates AgentConnection entirely from the normal mode.\n\nActually, this is getting too complex with workarounds. Let me just add the agent spawn and pipe reads to the demo mode and see if it breaks.","signature":"149b3956-b64e-4764-95fa-2b52f884fbfc"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":49,"cache_creation