flow for message 1:\n1. Send message 1, `awaiting = true`, render (dirty)\n2. Main loop enters `while awaiting`\n3. Blocks on `read_line()` \n4. Agent sends `api_response` with content\n5. `stream.apply_payload()` → render is dirty\n6. Actually wait, we're still INSIDE `while awaiting`. We read another line.\n7. Agent sends `done`\n8. `state.push_message(msg)`, `awaiting = false`\n9. Exit the `while awaiting` loop\n10. Continue to render → draws the response!\n\nSo step 5-8 all happen inside the `while awaiting` loop. But the render only happens AFTER we exit the loop. During streaming (between `api_response` and `done`), dirty=true but no render happens.\n\nThen after `awaiting = false`, the code reaches the render section. But it's also processed keyboard events first (the `while let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv()` at the top of the loop).\n\nSo the flow is:\n1. After `while awaiting` finishes (got done message)\n2. Back to top of `loop`\n3. `while let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv()` — drain queued keyboard events from the reader thread\n4. If the user was typing, their typed characters are all queued up!\n5. The `handle_chat_key` processes them one by one\n6. This includes the Enter key that submits message 2!\n7. So message 2 is sent, `awaiting = true` again\n8. Then render is called — shows BOTH message 1 response AND message 2 being sent\n\nThe problem is:\n1. While awaiting response, keyboard events accumulate in the channel\n2. After response arrives, the main loop processes ALL accumulated events before rendering\n3. If the user typed and pressed Enter during the wait, message 2 gets sent immediately\n4. The render shows both responses together\n\nActually, the real issue is simpler. The render happens AFTER the keyboard processing and AFTER awaiting. So:\n1. Send msg1, awaiting=true, start waiting\n2. Response arrives, awaiting=false\n3. Go to top of loop\n4. Process keyboard events → user typed Enter for msg2 → send msg2, awaiting=true\n5. Start awaiting msg2 response\n6. Render happens NOW (after dirty=true) but awaiting=true again\n7. Now we're back in step 3 for the next iteration\n\nWait, no. Let me re-trace:\n\n```\nLoop iteration N:\n1. Process keyboard → no events (user is waiting)\n2. awaiting=true, enter while awaiting\n3. read_line blocks... response arrives!\n4. apply_payload (dirty=true)\n5. read_line blocks... done arrives!\n6. push_message (dirty=true), awaiting=false\n7. Exit while awaiting\n8. Render! → shows msg1 response\n9. Sleep 33ms\n\nLoop iteration N+1:\n1. Process keyboard → user typed characters + Enter!\n2. Send msg2, awaiting=true\n3. awaiting=true, enter while awaiting\n4. read_line blocks...\n```\n\nThis should work! The render at step 8 in iteration N shows msg1 response. Then iteration N+1 sends msg2. The user should see msg1 response before they need to type msg2.\n\nBut the user says: \"发第一个消息它会卡，但是发第二个消息它才回复第一个消息\" - msg1 causes a hang, and msg2 triggers msg1's response.\n\nWhat if the `while awaiting` is not rendering at all? The response is received, awaiting becomes false, but then... what if the render breaks?\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the code flow:\n\n```rust\n// ── Read agent response (only when awaiting) ──\nwhile awaiting {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    match agent_reader.read_line(&mut line) {\n        ...\n        Ok(_) => {\n            dirty = true;\n            ...\n            if trimmed.contains(\"\\\"done\\\"\") {\n                ...\n                awaiting = false;\n                state.mode = Mode::Chat;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nInside `while awaiting`, when we receive `done`, we set `awaiting = false`. Then the loop condition is checked again, `awaiting` is false, so the loop exits. `dirty = true` was already set.\n\nThen we reach the render section:\n```rust\nif dirty {\n    if terminal.draw(|f| crate::render::render_all(f, &state, &conn, &stream)).is_err() {\n        break;\n    }\n    dirty = false;\n    std::thread::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(33));\n}\n```\n\nThis should render. BUT — what if `terminal.draw()` returns an error? The `is_err()` check breaks the main loop! If render fails (like the terminal was resized and ratatui can't handle it), the loop breaks, and the program exits.\n\nActually, that would explain the user's symptom: \"it hangs\" → the render fails, loop exits, but the user doesn't notice because the cleanup restores the terminal quickly.\n\nMore likely: the render doesn't fail, but the flow goes through the top of the loop FIRST. After `while awaiting` exits, we go to the top of the outer `loop`:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    // Process keyboard\n    while let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv() {\n        ...\n        if let InputAction::SendMessage(text) = action {\n            let t = text.trim().to_string();\n            if !t.is_empty() {\n                ...\n                awaiting = true;\n```\n\nIf the keyboard thread has queued events (including the Enter key for msg2), they're processed HERE before rendering. If msg2's text happens to be the already-typed content from msg1... no, the chat buffer is cleared after SendMessage.\n\nWait, I think the issue is simpler. The keyboard thread captures ALL keyboard events while the main loop is waiting for the agent response. When the user presses Enter to send msg1, the keyboard thread sends the Enter event. Then any subsequent key presses (typing msg2) are also queued.\n\nWhen the main loop finishes `while awaiting` (got response to msg1), it goes to the top and processes ALL queued keyboard events. This includes the key presses that compose msg2. The chat handler processes them one by one, building up msg2 in the input buffer. Then the final Enter event triggers SendMessage for msg2.\n\nALL of this happens BEFORE the render. So by the time the user sees the display, msg2 has already been sent.\n\nThe fix: render BEFORE processing keyboard events. Move the render section to the END of the loop (after agent reading), but BEFORE going back to the top for keyboard processing.\n\nActually wait, the render IS at the end. Let me re-check:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    // 1. Keyboard processing\n    while let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv() { ... }\n    if quit { break; }\n    \n    // 2. Agent response reading\n    while awaiting { ... }\n    \n    // 3. Render\n    if dirty { terminal.draw(...); ... }\n    else { sleep(50ms); }\n}\n```\n\nThe render at step 3 happens AFTER keyboard processing at step 1. So when awaiting=false (after response received), we go through step 1 (process all queued keys including msg2's Enter), then step 2 (awaiting is false, skip), then step 3 (render).\n\nThe fix: process agent responses BEFORE keyboard events:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    // 1. Agent response (affects what we display)\n    while awaiting { ... }\n    \n    // 2. Keyboard (affects what we'll send next)\n    while let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv() { ... }\n    if quit { break; }\n    \n    // 3. Render\n    if dirty { terminal.draw(...); }\n}\n```\n\nBut wait, this doesn't fully fix it. The problem is that between response and render, we process keyboard events. If the user typed during the wait, those events get processed before the first render.\n\nThe real fix: Render immediately after agent response, before processing keyboard:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    // 1. Agent response\n    while awaiting { ... }\n    \n    // 2. Render if needed (shows response before processing more keys)\n    if dirty { terminal.draw(...); dirty = false; }\n    \n    // 3. Keyboard\n    while let Ok(event) = key_rx.try_recv() { ... }\n    if quit { break; }\n    \n    // 4. Render again if needed (shows typed characters)\n    if dirty { terminal.draw(...); dirty = false; }\n    \n    // 5. Sleep\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nActually, the SIMPLEST fix: process agent responses BEFORE keyboard, and check dirty both before and after keyboard.\n\nOr even simpler: just move the render before the keyboard processing. And render aga