{"parentUuid":"0952cb33-d71b-42f8-a65a-65dfa2379a02","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"2eec8a3c-6e60-449a-8fb9-ea876653af82","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is right. The compaction approach I implemented is too brute-force. The original architecture had a much more sophisticated approach to context management that prioritized cache hit rate.\n\nThe Anthropic API's prompt caching works by caching the prefix of the conversation. If the system prompt + early messages stay the same across requests, they get cached and subsequent requests are faster and cheaper.\n\nThe original DSX had:\n1. `ContextAssembler` - manages message alternation\n2. `ContextBuilder` - builds context with proper ordering:\n   - System prompt (cached)\n   - Long-term memory / architecture invariants (cached)\n   - Tools definitions (cached)\n   - Conversation history\n   - Recent messages (uncached)\n\nThe key insight: the FIRST ~80% of the context should be STABLE across requests to maximize cache hits. Only the last 20% (recent messages + new user input) changes.\n\nThe original code had `compact_turns` with levels L1-L4 that would progressively summarize/compress older turns rather than just dropping them.\n\nBut I should look at what the original code actually did. Let me check if there's anything in the dsx-agent library that handles this.\n\nActually, looking at the library modules:\n- `context.rs` - has `ContextBuilder` \n- `conversation.rs` - has `ContextAssembler`\n- `orchestrator/context_compactor.rs` - has compaction logic\n\nThe `ContextAssembler` is the core type-level state machine that enforces message alternation (user → assistant → tool_result → assistant, etc.). \n\nThe `ContextBuilder` takes `&ContextAssembler` and outputs `(String, Vec<AnthropicMessage>, TokenBreakdown)`. It assembles the system prompt + long-term memory + conversation into the final context.\n\nThe compaction logic (`compact_turns`) works on `Turn` objects (user+assistant pairs), not flat messages. L0 = no compaction, L1 = zlib compress long tool results, L2 = drop tool results, L3 = keep only last N turns, L4 = aggressive drop.\n\nFor the API call, the flow was:\n1. `ContextAssembler::to_anthropic_messages()` converts to Anthropic format\n2. These messages are sent to the API\n3. The API caches the prefix\n\nSo the proper approach for cache hit rate is:\n1. System prompt first (always cached)\n2. Then fixed content (tools, memory)  \n3. Then oldest conversation messages (these get cached)\n4. Then newest conversation messages (these change each request)\n\nThe current `runner.rs` skips ALL of this and just sends raw JSON messages.\n\nThe question is: should I wire in the library's ContextAssembler and ContextBuilder? Or implement a simpler version that follows the same principles?\n\nLet me check what it would take to use the library's ContextAssembler. The `conversation.rs` module has `ContextAssembler` which is a public type. And `context.rs` has `ContextBuilder`.\n\nBut `runner.rs` is a binary module, not the library. I'd need to import from `crate::conversation::ContextAssembler` etc.\n\nActually, looking at the runner.rs file location: it's at `crates/dsx-agent/src/runner.rs`, which is part of the `dsx-agent` crate. It CAN use `crate::conversation::ContextAssembler` and other library types.\n\nLet me check what the library's ContextAssembler API looks like and whether it can be used in the runner.\n\nActually, the issue is that `ContextAssembler` works with `Message` types from dsx-types, not `serde_json::Value`. The current conversation is `Vec<serde_json::Value>`. I'd need to convert between them.\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me take a simpler approach that follows the cache-aware principles:\n\n1. The conversation is ordered: oldest first, newest last\n2. The first messages (system prompt, early conversation) are stable → they'll be cached\n3. The last messages change each request → they won't be cached\n\nFor cache-friendly ordering:\n- The API request has `system` field (always cached separately)\n- Then `messages` array: oldest first, newest last\n\nThe Anthropic API caches the prefix starting from the beginning. So:\n- Messages[0..N] where N is ~80% of context → cached\n- Messages[N..] → fresh\n\nMy compaction should:\n1. NOT remove from the FRONT (that kills cache)\n2. Instead, compact/summarize old tool results in the MIDDLE\n3. Or, if needed, replace old assistant messages with summaries\n\nFor a practical implementation:\n1. Track total tokens\n2. If > 300K tokens, look for large tool_result messages in the middle of the conversation\n3. Replace the content of long tool results with a short summary marker\n4. This preserves the message count and cache prefix, just reduces size\n\nThis is similar to what the original L1/L2 compaction did.\n\nLet me implement a better compaction:\n- Find messages with role \"tool_result\" that have very long content\n- Truncate their content to ~200 chars with a \"[...truncated...]\" suffix\n- This preserves message structure (cache), reduces total tokens\n\nLet me also think about what the user actually needs right now. They said \"确保memory/上下文拼凑正确，否则会浪费token\" - ensure memory/context assembly is correct to avoid wasting tokens.\n\nThe key issue is: the current code sends ALL messages with every request, growing unboundedly. The user wants cache-friendly behavior.\n\nLet me implement a better approach:\n1. Track approximate token count\n2. If over ~300K tokens, identify large tool results and truncate them\n3. Never remove messages from the front\n4. Keep the conversation structure intact for cache prefix stability\n\nI don't need to wire up the full ContextAssembler for this. A smarter truncation function is sufficient.","signature":"2eec8a3c-6e60-449a-8fb9-ea876653af82"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":52,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_inpu