{"parentUuid":"04448087-834a-45b5-95f0-b672695562f4","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"547b52bd-0982-4921-b9ce-44527df21586","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full flow. The key change is:\n\n1. `handle_connection` currently reads one frame, dispatches, writes one response. For streaming, `api_chat` needs to write multiple frames to the stream.\n\n2. `dispatch_frame` returns a `String` — but for streaming, it needs to write directly to the stream.\n\n3. `handle_api_chat` currently collects all streaming output and returns a single JSON response. For streaming, it needs to write content_delta frames as they arrive.\n\nThe approach:\n1. Change `handle_connection` to pass a writer to `dispatch_frame` for api_chat frames\n2. Change `handle_api_chat` to accept a writer and write content_delta frames as they stream in\n3. The final api_response frame is still written at the end\n\nActually, the simplest approach is:\n- Make `handle_api_chat` take a `&mut impl Write` parameter\n- Inside the async block, for each `StreamEvent::ContentDelta`, write a `content_delta` JSON-LP line to the writer\n- At the end, write the final `api_response` frame\n\nBut `handle_connection` uses `stream.try_clone()` which gives a `TcpStream` for writing. The issue is `handle_api_chat` runs `rt.block_on()` which blocks the thread. If we pass the writer in, we need to make sure it's not borrowed across the async boundary.\n\nActually, looking at the current code more carefully:\n```rust\nlet response = dispatch_frame(trimmed, &service);\nif let Ok(mut w) = stream.try_clone() {\n    let _ = writeln!(w, \"{response}\");\n    let _ = w.flush();\n}\n```\n\nFor streaming, I need to change this so that for `api_chat` frames, the writer is passed into the handler and multiple lines are written. Let me restructure:\n\n1. Change `dispatch_frame` to accept an optional writer. For `api_chat`, it streams multiple frames. For other frame types, it returns a single string.\n2. Or better: make `handle_connection` detect `api_chat` frames and call `handle_api_chat_streaming` directly, passing the writer.\n\nLet me go with option 2 — it's cleaner. I'll:\n1. Create `handle_api_chat_streaming(line, writer)` that writes content_delta frames as they arrive\n2. Modify `handle_connection` to call this variant for api_chat frames\n\nLet me implement this.\n\nActually, looking at the code flow more carefully, I realize the simplest change is:\n\nIn `handle_connection`:\n```rust\nlet trimmed = buf.trim();\nif trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\") {\n    // Streaming path\n    if let Ok(mut w) = stream.try_clone() {\n        handle_api_chat_streaming(trimmed, &mut w);\n    }\n} else {\n    let response = dispatch_frame(trimmed, &service);\n    if let Ok(mut w) = stream.try_clone() {\n        let _ = writeln!(w, \"{response}\");\n        let _ = w.flush();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnd `handle_api_chat_streaming` writes:\n- `{\"type\":\"content_delta\",\"delta\":\"...\"}` for each token\n- `{\"type\":\"api_response\",\"content\":\"...\",\"tool_calls\":[...],...}` at the end\n\nThis is the minimal change. Let me implement it now.\n\nLet me also read the rest of handle_api_chat to see the full async block.","signature":"547b52bd-0982-4921-b9ce-44527df21586"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2345,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":145152,"output_tokens":854,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"6e4fc395-fefd-4ccc-9818-cfb6c4e902f5","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:00:20.396Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d