{"parentUuid":"025ba5db-d00c-41f2-ad5f-4072b092a9dc","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"b4a1ddba-31ea-49ac-87a9-8dc4e9730264","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user says they type a message and it gets sent (they can see it in the chat panel), but there's no response from the agent. This is not a keyboard input issue — the message IS being sent. The agent isn't responding.\n\nLet me think about what could cause this. The agent should receive the user_input frame, process it through HP, and send back a response. Let me check if there's an issue with the agent side.\n\nBut first, the readline mode DID work when I tested it just now. So the agent IS functional. The issue might be specific to the TUI mode.\n\nWait, when I tested readline mode, the agent restored a 127-message session from a live session file. In TUI mode, the same thing happens — the agent restores the session. But the TUI sends `user_input` frames via stdin, and the agent processes them.\n\nLet me look at how the agent handles `Discriminant(0)` — this is what the log shows: `dsx-agent: tui ← Discriminant(0)`. This suggests the TuiToAgent enum is being received but maybe not handled correctly? The `Discriminant(0)` is the debug output of the enum variant.\n\nActually, looking at the agent runner, when a frame is received:\n```rust\nlet frame: TuiToAgent = match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut tui_reader) {\n```\n\nBut then:\n```rust\neprintln!(\"dsx-agent: tui ← {frame}\");\n```\n\n`TuiToAgent` derives `Debug`, so it prints the variant name. `Discriminant(0)` is weird — that's not a normal Debug output for an enum with `#[serde(tag = \"type\")]`. This suggests the enum is being printed as just its discriminant number.\n\nWait, actually `TuiToAgent` uses `#[serde(tag = \"type\")]` which makes it an internally-tagged enum. But the `Debug` implementation would print the variant name, not \"Discriminant(0)\".\n\nHmm, actually, looking at the agent-rewire's changes more carefully — they said they migrated to `dsx_proto::read_frame`. But wait, the `read_frame` in dsx-proto is generic: `pub fn read_frame<T: for<'de> Deserialize<'de>>(...)`.\n\nLet me check what the agent runner actually sends from the TUI side. The TUI writes:\n```rust\nlet _ = writeln!(agent_writer, r#\"{{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"{msg}\"}}\"#);\n```\n\nAnd the agent reads: `TuiToAgent` which expects `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"...\"}`. The `TuiToAgent` enum has:\n```rust\n#[serde(rename = \"user_input\")]\nUserInput { text: String },\n```\n\nThis should deserialize correctly. Let me check if there's an issue with the `write_to_agent` format.\n\nWait, actually, let me re-read the write_to_agent:\n```rust\nlet mut write_to_agent = |msg: &str| {\n    let _ = writeln!(agent_writer, r#\"{{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"{msg}\"}}\"#);\n    let _ = agent_writer.flush();\n};\n```\n\nThis uses `r#\"...\"#` raw string. Inside the raw string, `{{` becomes `{`, `}}` becomes `}`, and `{msg}` captures the `msg` variable. So the output would be: `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"<msg>\"}` — correct.\n\nBUT WAIT. I just noticed something. In the old code, the format was:\n```rust\nwriteln!(agent_writer, r#\"{{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"{msg}\"}}\"#)\n```\n\nIn my refactored code it's the same. But let me check the old code more carefully — was it using `format!` or `writeln!`?\n\nActually, looking at the ORIGINAL code from the conversation summary:\n```rust\nlet mut send_to_agent = |msg: &str| {\n    state.push_message(Message::user(msg));\n    let _ = writeln!(agent_writer, r#\"{{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"{msg}\"}}\"#);\n    let _ = agent_writer.flush();\n    stream.clear();\n    awaiting = true;\n    state.mode = Mode::Streaming;\n};\n```\n\nThis is the same format as what I have. So the formatting is correct.\n\nBut actually, now I realize — the agent-rewire changed the agent side to use `dsx_proto::read_frame::<TuiToAgent>()`. This uses serde to deserialize. The old agent code used raw string matching:\n```rust\nframe.contains(\"\\\"type\\\":\\\"user_input\\\"\")\n```\n\nMaybe the TuiToAgent deserialization is failing? Let me check what error would happen.\n\n`TuiToAgent` in dsx-proto:\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(tag = \"type\")]\npub enum TuiToAgent {\n    #[serde(rename = \"user_input\")]\n    UserInput { text: String },\n    #[serde(rename = \"tool_call\")]\n    ToolCall { id: String, name: String, action: String, args: serde_json::Value },\n    #[serde(rename = \"shutdown\")]\n    Shutdown,\n}\n```\n\nThis expects `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"...\"}`. The TUI sends exactly that. So deserialization should work.\n\nOK but wait, maybe the issue is that the agent runner is eating the frame silently? Let me check the agent runner's main loop:\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut tui_reader) {\n        Ok(Some(frame)) => {\n            match frame {\n                TuiToAgent::UserInput { text } => {\n                    handle_user_input(&mut agent, &text, ...)\n                }\n                TuiToAgent::Shutdown => break,\n                _ => { /* echo */ }\n            }\n        }\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis should handle UserInput correctly. Unless the deserialization is failing silently and the frame is being ignored.\n\nActually, let me check: `dsx_proto::read_frame` returns `io::Result<Option<T>>`. If deserialization fails, it returns an error. Let me see what the runner does:\n\nIn the runner:\n```rust\nlet frame: TuiToAgent = match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut tui_reader) {\n    Ok(Some(f)) => f,\n    Ok(None) => break,\n    Err(e) => {\n        log::error!(\"TUI parse error: {e}\");\n        continue;\n    }\n};\n```\n\nIf there's a parse error, it logs and continues. But the user would see the error in stderr.\n\nHmm, actually let me just check the IMPLIED agent-rewire changes. The agent-rewire's report says they migrated to typed enums. But maybe the dsx_proto::read_frame is using `io::Result` instead of the old `Option<String>`. The io::BufRead's `read_line` might be getting a different value.\n\nActually wait, let me check if this is a runtime error that I can reproduce. Let me run dsx again but capture stderr.\n\nActually, let me just re-read the agent runner to check if the main loop is processing frames correctly.\n\nActually, I think the problem might be much simpler. Let me check if the agent is even STARTING. In my test run, it did start. But the key question is: does the TUI properly spawn the agent?\n\nIn run_impl(), the agent is spawned as:\n```rust\nlet mut child = Command::new(&agent_bin).arg(\"agent\")\n    .stdin(Stdio::piped()).stdout(Stdio::piped()).stderr(Stdio::null())\n    .spawn()?;\n```\n\nstderr is nulled! So if the agent crashes on startup or has a parse error, we'd never see it in the TUI. That's a problem.\n\nBut the agent DID work in readline mode when I tested it. So it's starting fine.\n\nHmm, let me try to reproduce. Let me run dsx and look at stderr more carefully in TUI mode... actually I can't test TUI mode from here.\n\nLet me just focus on the most likely cause: maybe the agent-rewire's changes to the main loop broke something. Let me re-read the runner's main loop to see if it's correct.\n\nActually wait, I just thought of something. The agent-rewire said they changed the main loop to use `dsx_proto::read_frame::<TuiToAgent>()` and process `TuiToAgent::UserInput { text }`. But what about the `handle_user_input` function signature? Let me check if it still accepts `&str` for the frame text.\n\nIn the OLD code:\n```rust\nhandle_user_input(\n    &mut agent, &frame,\n    &mut hp_reader, &mut hp_writer,\n    &mut tools_reader, &mut tools_writer,\n    &mut tui_writer,\n);\n```\n\nThe `&frame` was the raw JSON string. It was parsed inside `handle_user_input`. In the new code, the frame is already parsed as `TuiToAgent::UserInput { text }`, so it should pass `&text` instead of `&frame`.\n\nBut wait, looking at the runner code I read earlier, `handle_user_input` still accepts `frame: &str` as the second parameter! So if the new code passes `text` (the user's message text), it would be \"hello\" — `handle_user_input` would then try to parse \"hello\" as JSON, which would fail.\n\nTHIS IS THE BUG. The `handle_user_input` function still expects a JSON frame string like `{\"type\":\"user_input\",\"text\":\"hello\"}`, but the caller now passes just the text \"hello\" extracted from the deserialized `TuiToAgent::UserInput { text }`.\n\nLet me verify this by reading the runner more carefully.\n\nOK actually, I'm not 100% sure this is the case. Let me just try to reproduce the issue by checking the binary's stderr output.\n\nActually wait, I tested the readline mode earlier and it worked! Let me look at that test more carefully. The readline mode uses `run_readline_direct()` which has a completely separate implementation from `run_impl()`. So readline working doesn't tell us anything about the TUI mode.\n\nBut the key question: does the TUI mode work? The user says it doesn't.\n\nLet me actually look at what the agent runner's main loop does now. The agent-rewire reported:\n```\nTUI → Agent: `frame.contains(\"\\\"type\\\":\\\"user_input\\\"\")` → `TuiToAgent::UserInput { text }`\n```\n\nThis means the agent's main loop now parses `TuiToAgent` enum. But `handle_user_input` still takes `frame: &str` and internally does `serde_json::from_str(frame)` to extract text. If the caller is passing `text` (just the message like \"hello\"), the JSON parsing would fail.\n\nBut I can't be sure without reading the actual code. Let me check.","signature":"b4a1ddba-31ea-49ac-87a9-8dc4e9730264"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":985,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":228736,"output_tokens":2530,"server_tool_use":{"w