{"parentUuid":"51d1d0c2-29c6-46df-b6d4-d579a218b31e","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"a500fad7-ce27-4c1d-a5a0-914965667a6e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! The agent output shows:\n- Connected to HP\n- Tools OK\n- Found live session, restored 127 messages\n- Then \"shutdown complete\" WITHOUT any API call\n\nThe agent never called HP! It restored the session, did some processing, and then shut down. The stdout only has `{\"type\":\"done\"}`.\n\nThis means `handle_user_input` sent NOTHING to stdout. But the main loop sent `Done`. So either:\n1. handle_user_input didn't write any response (the HP call path wasn't entered)\n2. handle_user_input returned because HP was not available\n\nWait, the stderr shows \"connected to HP on port 44923\". But then in the main loop:\n```rust\nif let (Some(ref mut hp_r), Some(ref mut hp_w)) = (&mut hp_reader, &mut hp_writer) {\n    handle_user_input(...);\n} else {\n    // Echo\n}\n```\n\nThe HP reader and writer SHOULD be Some since the agent connected. But let me check...\n\nActually, wait. The `if let` destructures `&mut hp_reader` and `&mut hp_writer`. These are `&mut Option<BufReader<...>>` and `&mut Option<BufWriter<...>>`. If they're Some, the if branch executes. If None, the else branch executes (echo).\n\nBut the output shows no echo either. So the if branch must have been taken. And handle_user_input should have sent api_chat...\n\nUnless handle_user_input panics or returns early. Let me check the handle_user_input flow more carefully for the restored session case.\n\nWhen a session is restored with 127 messages:\n1. `agent.session_seed` is set\n2. `agent.ctx` (ContextAssembler) has 127 messages\n3. `agent.messages` is synced from ctx\n\nThen in the tool-calling loop:\n```rust\nfor _round in 0..10 {\n    session_persistence::repair_messages(agent);\n    let (system, _anthropic_msgs, breakdown) = orchestrator::context_compactor::prepare_and_compact(agent);\n    let messages_json = messages_to_json(&agent.messages);\n    let chat = AgentToHp::ApiChat { ... };\n    let _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(hp_writer, &chat);\n    // Read HP response...\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the `messages_to_json` filters out messages that can't be converted:\n```rust\nfn messages_to_json(messages: &[Message]) -> Vec<serde_json::Value> {\n    messages.iter().filter_map(|m| {\n        let mut v = serde_json::json!({\"role\": m.role.clone()});\n        if let Some(ref c) = m.content { v[\"content\"] = serde_json::json!(c); }\n        ...\n        Some(v)\n    }).collect()\n}\n```\n\nThis function returns all messages (no filter condition that returns None). So the messages_json should contain all 128 messages (127 from session + 1 user message). But with 128 messages at ~50 tokens each, that's ~6400 tokens. With a 1M context limit, this should be fine.\n\nThen the agent sends api_chat to HP and waits for a response. The HP should process the request and send back content_delta + api_response frames.\n\nBut the output shows NO HP-related error and NO API response. The agent just shut down.\n\nAh wait — I just realized: The agent's main loop processes ONE frame, then iterates. On the NEXT iteration, it reads from stdin again. But stdin has the `sleep 25` still running — the pipe is still open. So `read_frame` would block on the next `read_line`.\n\nBUT — `handle_user_input` was called with `hp_reader` and `hp_writer`. Inside the function, it wrote the api_chat frame and then entered the streaming loop to read HP response. If the HP response arrives, the function should process it and return. If the HP takes a long time, the function would BLOCK.\n\nSo either:\n1. HP responded quickly with an error\n2. HP didn't respond at all\n3. handle_user_input encountered an error\n\nThe fact that there's no stderr output from handle_user_input suggests either (1) or (3) happened silently.\n\nWait, I just had an idea. Let me check if the HP's `handle_api_chat_streaming` is actually being invoked. The stderr for HP is also redirected — but in the TUI's `ensure_hp_running`, HP is spawned with `stderr(Stdio::null())`. So HP errors are also hidden.\n\nBut actually, HP was already running (from the previous test). The port was 44923. Let me kill the old HP and start fresh.\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. Let me just check if the STREAMING handler is the problem. The non-streaming `handle_api_chat` should still work. Let me check: when the agent sends `api_chat`, the HP's `handle_connection` detects it with `trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\")` and routes to `handle_api_chat_streaming`. Maybe this function has a bug.\n\nLet me compare: the old `handle_api_chat` returned a string and was called from `dispatch_frame`. Now for streaming, the connection handler checks `contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\")` and calls `handle_api_chat_streaming` directly.\n\nWait, but `handle_api_chat_streaming` has a DIFFERENT message conversion than `handle_api_chat`. Let me check if it also converts messages correctly.\n\nActually wait, I just realized something: `handle_api_chat_streaming` has its OWN message conversion code that I copied from `handle_api_chat`. So the conversion is identical. The key difference is that it writes multiple frames to the writer instead of returning a string.\n\nLet me also check: what if the `handle_api_chat_streaming` function is parsing the request differently? Like, what if the agent sends `AgentToHp::ApiChat` which serializes to JSON, but the HP's message conversion expects a different format?\n\nThe agent sends:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"api_chat\",\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"system\":\"...\",\"messages\":[...]}\n```\n\nThe HP's handle_api_chat_streaming parses this as:\n```rust\nlet v: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(line)?;\nlet model = v.get(\"model\")...;\nlet system = v.get(\"system\")...;\nlet messages_val = v.get(\"messages\").and_then(|v| v.as_array())?;\n```\n\nThis should work since the JSON format matches.\n\nHmm, I'm running out of ideas from code analysis alone. Let me try a direct HP test using a small Rust program. Actually, let me just use curl to test if HP responds.\n\nActually no, let me try something simpler. Let me check if the `handle_api_chat_streaming` function's message conversion is working by checking if it handles the \"tool\" role messages correctly. The restored session has tool_call and tool_result messages. If these are being converted to Anthropic format incorrectly, the HP might crash or return an error.\n\nWait, actually, maybe the HP IS returning an error, but the agent's streaming loop doesn't print it. In the streaming loop:\n```rust\nHpToAgent::Error { message } => {\n    let _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(tui_writer, &AgentToTui::Error { message: message.clone() });\n    agent.health.record_api_error();\n    return;\n}\n```\n\nThis writes ERROR to tui_writer. But the test output shows only `{\"type\":\"done\"}` — no error frame. So either HP didn't respond, or the error wasn't in the expected format.\n\nOK let me try yet another approach. Let me just disable session restore to see if the issue is related to the restored session. I'll temporarily add a flag or just delete the session files.","signature":"a500fad7-ce27-4c1d-a5a0-914965667a6e"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":398,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":249728,"output_tokens":1841,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"26df7ca9-bca9-4399-b7bd-ac6e2ecadcff","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:52:35.890Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","v