&text, hp_r, hp_w, ...);\n}\n```\n\nLine 517 uses `ref mut r` — this borrows `hp_reader` mutably. After the drain, the borrow is released. Then at line 581, `hp_reader` is borrowed again. This should work.\n\nBut wait — what if the drain consumed the reader? `dsx_proto::read_frame(r)` reads ONE line from the reader. After the drain, the reader is at position 1 (the register response was consumed). The api_chat write goes to `hp_writer` (a different object — the TcpStream write half). So the read and write halves are independent. This should be fine.\n\nOK, let me think about this differently. The handle_user_input function takes `hp_reader: &mut impl BufRead` and `hp_writer: &mut impl Write`. But the call passes `hp_r` which is `&mut BufReader<TcpStream>`, and `hp_w` which is `&mut TcpStream`.\n\nInside handle_user_input:\n1. Write api_chat to `hp_writer` — this writes to the TcpStream\n2. Read from `hp_reader` — this reads from the BufReader wrapping the TcpStream\n\nThe TcpStream is the same underlying connection. Writing to one half and reading from the other should work.\n\nBut wait — is `hp_writer` the SAME TcpStream as what `hp_reader` wraps? Let me check connect_hp:\n\n```rust\nfn connect_hp() -> Option<(BufReader<TcpStream>, TcpStream)> {\n    let stream = TcpStream::connect(...)?;\n    // Register\n    let mut writer = stream.try_clone()?;\n    write_frame(&mut writer, &reg);\n    let reader = BufReader::new(stream);\n    Some((reader, writer))\n}\n```\n\nAh HA! `writer` is `stream.try_clone()` — a CLONE of the TcpStream. And `reader` wraps the ORIGINAL stream. So the read handle and write handle are DIFFERENT TCP stream objects.\n\nBUT — does `TcpStream::try_clone()` create an independent copy? Yes, on Linux, `try_clone()` duplicates the file descriptor. So both the read and write handles refer to the same TCP connection. Writing to one and reading from the other should work.\n\nHmm, but in `connect_hp`, the registration is sent on the writer. Then the reader wraps the original stream. But the registration response comes back on both file descriptors (they refer to the same connection). The reader should be able to read the registration response because both FDs point to the same kernel socket buffer.\n\nOK this should work. Let me check if there's an issue with the reader not being able to read because the stream is in non-blocking mode or something.\n\nActually, let me just go back to basics and add some eprintln! debugging to handle_user_input to see where it's failing.\n\nWait, I just thought of another possibility. What if the `AgentToHp::ApiChat` serialization is wrong? Like, what if the JSON format the agent sends doesn't match what the HP expects?\n\nThe agent sends:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"api_chat\",\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"system\":\"...\",\"messages\":[...]}\n```\n\nThe HP checks:\n```rust\nif trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\") { ... }\n```\n\nThis should match. Then inside handle_api_chat_streaming, it parses the JSON and converts messages. If message conversion fails (returns empty vec), it returns an error:\n```rust\nif anthropic_msgs.is_empty() {\n    let _ = writeln!(writer, \"{}\", json_response(\"error\", \"api_chat: no valid messages\"));\n    return;\n}\n```\n\nMaybe the message conversion is failing? The restored session has messages with roles like \"assistant\", \"user\", \"tool_result\". My message conversion code should handle all these. But what if the new session's first message (the user input \"say hi\") has a format issue?\n\nActually wait, with a new session (no restore), there's only one message: the user's \"say hi in one word\". That should convert fine: `{role: \"user\", content: \"say hi in one word\"}` → `AnthropicMessage { role: \"user\", content: [Text { text: \"say hi in one word\" }] }`. This is valid.\n\nSo the message conversion should work. Let me think about what else could go wrong...\n\nActually, you know what, let me just test the HP directly. I'll send an a