 dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut tui_writer, &AgentToTui::Done);\n}\n```\n\nThe Done is written AFTER handle_user_input. And `dsx_proto::write_frame` writes to a pipe connected to the TUI parent process. This write should succeed as long as the pipe is open.\n\nBut in my Python test, Done was the ONLY output. No hp response before it. So handle_user_input DID return, but without writing anything to tui_writer.\n\nINSIDE handle_user_input, is there an early return path that doesn't write to tui_writer? Let me check...\n\nLooking at the function:\n1. `if text.is_empty() { return; }` — not empty\n2. Session init\n3. `if agent.session_seed.is_empty() { init_session(agent); ... }` — seed is set\n4. Skill matching\n5. `agent.ctx.push_user(text)` — pushes user message\n6. `agent.sync_messages()`\n7. Per-turn state reset\n8. `for _round in 0..10 { ... }` — the tool-calling loop\n\nInside the loop:\n```rust\nfor _round in 0..10 {\n    repair_messages(agent);\n    let (system, _, breakdown) = prepare_and_compact(agent);\n    let messages_json = messages_to_json(&agent.messages);\n    let chat = AgentToHp::ApiChat { model, system, messages, ... };\n    let _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(hp_writer, &chat);\n    \n    // Read HP response loop\n    loop {\n        let hp_resp: HpToAgent = match dsx_proto::read_frame(hp_reader) { ... };\n        match hp_resp {\n            HpToAgent::ApiResponse { ... } => {\n                ...\n                // If no tools: write AgentToTui::ApiResponse and return\n                // If tools: execute them and continue the for loop\n            }\n            HpToAgent::Error { ... } => {\n                // Write error and return\n            }\n            _ => { /* ignore */ }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe function returns from inside the HP response loop. There are two return points:\n1. After final response (no tools): writes AgentToTui::ApiResponse and returns\n2. After error: writes AgentToTui::Error and returns\n\nBoth paths write to tui_writer before returning. So the function SHOULD write something to stdout.\n\nUNLESS the HP response doesn't match any arm. For example, if HP returns a `content_delta` frame, the `_ => { /* ignore */ }` arm catches it and continues the loop. If HP returns ONLY content_delta and NEVER sends the final api_response, the loop would block forever on the next read_frame, waiting for the api_response that never comes.\n\nBUT — if HP sent content_delta, the agent would forward it to tui_writer. We should see content_delta on stdout. We don't. So either HP sent nothing, or HP sent frames that don't match any arm.\n\nWait, let me re-check the `_` arm:\n```rust\n_ => { /* ignore non-stream frames during api_chat */ }\n```\n\nIt ignores frames. But what if the HP registration response (the \"ok\" frame) was NOT fully drained? The drain code reads ONE frame:\n```rust\nif let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n    let _: Option<HpToAgent> = dsx_proto::read_frame(r).ok().flatten();\n}\n```\n\nThis reads one frame from hp_reader. But what if the HP sends MULTIPLE frames in response to registration? Or what if there's a heartbeat or other frame in the buffer?\n\nActually, the HP sends exactly one response to registration: `{\"type\":\"ok\",\"message\":\"registered\"}`. This is deserialized as `HpToAgent::Ok { message: \"registered\" }`. The drain reads it and discards it.\n\nAfter the drain, the reader should be empty. Then when api_chat is sent, the HP sends back content_delta + api_response frames. These should be read by the handler.\n\nOK I just realized I should check if the HP is really responding. Let me test by sending a minimal api_chat request directly to the HP port using Python.","signature":"7ef62c86-ef72-45d3-bad8-d695ea065e8e"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":90,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":257024,"output_tokens":1708,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"stand