{"parentUuid":"261b8c9a-9164-4060-8e30-277da797fb15","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"a2d8652a-d5dd-4885-92ca-1de20e347a7e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"HP IS working! It returned `{\"content\":\"Hi! How can I help you today?\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"type\":\"api_response\"}`. Note: this is the OLD format — no `tool_calls` wrapper, just `content` directly. This is the `handle_api_chat` response (non-streaming), not the streaming handler.\n\nBut wait — the response format is different from what I expected. It has `\"content\"` at the top level, not inside `\"tool_calls\"`. And there's no `reasoning_content`. This is the NON-STREAMING `handle_api_chat` response.\n\nBut earlier I changed the connection handler to route api_chat to `handle_api_chat_streaming`. Let me check: the response format `{\"content\":\"...\",\"stop_reason\":\"...\",\"type\":\"api_response\"}` doesn't match `HpToAgent::ApiResponse`. Let me see:\n\n```rust\nHpToAgent::ApiResponse {\n    content: String,\n    tool_calls: Option<serde_json::Value>,\n    stop_reason: Option<String>,\n    reasoning_content: Option<String>,\n    thinking_signature: Option<String>,\n    usage: Option<dsx_types::UsageInfo>,\n}\n```\n\nThis expects `tool_calls` as an optional field. The actual response has `content` and `stop_reason`. The `HpToAgent` enum should deserialize this correctly since all extra fields are `Option`.\n\nBut wait — the response I got is from the OLD `handle_api_chat`, not the streaming one! Let me check: I sent the request and got a SINGLE response line. The streaming handler would send multiple lines (content_delta + api_response). But I got only one line.\n\nThis means the HP's `handle_connection` is NOT routing to the streaming handler. It's routing to the OLD `dispatch_frame` → `handle_api_chat`.\n\nWait, let me check my edit to handle_connection:\n```rust\nif trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\") {\n    if let Ok(mut w) = stream.try_clone() {\n        handle_api_chat_streaming(trimmed, &mut w);\n    }\n} else {\n    let response = dispatch_frame(trimmed, &service);\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThis SHOULD route api_chat to the streaming handler. But the response I got is a single `api_response` line, not multiple content_delta + api_response lines.\n\nHmm, maybe the streaming handler IS being called but it's not sending content_delta because the DeepSeek API response doesn't have streaming content for a simple \"say hi\" query. Let me check: what does the streaming handler do for the StreamEvent::Done event?\n\n```rust\nStreamEvent::Done { raw_message, stop_reason: sr, usage } => {\n    if let Some(text) = raw_message.content {\n        full_content = text;\n    }\n    // Build api_response\n    let mut resp = serde_json::json!({\n        \"type\": \"api_response\",\n        \"content\": full_content,\n    });\n    ...\n    let _ = writeln!(writer, \"{s}\");\n    let _ = writer.flush();\n}\n```\n\nThis sends a SINGLE api_response frame. It doesn't send content_delta before it. The content_delta is only sent when `StreamEvent::ContentDelta` events arrive. If the API response is immediate (no streaming), only the final api_response is sent.\n\nBut that's fine — the agent's streaming loop handles both content_delta and api_response. For an immediate response, only the api_response arrives. The agent should process it normally.\n\nSo the HP IS working. The issue is somewhere in the agent.\n\nActually, I think I just found a clue. The HP response was: `{\"content\":\"Hi! ...\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"type\":\"api_response\"}`. This is the output from `handle_api_chat` (non-streaming), not `handle_api_chat_streaming`. The `handle_api_chat` returns a JSON string that's written by dispatch_frame.\n\nBut wait — I changed the connection handler to detect `api_chat` and route to the streaming handler. So the streaming handler SHOULD be called. Unless my Python test accidentally matched a different path.\n\nActually, looking at my test: I sent `{\"type\":\"api_chat\",\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"messages\":[...]}`. The connection handler checks `trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\")`. My test string contains `\"api_chat\"`, so it should route to the streaming handler.\n\nBut the response format looks like the non-streaming handler's output. Let me check: the streaming handler's output format is:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"api_response\",\"content\":\"Hi!...\",\"reasoning_content\":null,\"tool_calls\":null,\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\"}\n```\n\nThe non-streaming handler's output is:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"api_response\",\"content\":\"Hi!...\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\"}\n```\n\nThe difference: streaming version has `reasoning_content`, `thinking_signature`, `tool_calls`, `usage` fields. The response I got has only `content` and `stop_reason`. This matches the NON-streaming handler.\n\nCould it be that the HP binary is OLD? I built the HP binary as part of `target/release/dsx`. Let me check if the HP daemon that's running is the latest build.\n\nThe HP was started by `ensure_hp_running()` which does `Command::new(&exe).arg(\"hp\")`. But the HP might have been started from a PREVIOUS build. If the old HP is still running, it doesn't have the streaming handler.\n\nThat's it! The HP daemon is running the OLD code. The old `handle_connection` doesn't have the streaming routing — it dispatches all frames through `dispatch_frame` → `handle_api_chat`.\n\nBut wait, when I rebuilt and ran the TUI, `ensure_hp_running()` checks if the port file exists and if HP is alive. If the old HP is alive, it doesn't restart. So the old HP is serving requests!\n\nBut the user restarted dsx after rebuilding. The `ensure_hp_running()` function checks if HP is alive and reuses it. The old HP (started before the latest build) is still running.\n\nThis isn't the source of the user's problem though. The user's problem is that the agent doesn't get past handle_user_input.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The HP response I got was `{\"content\":\"Hi! ...\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"type\":\"api_response\"}`. In the agent's HpToAgent enum, this should match `ApiResponse { content: \"Hi!...\", tool_calls: None, stop_reason: Some(\"end_turn\"), reasoning_content: None, thinking_signature: None, usage: None }`. This should work.\n\nBut the key question remains: why does the agent send Done without first writing an ApiResponse? Let me re-examine.\n\nOh! I think I just found it. Let me check the drain code again:\n\n```rust\nif let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n    let _: Option<HpToAgent> = dsx_proto::read_frame(r).ok().flatten();\n}\n```\n\nAfter the drain, `r` is the BufReader. The drain reads one line from it. But what if the TCP connection has data buffered? BufReader might have read MORE than one line into its internal buffer.\n\n`BufReader::new(stream)` with default capacity (8KB). `read_frame` calls `reader.read_line(&mut line)`. `read_line` reads until `\\n`. If the HP sent multiple lines, `BufReader` might have buffered them.\n\nBut the registration only produces one response line. So the drain should read exactly one line.\n\nOK, let me try a completely different approach. Let me kill the old HP and test with a fresh one.","signature":"a2d8652a-d5dd-4885-92ca-1de20e347a7e"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":62,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":258816,"output_tokens":1981,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"16355625-ae16-47c1-8797-aae0ed97f4e6","timestamp":"2026-05-15T06:58:00.686Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081