nnot connect HP: {e}\");\n            return None;\n        }\n    };\n\n    let pid = std::process::id();\n    let reg = AgentToHp::Register {\n        kind: \"Agent\".into(),\n        name: \"dsx-agent\".into(),\n        pid,\n    };\n    let mut writer = stream.try_clone().unwrap();\n    let _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut writer, &reg);\n\n    let reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap());\n\n    eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: connected to HP on port {port}\");\n    Some((reader, writer))\n}","numLines":30,"startLine":35,"totalLines":643}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a9d326ce-ee92-4358-be4c-b01f67438c38","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
{"parentUuid":"479c7c08-231d-42aa-9ba4-5162a58bc38b","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"da7ff30b-f89b-4675-a106-cd3af00a5cd9","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I found the bug! Look at lines 57-60:\n\n```rust\nlet mut writer = stream.try_clone().unwrap();\nlet _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut writer, &reg);\nlet reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap());\n```\n\n1. `writer = stream.try_clone()` — clones the TCP stream for writing\n2. Write register to writer\n3. `reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone())` — clones AGAIN for reading\n\nBut `stream` (the original) is NEVER used and gets dropped! When `stream` (the original TcpStream) is dropped, it closes its half of the connection. Since the clones share the same kernel file descriptor... actually, `try_clone()` creates a new file descriptor via `dup()`. So the original `stream` being dropped should NOT affect the clones.\n\nWait, actually, `stream` is `TcpStream` which uses `Arc` internally. Dropping `stream` just decrements the reference count. The clones keep the connection alive.\n\nBut there's a problem: the BufReader is created from `stream.try_clone()`, not from the original `stream`. And `writer` is also from `try_clone()`. So we have TWO clones, and the original is dropped. The two clones should keep the connection alive.\n\nWait, but `BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap())` — the `try_clone()` returns a new TcpStream. This goes into BufReader. The BufReader owns it. When the BufReader is dropped, the connection half is dropped.\n\nBut actually, there's a subtle issue: when `stream` (the original) is dropped, what happens to the TCP connection? On Linux, `try_clone()` calls `dup()`, creating a new file descriptor. The kernel's TCP stack keeps the connection alive until ALL file descriptors are closed.\n\nSo the connection should stay alive after `stream` is dropped, because `writer` and `reader` still hold file descriptors.\n\nBUT — actually, is `try_clone()` on TcpStream doing what I think? Let me check: `TcpStream::try_clone()` in the standard library calls `dup()` on the underlying fd. So each clone is an independent fd. All three (stream, writer, reader) are independent. Dropping one doesn't affect the others.\n\nSo the connection should be alive. But the HP log says \"connection closed\" immediately after registration.\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the HP log:\n```\ndsx-hp: listening on 127.0.0.1:35681 (timeout=30s)\n35681\ndsx-hp: connection closed: 127.0.0.1:51634\n```\n\nAnd the agent log:\n```\ndsx-agent: connected to HP on port 35681\n```\n\nThe \"connection closed\" happens AFTER the agent connects. But the agent only sends register and then... doesn't send anything else immediately. The HP's `handle_connection` waits for the next frame with `reader.read_line(&mut buf)`. If the agent's writer is not used again, the HP blocks on read_line.\n\nBut the HP log says \"connection closed\". That means `read_line` returned `Ok(0)` (EOF). Why would the agent close the write half?\n\nActually, I think I found it. Let me look at lines 511-514:\n```rust\nlet hp = connect_hp();\nlet (mut hp_reader, mut hp_writer) = match hp {\n    Some((r, w)) => (Some(r), Some(w)),\n    None => (None, None),\n};\n```\n\n`connect_hp` returns `Option<(BufReader<TcpStream>, TcpStream)>`. The `hp_writer` is `Some(w)` where `w` is a `TcpStream`.\n\nThen at line 517:\n```rust\nif let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n    let _: Option<HpToAgent> = dsx_proto::read_frame(r).ok().flatten();\n}\n```\n\nThis drains `hp_reader`. But `hp_writer` is still Some.\n\nThen later at line 581:\n```rust\nif let (Some(ref mut hp_r), Some(ref mut hp_w)) = (&mut hp_reader, &mut hp_writer) {\n    handle_user_input(&mut agent, &text, hp_r, hp_w, ...);\n}\n```\n\nThis passes `hp_r` (BufReader) and `hp_w` (TcpStream) to handle_user_input.  \n\nInside handle_user_input, `hp_writer: &mut impl Write` receives the TcpStream. `dsx_proto::write_frame(hp_writer, &chat)` writes to the TcpStream.\n\nBut wait — the HP connection closed BEFORE handle_user_input was called. The HP log says \"connection closed\" right after registration. So somehow the agent's write half was dropped or closed.\n\nActually, maybe the issue is simpler. Let me check if the HP's `handle_connection` thread panicked. The HP log shows:\n```\ndsx-hp: connection closed: 127.0.0.1:51634\n```\n\nThis is printed when the connection handler exits:\n```rust\nfn handle_connection(stream: TcpStream, service: Arc<Mutex<HealthService>>) {\n    let peer = stream.peer_addr().ok();\n    let mut reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap());\n    let mut buf = String::new();\n    loop {\n        buf.clear();\n        match reader.read_line(&mut buf) {\n            Ok(0) => break,\n            Ok(_) => { ... response writing ... }\n            Err(_) => break,\n        }\n    }\n    if let Some(addr) = peer {\n        eprintln!(\"dsx-hp: connection closed: {addr}\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe \"connection closed\" message means the HP's read loop exited. It exited because `reader.read_line()` returned `Ok(0)` (EOF). EOF means the agent closed its write half.\n\nSo the agent IS closing the write half. But why? Let me check if the `TcpStream` in `hp_writer` is being dropped.\n\nAfter `connect_hp`:\n- `stream` (original) is dropped → one fd closed (OK, clones still alive)\n- `writer` (clone) is returned as hp_writer\n- `reader` (clone) is returned as hp_reader\n\nIn the main loop, `hp_writer` is used in handle_user_input. But handle_user_input's `hp_writer` parameter is `&mut impl Write`. The TcpStream is borrowed mutably but not consumed. After handle_user_input returns, the TcpStream is still alive.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me check something else. `connect_hp` returns `Option<(BufReader<TcpStream>, TcpStream)>`. But what happens when this is destructured?\n\n```rust\nlet (mut hp_reader, mut hp_writer) = match hp {\n    Some((r, w)) => (Some(r), Some(w)),\n    None => (None, None),\n};\n```\n\nThis should preserve both r and w. Then in the main loop, they're used. At the end of `run()`:\n```rust\nif let Some(ref mut w) = hp_writer {\n    let frame = ...;\n    dsx_proto::write_frame(w, &frame);\n}\n```\n\nThis unregisters from HP. After this, hp_writer is dropped (when run() returns).\n\nBut the HP connection closed BEFORE the unregister. So something else is closing the connection.\n\nActually wait, let me look at `connect_hp` one more time:\n\n```rust\nlet mut writer = stream.try_clone().unwrap();\nlet _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut writer, &reg);\nlet reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap());\n```\n\nLines:\n1. `writer = stream.try_clone()` — clone #1\n2. Write register to writer\n3. `reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone())` — clone #2\n4. `stream` (original) is dropped at end of function\n\nSo we have two clones and the original is dropped. The two clones keep their respective fds open.\n\nBut wait — do we need to keep the original alive? The original `stream` holds a reference to the TCP socket. When it's dropped... in Rust's TcpStream, `try_clone` uses `dup()` on POSIX. This creates a completely independent file descriptor. The original can be dropped without affecting the clones.\n\nBut `TcpStream` is backed by an `Arc<sys::TcpStream>`. The `try_clone` increments the Arc refcount. When the original `stream` is dropped, the Arc refcount decreases. As long as there are other references, the connection stays alive.\n\nSo the clones should keep the connection alive. Unless `try_clone` is NOT using Arc on this platform? Let me check the Rust standard library source... no, I can't. But the standard behavior is that `try_clone` creates an independent fd.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be with `BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap())`. Let me trace:\n1. `stream.try_clone()` creates a new TcpStream (clone #2)\n2. `BufReader::new(clone2)` takes ownership of clone2\n3. This BufReader is returned as `hp_reader`\n\nThis should work. But there's a subtle issue: when `BufReader` wraps a `TcpStream`, and the BufReader is used with `read_line`, it reads from the TcpStream. The TcpStream's read blocks until data is available. This should be fine.\n\nOK I've been going in circles. Let me just try a different approach: instead of trying to find the theoretical bug, let me add stderr debugging to the agent and rebuild.\n\nActually, let me try one more thing. Let me simplify `connect_hp` to avoid the try_clone and just use the original stream.","signature":"da7ff30b-f89b-4675-a106-cd3af00a5cd9"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":301,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":262784,"output_tokens":2795,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"a5ed81fe-974e-4925-b25f-e71c87c3ab50","timestamp":"2026-05-15T07:00:52.481Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
