{"parentUuid":"0e6f802d-50f8-417c-a6e5-9b0bc68f7695","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"13bc1d87-a76a-41a8-b5e9-5ab538f3fb68","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"STILL the same issue! HP connection closes immediately after registration. The agent connects, registers, and then the HP says \"connection closed\". The agent doesn't get a chance to send api_chat.\n\nLet me look at this more carefully. The HP log says:\n```\ndsx-hp: connection closed: 127.0.0.1:35240\n```\n\nAnd the agent log says:\n```\ndsx-agent: connected to HP on port 45589\n```\n\nThe port numbers match. The connection is from agent (port 35240) to HP (port 45589). After registration, the connection closes.\n\nBut WHY? The agent code should keep the TcpStream alive (it's returned as hp_writer). Unless... the TcpStream is being dropped somewhere in the main function.\n\nLet me check: after `connect_hp()`, the stream is destructured:\n```rust\nlet hp = connect_hp();\nlet (mut hp_reader, mut hp_writer) = match hp {\n    Some((r, w)) => (Some(r), Some(w)),\n    None => (None, None),\n};\n```\n\n`r` is `BufReader<TcpStream>` and `w` is `TcpStream`. These are wrapped in `Some` and stored in `hp_reader` and `hp_writer`.\n\nThen:\n```rust\n// Drain register response\nif let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n    let _: Option<HpToAgent> = dsx_proto::read_frame(r).ok().flatten();\n}\n```\n\nThis reads ONE line from hp_reader. `read_frame` calls `reader.read_line(&mut line)`. But `BufReader` might buffer MORE data than one line. If the TCP stack had the \"ok\" message and the \"connection closed\" comes later... but actually, HP only wrote one line (\"ok\") at this point. So BufReader should have buffered only that line.\n\nAfter the drain, hp_writer should still be alive. But the HP reported \"connection closed\" BEFORE the agent received user_input. This means the connection was closed before handle_user_input was even called.\n\nWait, let me re-read the HP log order:\n```\ndsx-hp: connection closed: 127.0.0.1:35240\n```\n\nThis appeared in my test output BEFORE the agent's stderr. But the order in the combined output might be misleading since stdout and stderr are separate streams.\n\nActually, in my test, I run `dsx hp &` to start HP in the background, then sleep 2, then run `dsx agent`. The HP log \"connection closed\" appears when the agent connects and then... does something that closes the connection.\n\nLet me add timing information. Actually, let me check: could it be that the BufReader's `read_line` is consuming all pending data from the TCP stream, including the response to a future api_chat? No, that can't be — the api_chat hasn't been sent yet.\n\nWait, I just realized something. Let me check if `BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap())` might cause the issue. The `try_clone()` returns a new TcpStream. But the ORIGINAL `stream` is used as the writer. When `BufReader` is created from the clone, it immediately tries to fill its buffer. If there's no data available, `fill_buf` would block... no, BufReader only reads when `read()` or `read_line()` is called.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach: dump the raw byte-level communication between agent and HP using strace or tcpdump.\n\nActually, let me just add explicit eprintln! statements to the agent to trace the flow.","signature":"13bc1d87-a76a-41a8-b5e9-5ab538f3fb68"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":433,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":266624,"output_tokens":1172,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"fea74cd2-600e-4409-82e6-5f6ff550894e","timestamp":"2026-05-15T07:02:29.214Z","userType":"external","entrypoint"