{"parentUuid":"ab9ca5e1-cd96-4998-9bea-3fe757bc04e0","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"878db261-6ced-48b5-b231-7d312690f94b","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now this is very revealing! The HP IS working and sending streaming responses:\n\n1. A whole bunch of `content_delta` frames with reasoning (thinking chain!)\n2. Content deltas for the actual response\n3. A final `api_response` with content, reasoning_content, thinking_signature, usage\n\nSo the HP's streaming handler works correctly. The Python client (using a single socket for both sending and receiving) successfully received ALL the streaming frames.\n\nNow why doesn't the agent receive these? The agent uses `try_clone()` to create separate read and write halves. Could the clone be the issue?\n\nLet me think about this: when the HP writes to the TCP connection, the data arrives at the kernel's socket buffer. Any file descriptor that refers to this socket can read from the buffer. So the reader clone should see the data.\n\nBut wait — the HP log says \"connection closed: ...\" right after registration. Let me re-examine the order of events:\n\nIn my test output:\n```\ndsx-hp: connection closed: 127.0.0.1:35240\n---STDERR---\ndsx-agent starting\n...\ndsx-agent: connected to HP on port 45589\n```\n\nWait — \"connection closed\" appears BEFORE \"dsx-agent starting\" in the combined output. But that's because stderr and stdout are separate streams and might be interleaved. Let me check the timing by looking at the HP log order.\n\nOK actually, let me also check: the new connect_hp code I just wrote:\n\n```rust\nlet _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut stream, &reg);\nlet _ = stream.flush();\nlet reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap());\nSome((reader, stream))\n```\n\nThis writes the register to `stream`, flushes, then clones for the reader. The reader wraps its own clone. The writer is the original stream. Both should be alive when returned.\n\nBut then in the main function:\n```rust\nlet (mut hp_reader, mut hp_writer) = match hp {\n    Some((r, w)) => (Some(r), Some(w)),\n    None => (None, None),\n};\n```\n\n`hp_reader = Some(BufReader<TcpStream>)` and `hp_writer = Some(TcpStream)`. Both are Some.\n\nThen drain:\n```rust\nif let Some(ref mut r) = hp_reader {\n    let _: Option<HpToAgent> = dsx_proto::read_frame(r).ok().flatten();\n}\n```\n\nThis reads one frame from the reader. It should succeed.\n\nBut then... what happens? The HP says \"connection closed\". So something is closing the TCP connection on the agent side.\n\nHmm wait, let me check: could the main function be exiting early? After the main loop:\n```rust\nloop {\n    match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut tui_reader) { ... }\n}\n// Cleanup\ndrop(tools_writer);\nagent.maybe_save_session();\nif let Some(ref mut w) = hp_writer { ... };\n```\n\nThe main loop processes user_input, calls handle_user_input, writes Done, and loops. On the next iteration, read_frame blocks on stdin. When stdin closes, the loop breaks and cleanup runs. In cleanup, `hp_writer` is used to unregister.\n\nBut here's the thing: after the drain, the next use of hp_writer is in handle_user_input. Between the drain and handle_user_input, there's:\n1. Session init (deferred to first user message)\n2. Tools spawn + init\n\nThese should be fast (milliseconds). The HP should be waiting on read_line for the next frame. The HP shouldn't see EOF unless the agent closes the connection.\n\nOH WAIT. I just realized something. Let me check if `BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap())` might cause the issue. `TcpStream::try_clone()` uses `dup()` to create a new fd. But after `dup()`, both fds share the same file offset in the kernel. Wait, no — sockets don't have file offsets. `dup()` creates a new fd that refers to the same socket. Reading from either fd reads from the same socket buffer. Writing to either fd writes to the same connection.\n\nSo the clones should work correctly. The reader clone can read what the HP sends, and the writer clone can send to HP.\n\nBut the HP says \"connection closed\" — which happens when the TCP connection gets a FIN from the client (the agent). The FIN is sent when the LAST fd referring to the socket is closed. In Rust, `TcpStream` closes the fd in its `Drop` implementation.\n\nOK, let me count the fds:\n1. Original `stream` after `TcpStream::connect()` — fd #1\n2. `reader = stream.try_clone()` — fd #2 (dup of fd #1)\n3. `writer = stream` — actually, writer IS stream. But `stream` is moved into `Some((reader, stream))`.\n\nWait, in my new code:\n```rust\nlet mut stream = TcpStream::connect(...)?;  // Creates TcpStream with fd #1\nlet _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut stream, &reg); // Writes to fd #1\nlet _ = stream.flush();\nlet reader = BufReader::new(stream.try_clone().unwrap()); // Creates new TcpStream from dup() → fd #2\n// stream is still fd #1\nSome((reader, stream)) // Returns both\n```\n\nSo we return `reader` (containing TcpStream with fd #2) and the original `stream` (fd #1). Both are alive. The HP should NOT see a connection close.\n\nBut what if Rust's `TcpStream` has different behavior? Let me check: when `TcpStream::try_clone()` is called, it's documented to create a new TcpStream that shares the underlying socket. Both the original and the clone refer to the same socket. Dropping one doesn't close the socket. The socket is only closed when ALL handles are dropped.\n\nSo with fd #1 and fd #2 both alive, the socket should stay open. The HP should NOT see a close.\n\nBut the test output clearly shows \"connection closed\" right after registration. What's going on?\n\nWait, let me check one more thing. Maybe the issue is that my test output is MISLEADING. Let me check: the `dsx hp &` starts HP in the background. Then `dsx agent` runs. The HP's \"connection closed\" message goes to stderr. In the combined output, it might appear before the agent's stderr due to buffering.\n\nLet me run a cleaner test.","signature":"878db261-6ced-48b5-b231-7d312690f94b"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2751,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":268160,"output_tokens":1722,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"5b067bf9-54ed-4657-8688-bfc9aad8edb4","timestamp":"2026-05-15T07:03:47.623Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
