{"parentUuid":"4f1e6edc-b87c-4aa5-8f8e-f0b6cfaf1cb9","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"9c5040a0-050c-4605-b073-7082338178ed","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the problem more clearly. The debug output shows:\n\n```\ndsx-agent: handle_user_input: text=\"say hi\" seed=\ndsx-agent: found live session 2fb273ec (2 msgs, )\ndsx-agent: session 2fb273ec restored (2 messages)\ndsx-agent: sending api_chat (messages=2, tokens≈362)\n{\"type\":\"done\"}\ndsx-agent: shutdown complete (session 2fb273ec, 1 turns, 0 tokens)\n```\n\nSo:\n1. handle_user_input IS called (text=\"say hi\", seed=\"\" initially)\n2. Session IS initialized (seed was empty, so init_session is called)\n3. Session IS restored (2fb273ec with 2 messages)\n4. api_chat IS SENT to HP (messages=2, tokens≈362)\n5. But then... Done is written, and \"shutdown complete\" with 0 tokens\n\nThe api_chat was SENT. The HP should respond. But the Done is written without any API response.\n\nWhy? Let me check: after `write_frame(hp, &chat)`, the code enters the streaming loop to read from HP. But the HP response might not be arriving yet... no, the HP should respond quickly.\n\nWait — could the issue be that `hp_reader = BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` is reading from a STALE buffer? Since `hp` is the TcpStream, and we just wrote to it, and now we're reading from it... TcpStream is full duplex. Writing goes to the write buffer, reading comes from the read buffer. They're independent. So writing shouldn't affect reading.\n\nUnless the BufReader has a buffering issue. `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` creates a new BufReader. Its internal buffer is empty. The first `read_line` call fills the buffer from the TcpStream.\n\nBut wait — the `hp` parameter is `&mut TcpStream`. And I'm creating `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)`. This borrows `hp` mutably. But on the PREVIOUS iteration of the for loop, `hp` was also borrowed by the old BufReader. When the old BufReader is dropped (end of loop iteration), the borrow is released.\n\nOn the current iteration:\n1. Write to `hp` (mutable borrow)\n2. Create BufReader from `&mut *hp` (mutable borrow)\n3. Read from hp_reader (mutable borrow through hp_reader)\n4. End of iteration → hp_reader dropped, borrow released\n\nSteps 1, 2, 3 are sequential (not concurrent), so there shouldn't be a borrow conflict.\n\nBUT — in step 1, `dsx_proto::write_frame(hp, &chat)` writes to the TcpStream. The TcpStream is a kernel socket. Data is written to the socket's send buffer. `flush()` is called (part of `write_frame`), ensuring data is sent. After flush, the data should be in the kernel's send buffer, on its way to the HP.\n\nThen in step 2, `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` wraps the same TcpStream. Its internal buffer is empty. \n\nIn step 3, `read_frame(&mut hp_reader)` calls `read_line()`. `read_line()` reads from the BufReader, which reads from the underlying TcpStream. The TcpStream's `read()` blocks until data arrives from HP. HP should respond within a few seconds.\n\nSo in step 3, the agent SHOULD block waiting for HP's response. But it doesn't — the output shows Done immediately. This means `read_frame` returned (Either Ok(None), Err, or the response arrived instantly).\n\nLet me check: maybe the response IS arriving instantly (the HP might be using the non-streaming handler which returns immediately). But then the agent would process it and write an ApiResponse to tui_writer.\n\nBut the stdout shows only `{\"type\":\"done\"}` — no ApiResponse. So either:\n- read_frame returned None/Error (connection closed)\n- Or the response was processed but the ApiResponse wasn't written to stdout\n\nWait — maybe the API response IS being processed but the TuiWriter output is being eaten somewhere?\n\nActually, let me check one thing: `tui_writer` is `std::io::stdout()`. The Done IS being written to stdout (we see it). But if ApiResponse is written BEFORE Done, we should see both. We see only Done. So ApiResponse is NOT being written.\n\nThis means handle_user_input returned before processing the HP response. The only way this happens is if the HP response read loop exits before getting an api_response.\n\nIn the streaming loop:\n```rust\nloop {\n    let hp_resp: HpToAgent = match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut hp_reader) {\n        Ok(Some(r)) => r,\n        Ok(None) => {\n            eprintln!(\"dsx-agent: HP connection closed\");\n            agent.health.record_api_error();\n            return; // <-- THIS PATH\n        }\n        Err(e) => {\n            log::error!(\"HP parse error: {e}\");\n            agent.health.record_api_error();\n            return; // <-- OR THIS PATH\n        }\n    };\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nIf `read_frame` returns `Ok(None)` (EOF), the function returns with an eprintln!. But the stderr doesn't show \"HP connection closed\". So it's not EOF.\n\nIf `read_frame` returns `Err(e)`, the function returns with `log::error!`. But `log` goes to `dsc_log` which might be file-based. Let me check if the log error is visible.\n\nActually, the eprintln! line at 157 (`handle_user_input: text=...`) IS visible. So eprintln! works. But \"HP connection closed\" doesn't appear. So `Ok(None)` is NOT being returned.\n\nThis means `read_frame` is either:\n1. Still blocking (waiting for HP)\n2. Returning `Ok(Some(response))` but the response doesn't match any arm\n\nIf option 2, the response might be a format we don't handle, falling into the `_ => {}` arm. But the Python test showed the response format is correct (`content_delta` and `api_response`).\n\nUnless the HP response format has changed between the two tests? Let me check: the HP I tested with Python was the OLD HP (before I killed it and restarted). The new HP might be different.\n\nActually, I've been killing and restarting HP. Let me verify: what HP is currently running?\n\nActually, let me add debug logging inside the streaming loop to see what frames arrive.","signature":"9c5040