{"parentUuid":"57f3ab1d-8c9f-461d-b55a-67f7dc9f232c","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"3e2d4139-9bd3-4d40-8425-68fff687142e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The debug code IS in place. But the test output doesn't show any \"hp ←\" messages. This means either:\n1. `read_frame(&mut hp_reader)` is BLOCKING and never returns\n2. Or the streaming loop isn't entered at all\n\nBut we see `done` after `sending api_chat`. If read_frame is blocking, how would done be written?\n\nOH WAIT. I think I see the issue. The test command pipes stdin to the agent:\n```\n(echo '...'; sleep 15) | timeout 20 ./target/release/dsx agent\n```\n\nThe agent reads the user_input frame, processes it, and then... in handle_user_input, the streaming loop reads from HP. But HP may not respond because the HP process might be dead or not receiving the api_chat.\n\nLet me check: is HP alive?\n```\ncurl or python test to verify HP\n```\n\nActually, HP was started in step 1 (`dsx hp &`). Let me check if HP is still running when the agent connects.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-examine the test output order:\n\n```\ndsx-agent: sending api_chat (messages=2, tokens≈362)\n{\"type\":\"done\"}\n```\n\n\"sending api_chat\" is written by `eprintln!`. `{\"type\":\"done\"}` is written by `dsx_proto::write_frame(&mut tui_writer, ...)`.\n\nIf the streaming loop was entered and `read_frame` blocked, we'd see \"sending api_chat\" but NOT \"done\". But we see both, in sequence. So:\n1. api_chat is sent to HP\n2. The streaming loop processes HP response\n3. handle_user_input returns\n4. Done is written\n\nBut there are no \"hp ←\" messages. This means the streaming loop didn't print any debug for the HP response frames.\n\nUNLESS the HP response was `ApiResponse` but the match arm doesn't print debug. Let me check: the first `hp_resp` match is:\n```rust\nmatch hp_resp {\n    HpToAgent::ContentDelta { ... } => { ... }\n    HpToAgent::ToolProgress { ... } => { ... }\n    HpToAgent::ApiResponse { ... } => { content = c; ... break; }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe debug print is at the `read_frame` level (before the match). So EVERY frame should print \"hp ← ...\".\n\nIf there's NO \"hp ←\" output, then `read_frame` was never called inside the streaming loop. Which means the loop body was never entered. Which means... something is wrong with the loop.\n\nWait, let me check the loop structure more carefully. I added:\n```rust\nlet mut hp_reader = BufReader::new(&mut *hp);\nlet mut content = String::new();\n...\nloop {\n    let hp_resp: HpToAgent = match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut hp_reader) {\n        Ok(Some(r)) => { eprintln!(...); r }\n        ...\n    };\n    match hp_resp {\n        HpToAgent::ContentDelta { ... } => { ... }\n        HpToAgent::ToolProgress { ... } => { ... }\n        HpToAgent::ApiResponse { ... } => { ... break; }\n        HpToAgent::Error { ... } => { ... return; }\n        _ => { ... }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe loop should execute at least once. But the read_frame call should print debug before any match. Since there's no debug output...\n\nHmm, what if `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` is somehow consuming all data from the HP? When `BufReader` is created, it doesn't read anything. The first `read` is in `read_line`.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check: `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` where `hp` is `&mut TcpStream`. `&mut *hp` reborrows `hp` to get `&mut TcpStream`. `BufReader::new()` takes ownership of the `&mut TcpStream`. So `hp_reader` is a `BufReader<&mut TcpStream>`.\n\nWhen `read_frame(&mut hp_reader)` is called, it calls `hp_reader.read_line(&mut line)`. This reads from the BufReader, which reads from the TcpStream. If there's no data available, `read()` blocks.\n\nBut the output shows \"done\" immediately after \"sending api_chat\". No blocking. So `read_line` returned immediately with data. But the response was not printed...\n\nUnless the HP response is being sent to the TcpStream but `BufReader` is not seeing it because of some buffering issue. Or maybe the HP response went to a different fd.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check: the `hp` TcpStream was used to WRITE the api_chat. Then it's used to CREATE the BufReader for reading. But when `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` is called, `hp` is `&mut TcpStream`. The BufReader wraps this reference. It reads from the same underlying TcpStream that was used for writing.\n\nBut here's the question: does the TcpStream have any data in its internal buffer from the write? Or does the write flush clear everything? In `write_frame`, `writer.flush()` is called. This flushes any buffered write data to the kernel. After flush, the TcpStream's read buffer should be empty (writes go to the write buffer, reads come from the read buffer — they're separate kernel buffers).\n\nSo the read should block waiting for HP's response. But the output shows no blocking.\n\nOK I'm going to try a completely different approach. Let me just check if HP is receiving the api_chat request by printing to stderr in the HP handler.","signature":"3e2d4139-9bd3-4d40-8425-68fff687142e"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":206,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":285312,"output_tokens":1342,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"daa28c04-efa7-43e6-b842-467860563fbc","timestamp":"2026-05-15T07:11:48.453Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
