{"parentUuid":"9a6dcb68-cdae-41e3-9e6e-e033728f2704","isSidechain":false,"teamName":"dsx-integration","message":{"id":"2d62f451-74cd-4ecd-adc4-2eb8d0dbf5df","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"NOW I see it!\n\n```\ndsx-agent: HP parse error: expected `,` or `}` at line 1 column 51\n```\n\nThe HP response is arriving, but it can't be parsed as `HpToAgent`. The error is \"expected `,` or `}` at line 1 column 51\". Column 51 in the HP's register response... wait, the register response was drained! This should be a fresh read.\n\nBut wait, the `BufReader` was created from `&mut *hp`. The `hp` TcpStream already had the BufReader wrapping it before (for the drain). The drain consumed the register response. But maybe there's buffered data in the PREVIOUS BufReader that was dropped?\n\nNo, the BufReader was dropped. Its internal buffer is deallocated when dropped. So there's no stale data.\n\nBut actually, `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)` wraps a FRESH mutable reference. This means there's a new BufReader. Its internal buffer is empty. When `read_line` is called, it fills the buffer from the TcpStream.\n\nBut what if the TcpStream has data in its READ buffer from a previous read? No — TcpStream reads from the kernel's socket receive buffer. Once data is read, it's removed from the kernel buffer. So there's no way for the TcpStream to re-read old data.\n\nUnless... `TcpStream` has internal buffering. Let me check: does `std::net::TcpStream` do internal buffering? No, `TcpStream` is a wrapper around the OS socket. It doesn't buffer reads.\n\nSo the \"HP parse error\" is from a NEW response from HP. The HP is sending something that can't be parsed as `HpToAgent`. Let me check what the HP sends back as the register response.\n\nThe HP's register response: `{\"type\":\"ok\",\"message\":\"registered\"}`\n\nIs this in the `HpToAgent` enum? YES: `#[serde(rename = \"ok\")] Ok { message: String }`. This should parse correctly.\n\nAfter the drain, the HP processes the api_chat frame and sends back content_delta + api_response. These are also in HpToAgent. They should parse correctly.\n\nBut the parse error is at column 51. What could be at column 51?\n\nLet me think about this differently. The register drain works correctly (no error). Then api_chat is sent. The HP's streaming handler writes content_delta frames. The FIRST frame after api_chat should be a content_delta. \n\nA content_delta frame looks like: `{\"delta\":\"...\",\"reasoning\":\"...\",\"type\":\"content_delta\"}`. This is ~80-100 chars. Column 51 would be in the middle of the reasoning or delta field.\n\nBut the `HpToAgent` enum has `ContentDelta { delta: String, reasoning: Option<String> }`. serde tag = \"type\" means it looks for the \"type\" field to determine the variant. The frame has `\"type\":\"content_delta\"`. So it should match the ContentDelta variant.\n\nWait, let me check: `HpToAgent` is tagged with `#[serde(tag = \"type\")]`. The ContentDelta variant has `#[serde(rename = \"content_delta\")]`. So the deserializer looks at the \"type\" field, finds \"content_delta\", and maps to the ContentDelta variant. Then it deserializes the remaining fields.\n\nBut what if the ContentDelta frame has extra fields that ContentDelta doesn't expect? Like, what if the HP sends a ContentDelta frame with an extra field like `id` or `stream_type`?\n\nLooking at the HP's streaming handler:\n```rust\nStreamEvent::ContentDelta(delta) => {\n    let frame = serde_json::json!({\n        \"type\": \"content_delta\",\n        \"delta\": delta,\n    });\n```\n\nThis sends `{\"type\":\"content_delta\",\"delta\":\"...\"}`. The HpToAgent::ContentDelta expects `{ delta: String, reasoning: Option<String> }`. The \"reasoning\" field is optional (skip_serializing_if). So a frame without \"reasoning\" should parse correctly.\n\nBut the HP ALSO sends `StreamEvent::ReasoningDelta`:\n```rust\nStreamEvent::ReasoningDelta(delta) => {\n    let frame = serde_json::json!({\n        \"type\": \"content_delta\",\n        \"delta\": \"\",\n        \"reasoning\": delta,\n    });\n```\n\nThis sends `{\"type\":\"content_delta\",\"delta\":\"\",\"reasoning\":\"...\"}`. The HpToAgent::ContentDelta expects both fields. This should parse correctly.\n\nSo both formats should work. The parse error must be from something else.\n\nWait, actually, let me check: what if the drain consumed MORE than one line? The drain calls `read_frame(r)` which reads one line. But `BufReader` might have buffered MORE data. Let me trace:\n\n1. Agent connects, sends register\n2. HP receives register, sends `{\"type\":\"ok\",\"message\":\"registered\"}\\n`\n3. Agent drain: creates BufReader, calls read_line\n4. BufReader fills its internal buffer. If the HP has already responded, the \"ok\" response is in the TCP buffer\n5. read_line reads until \\n. Returns \"ok\" frame.\n6. BufReader is dropped. Internal buffer is deallocated.\n7. Later: api_chat is sent\n8. New BufReader created, read_line called\n9. BufReader fills from TCP. HP's response (content_delta + api_response) is in the TCP buffer\n10. read_line reads one line. Should be a content_delta frame.\n\nThis should work. The BufReader doesn't share buffers between instances.\n\nUnless... step 4: what if the HP has ALREADY sent more data after the \"ok\" response? Like a heartbeat or something? Let me check if HP sends any unsolicited data.\n\nLooking at HP's handle_connection: it reads a frame, dispatches, writes a response, reads next frame. No unsolicited data.\n\nOK, one more theory: maybe the HP's `handle_api_chat_streaming` is using the non-streaming `handle_api_chat` path. Let me check: maybe the HP routing isn't detecting \"api_chat\" correctly.\n\nThe routing check is:\n```rust\nif trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_chat\\\"\") {\n    handle_api_chat_streaming(trimmed, &mut w);\n}\n```\n\nThe agent sends:\n```json\n{\"type\":\"api_chat\",\"model\":\"deepseek-v4-flash\",\"system\":\"...\",\"messages\":[...],\"effort\":null,\"max_tokens\":16000,\"tools\":null}\n```\n\nThis contains `\"api_chat\"` (in the `\"type\":\"api_chat\"` field). So it should match.\n\nBut what if `trimmed` doesn't contain `\"api_chat\"`? Let me check: `trimmed = buf.trim()`. The agent's JSON is one line without extra whitespace. `trim()` just removes leading/trailing whitespace. So it should contain `\"api_chat\"`.\n\nUNLESS... the JSON is truncated or malformed. Let me check what the agent sends:\n\n```rust\nlet chat = AgentToHp::ApiChat {\n    model: agent.config.model.clone(),\n    system,\n    messages: serde_json::json!(messages_json),\n    effort: agent.config.effort.clone(),\n    max_tokens: Some(agent.config.max_tokens),\n    tools: None,\n};\nlet _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(hp, &chat);\n```\n\nThis serializes `AgentToHp::ApiChat` to JSON. The serialized JSON has type \"api_chat\". This should be valid.\n\nOK, let me try something different. Let me check the EXACT bytes that cause the parse error. The error says \"column 51\". Let me look at what frame could have an issue at column 51.\n\nActually, maybe this is a red herring. The parse error might be from something else entirely. Let me just print the EXACT line that fails to parse.","signature":"2d62f451-74cd-4ecd-adc4-2eb8d0dbf5df"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":324,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":287360,"output_tokens":2218,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"f7c020a0-8e84-4393-a308-719d8205246f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T07:13:24.926Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiti