it:\n```rust\nif !reasoning.is_empty() {\n    resp[\"reasoning_content\"] = serde_json::json!(reasoning);\n}\n```\n\nSo reasoning is in the api_response AND was streamed via content_delta.\n\nWhen the TUI processes done, it creates a Message with reasoning from `stream.reasoning`. But the stream was updated during the content_delta phase with reasoning. So `stream.to_message()` should include it.\n\nBut wait — the `api_response` in the TUI only updates `stream.content`, not `stream.reasoning`. So if the reasoning arrived before the api_response, it's preserved in stream.reasoning. If the reasoning arrived AFTER the api_response... that shouldn't happen because api_response is the LAST frame.\n\nSo the flow should work: reasoning arrives via content_delta frames → stream.reasoning accumulates → done finalizes → to_message includes reasoning → Message.reasoning_content is set → render shows thinking block.\n\nLet me check if there's an issue with the completed message rendering. In chat.rs:\n\n```rust\nif let Some(ref r) = msg.reasoning_content {\n    if !r.is_empty() {\n        let w = area.width.saturating_sub(4) as usize;\n        lines.push(Line::from(\"\"));\n        lines.extend(render_thinking_block(r, w, ctx.tt().thinking_block));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis renders the thinking block for completed messages. So reasoning SHOULD be visible both live and in history.\n\nI think the issue might be that it IS working but the user just doesn't see it because they're looking at the wrong thing. Or maybe there's a bug in how the TUI handles the api_response overwriting.\n\nActually, I just realized something. Let me re-check my TUI code:\n\n```rust\n} else if trimmed.contains(\"\\\"api_response\\\"\") {\n    if let Ok(v) = serde_json::from_str::<serde_json::Value>(trimmed) {\n        if let Some(content) = v.get(\"content\").and_then(|c| c.as_str()) {\n            if stream.content.is_empty() {\n                stream.apply_payload(content, None, &[]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `api_response` only sets content, not reasoning. And only if stream.content is empty. So the reasoning that accumulated during streaming is preserved. When done arrives, to_message includes both content and reasoning. This seems correct.\n\nBUT — `AgentToTui::ApiResponse` might not be deserialized by the TUI. The TUI reads raw JSON and does string matching, not structured deserialization. So it doesn't use the `AgentToTui` type. It manually extracts fields.\n\nLooking at the TUI's parsing of api_response:\n```rust\nlet content = v.get(\"content\").and_then(|c| c.as_str());\n```\n\nIt only reads \"content\". It does NOT read \"reasoning_content\" from the api_response. So the reasoning_content from api_response is LOST.\n\nBut the reasoning arrived via content_delta frames BEFORE the api_response. So stream.reasoning already has the data. The api_response doesn't overwrite it (it only overwrites content, and only if empty).\n\nSo the flow IS correct. The reasoning streams live, and is preserved in the completed message.\n\nHmm, let me just verify this by checking if the user actually sees the reasoning or not. They said \"为什么思考链路不是流式的\" — meaning the thinking chain isn't streaming. But the code suggests it should be.\n\nLet me check ONE more thing: the agent's `AgentToTui::ApiResponse` — does it include `reasoning_content`?\n\nActually, let me look at the agent's final response:\n```rust\nlet tui_resp = AgentToTui::ApiResponse {\n    content: content.clone(),\n    tool_calls: None,\n    stop_reason: None,\n    usage,\n};\n```\n\nNO `reasoning_content`! The final api_response doesn't include reasoning. But the TUI doesn't need it because the reasoning was already streamed.\n\nWait, but the agent also covers the case where there's NO streaming (non-streaming response). In that case, only the final api_response arrives, and reasoning would be lost.\n\nBut actually, we're using the streaming handler now (`handle_api_chat_streaming`), so conten