ols.map(|tds| {\n    tds.into_iter()\n        .map(|td| AnthropicTool {\n            name: td.function.name,\n            description: td.function.description,\n            input_schema: td.function.parameters,\n        })\n        .collect()\n});\n```\n\nThis converts ToolDef to AnthropicTool. But `AnthropicTool` uses `input_schema` field (which matches DeepSeek's Anthropic API spec). This should work.\n\nBut now, the tool definitions sent from the agent use `td.function.parameters` as `input_schema`. But `ToolFunction.parameters` is `serde_json::Value` — it contains JSON Schema. The key question: are these parameters valid JSON Schema?\n\nLet me check the actual tool registration to see what the parameters look like. Actually, we don't need to check that right now. The tools DO have parameters (they're registered in dsx-tools), and they're sent to the agent, which forwards them to HP, which converts and sends to the API.\n\nThe issue might be simpler: maybe the `input_schema` is empty or invalid. Or maybe the model just doesn't know how to use these specific tools.\n\nBut wait — the model IS receiving the tools (they're in the request). The documentation says tool use IS supported. But the model is outputting XML.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Maybe the issue is that `deepseek-v4-flash` doesn't support tool calling, and when the API receives tools, it maps to `deepseek-v4-flash` which ignores them. The doc says \"传入不支持模型名时，API 会自动将其映射到 deepseek-v4-flash\". But `deepseek-v4-flash` IS a supported model.\n\nActually, the doc says the Anthropic API does support tool use. But individual models might have different capabilities. `deepseek-v4-flash` might not be trained for tool use.\n\nBut the user showed that the model DOES respond with tool calls — just in XML format. The model understands the concept of tools but outputs them as XML. This suggests the model was trained on some tool-calling format but not the Anthropic one.\n\nOK, I think the practical 