rap_or(\"\");\n        // Support both flat {name, arguments} and nested {function: {name, arguments}}\n        let (name, arguments) = if let Some(func) = tc.get(\"function\") {\n            let n = func.get(\"name\").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or(\"\");\n            let a = func.get(\"arguments\").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or(\"{}\");\n            (n, a)\n        } else {\n            let n = tc.get(\"name\").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or(\"\");\n            let a = tc.get(\"arguments\").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or(\"{}\");\n            (n, a)\n        };\n        if id.is_empty() || name.is_empty() { return None; }\n        Some(ToolCall {\n            id: id.to_string(),\n            call_type: \"function\".to_string(),\n            function: FunctionCall { name: name.to_string(), arguments: arguments.to_string() },\n        })\n    }).collect()\n}\n\n// ── User input handler (fully wired) ──\n","numLines":25,"startLine":266,"totalLines":845}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"89bfbca3-1d1a-463a-ad95-ffb6395b3b42","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
{"parentUuid":"57a7e56a-54a6-4c28-bb05-a01544245d91","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"0829e367-2b4c-42c3-8154-2154fd6a00e4","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The `parse_tool_calls` function handles:\n1. Flat format: `{id, name, arguments}` — This is what HP sends (line 450-452 of HP runner)\n2. Nested format: `{id, function: {name, arguments}}` — OpenAI API format\n\nLooking at line 269: `tc.get(\"id\")` — works for both formats since both have `id`.\nLine 271: `tc.get(\"function\")` — works for OpenAI format.\n\nBut the HP's api_response format has `{\"id\": \"...\", \"name\": \"...\", \"arguments\": \"...\"}` — this is the FLAT format. The `parse_tool_calls` function tries `tc.get(\"function\").get(\"name\")` first, and if that fails, falls back to `tc.get(\"name\")`. So flat format should work.\n\nWait, actually there might be a subtle issue. In the HP's api_response handler:\n```rust\nlet mut tool_calls: Vec<serde_json::Value> = Vec::new();\nif let Some(tcs) = raw_message.tool_calls {\n    for tc in tcs {\n        tool_calls.push(serde_json::json!({\n            \"id\": tc.id,\n            \"name\": tc.function.name,\n            \"arguments\": tc.function.arguments,\n        }));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis creates: `{\"id\": \"call_xxx\", \"name\": \"read_file\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"path\\\": \\\"/tmp/test.txt\\\"}\"}`\n\nThese are in FLAT format. The agent's `parse_tool_calls` tries the nested first, then flat. For flat format: `tc.get(\"name\")` works. `arguments` field should also work since it's a single string value.\n\nBut wait — the OpenAI gateway stores tool calls with `function.arguments` as a String (already serialized JSON). In the Done handler, `atc.arguments` is `String`. Then in the HP, `tc.function.arguments` is also `String`. So the HP's serialized JSON has `\"arguments\": \"{\\\"path\\\":...}\"` — a JSON string containing another JSON string.\n\nThe `parse_tool_calls` function does `tc.get(\"arguments\").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or(\"{}\")`. For `\"arguments\": \"{\\\"path\\\":...}\"`, `tc.get(\"arguments\")` returns a `serde_json::Value::String(\"{\\\"path\\\":...}\")`. `as_str()` on a `Value::String` works. So `arguments` becomes `\"{\\\"path\\\":...}\"`. This is correct.\n\nOK so the parsing looks correct. The issue must be elsewhere.\n\nWait, let me re-read the problem. The user said \"tui下调用工具直接报错400\" — when using the TUI to call tools, they get a 400 error. But in my test with the agent directly (without TUI), the agent only showed tool_progress followed by done — no actual tool execution.\n\nSo the 400 might be a secondary issue. The primary issue is: the tool call is NOT being executed at all. The agent receives the streamed tool_progress events but doesn't actually process the tool calls.\n\nHmm, let me look at this from a different angle. The test output shows tool_progress events (13 of them) and then done. No CallReq/Call events. This means the agent's streaming loop is not receiving the api_response that contains the assembled tool_calls.\n\nThe possible issue: the tool_progress events are being sent by the HP during streaming, but the final api_response is either:\n1. Not being sent by the HP\n2. Being sent but the agent's BufReader is buffering it wrong\n3. Being sent but the agent's streaming loop is not reading it\n\nWait, I just realized: the first round of the agent's for loop (api_chat → HP response streaming loop) processes the model's tool calls. But looking at the agent output, there are 13 tool_progress events. Each tool_progress is a `HpToAgent::ToolProgress` event in the streaming loop. The agent forwards these to TUI and continues the loop.\n\nAfter the 13th tool_progress, the next HP frame should be the api_response (with assembled tool_calls in `tcs_raw`). But the agent's output shows tool_progress then done — no api_response.\n\nCould it be that the HP is sending the api_response, but the agent's `read_frame` on the HP connection is not seeing it? Or the api_response JSON is malformed?\n\nLet me check by looking at the HP's Done → api_response conversion one more time. The HP logs also showed that the function returns after writing the api_response. So the HP IS writing the api_response.\n\nBut wait — after the HP writes the api_response and returns, the `handle_connection` function reads the NEXT frame from the TCP connection. The agent, meanwhile, is still in the streaming loop and reads the api_response. But the agent's BufReader wraps a `BufReader::new(&mut *hp)`. After the streaming loop breaks (on api_response), the agent processes the tool calls and makes a SECOND api_chat request.\n\nBut the BufReader that was used for reading the first streaming loop is NOW DROPPED (end of for loop iteration). A new BufReader is created for the second api_chat's streaming loop. This should be fine.\n\nUnless... the issue is that the BufReader's `read_frame` returned an error or a different variant for the api_response line. Let me check by adding more debugging.\n\nActually, let me just fix the most likely issue: I think the problem is that the api_response contains tool_calls in the JSON, and the agent correctly parses them (via `parse_tool_calls`), but the `content` string is empty (since the model only returned tool_calls, no text). Then the agent's `if !has_tools` check fails (has_tools is true), and the agent enters the tool execution loop.\n\nThe tool execution loop dispatches to the tools process. But looking at the output, there are no `CallReq` or `Call` events. This means the tool execution loop never ran.\n\nWait, I just realized: the agent test sends ONE user_input frame and then stdin closes (timeout). The agent receives the user_input, processes it through handle_user_input, which runs the for loop (api_chat → HP response → process). If the HP response contains tool_calls, the agent executes them and continues the for loop for another round of api_chat.\n\nBut the test shows ONLY 1 TURN and 3546 tokens. If the tool execution happened, there should have been at least 2 turns (user → assistant with tool call → tool result → assistant summary).\n\nSo the tool call was never dispatched. Let me check if the tool_calls are actually being parsed. Let me add a debug line.\n\nActually wait — 3546 tokens for 1 turn. Let me check: 3546 tokens for the initial request with all 27 tool definitions. That's a lot but expected.\n\nIf the tool call was never dispatched, then `parse_tool_calls` returned an empty vec. Let me check why:\n- The HP sends tool_calls as flat JSON: `[{\"id\":\"call_xxx\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"path\\\":\\\"...\\\"}\"}]`\n- `parse_tool_calls` should parse this correctly\n\nBut wait — the `arguments` field from the HP is `String`. In the HP's Done handler:\n```rust\ntool_calls.push(serde_json::json!({\n    \"id\": tc.id,\n    \"name\": tc.function.name,\n    \"arguments\": tc.function.arguments,\n}));\n```\n\n`tc.function.arguments` is a `String` (e.g., `{\"path\":\"/tmp/test.txt\"}`). When serialized to JSON, this becomes a JSON string: `\"{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/tmp/test.txt\\\"}\"`. So the JSON value is:\n```json\n{\"id\":\"call_xxx\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/tmp/test.txt\\\"}\"}\n```\n\nIn `parse_tool_calls`, line 273: `let a = func.get(\"arguments\").and_then(|v| v.as_str())`. `tc.get(\"arguments\")` returns a `Value::String(...)`. `as_str()` on a `Value::String` works. So `arguments` = `{\"path\":\"/tmp/test.txt\"}`. This is correct.\n\nThen line 280: `if id.is_empty() || name.is_empty() { return None; }`. `id` = `\"call_xxx\"`, `name` = `\"read_file\"`. Both non-empty. So the tool call should be created.\n\nWith `has_tools = true`, the agent enters the tool execution loop:\n```rust\nfor tc in &parsed {\n    let name = &tc.function.name;\n    let id = &tc.id;\n    let args = &tc.function.arguments;\n    // gates...\n    // dispatch to tools process...\n}\n```\n\nThis should execute each tool. But the output shows no tool execution. \n\nUnless... the tools process crashed or the tool request was empty. Let me check: the agent sends `AgentToTools::CallReq { id, name, action, args, timeout }`. If `name` is empty or `args` is invalid, the tools process might hang or crash.\n\nWait, I just realized something. Looking at the agent's tool dispatch code:\n\n```rust\nlet call = AgentToTools::CallReq {\n    id: id.clone(),\n    name: name.clone(),\n    action: name.clone(),  // ← action = name!\n    args: args_val,\n    timeout_secs: Some(30),\n};\n```\n\n`action: name.clone()` — the action is set to the tool name. That's correct for most tools.\n\nBut the `args_val` is:\n```rust\nlet args_val: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(args).unwrap_or_default();\n```\n\n`args` is `tc.function.arguments` which is a JSON string (not a JSON object). `serde_json::from_str(\"{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/tmp/test.txt\\\"}\")` should parse as a JSON object. Let me check: the `arguments` field from `ToolCall.function.arguments` is a `String` containing JSON. So `serde_json::from_str(&args)` where args = `{\"path\":\"/tmp/test.txt\"}` should work.\n\nHmm, but the flat format from HP has `arguments` as a JSON string. When `parse_tool_calls` extracts it, `arguments` is `String`. So `args` = `{\"path\":\"/tmp/test.txt\"}`. Then `serde_json::from_str(args)` parses it as a JSON object. `unwrap_or_default()` gives `serde_json::Value::Object({\"path\":\"/tmp/test.txt\"})`. This should work.\n\nI'm running out of ideas. Let me just add a debug eprintln to see what's happening.\n\nActually, let me check one more thing: does the for loop actually execute? Maybe the `has_tools` check is wrong because `parsed` is empty.\n\nWait — looking at line 497:\n```rust\nif !has_tools {\n    // Final response — no tools\n    ...\n    return;\n}\n```\n\nIf `has_tools` is false, the agent enters the no-tools path, writes api_response to TUI, and returns. But we DON'T see api_response in the output. So `has_tools` must be TRUE. But we also don't see tool execution.\n\nUnless... the agent's for loop reaches `has_tools = true`, enters the tool execution path, but then immediately returns because of a failed gate (like `phase_check_tool` or `pre_tool_health_check`).\n\nThe gate functions are stubs in the TUI but real in the agent. Let me check:\n\n```rust\nif gates::phase_check_tool(agent, name, id) { continue; }\nif gates::explore_gate(agent, name, id, args) { continue; }\nif let Some(err_msg) = gates::pre_tool_health_check(agent, name) {\n    ...\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nIf any gate returns true (continue), the tool is skipped. What if `phase_check_tool` returns true for tools when no task phase is set?\n\nOK I think the issue might be the gates. Let me check the gates implementation.","signature":"0829e367-2b4c-42c3-8154-2154fd6a00e4"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":435,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":431360,"output_tokens":2800,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"0ad41728-a88e-4138-b2e1-b06dece01b93","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:21:06.719Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
