{"parentUuid":"3037cc25-334c-4154-8c15-085bab21be83","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"8a0d6dd7-63ff-4511-a4c9-caa00d0b9015","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The debug output didn't appear! The `eprintln!` in the streaming loop should have printed something. But the output shows only `tool_progress` frames. This means the streaming loop never hit the ApiResponse arm — it only saw ContentDelta and ToolProgress events.\n\nBut wait — the debug `eprintln!` should appear EVEN if the api_response is never reached. The `eprintln!` inside the ApiResponse arm would only print when the arm matches. If it never matches... that's the bug.\n\nBut the streaming loop must exit somehow (handle_user_input returned, \"done\" was written). If the streaming loop only processes content_delta and tool_progress frames, how does it exit?\n\nLooking at the streaming loop:\n```rust\nloop {\n    let hp_resp: HpToAgent = match dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut hp_reader) {\n        Ok(Some(r)) => r,\n        Ok(None) => { return; }\n        Err(e) => { return; }\n    };\n    match hp_resp {\n        ContentDelta { .. } => { ... }\n        ToolProgress { .. } => { ... }\n        ApiResponse { .. } => { ... break; }\n        Error { .. } => { ... return; }\n        _ => { /* ignore */ }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIf `read_frame` returns `Ok(None)` (EOF), the function returns. This could happen if the HP connection is closed. But we don't see \"HP connection closed\" in stderr (it goes to DscLog).\n\nLet me check: maybe the HP closes the connection after sending the final api_response. In the HP's streaming handler, after writing the api_response and returning, the HP waits in `handle_connection` for the next frame. But the agent is still trying to read from the BufReader (inside the streaming loop). The BufReader wraps the TcpStream. When the agent tries to read more, it gets EOF (connection still open, but no more data).\n\nWait, no — TCP connections don't buffer. The BufReader reads from the TcpStream. After the api_response line is sent, the HP waits for the next request. The agent's BufReader reads line by line. The api_response line is in the BufReader's buffer. When `read_line` is called, it returns the api_response line. The streaming loop processes it.\n\nUnless the BufReader has NOT buffered the api_response yet. Let me check: after the HP writes the api_response, it flushes. But there might be a timing issue where the agent's read happens before the data arrives in the kernel buffer.\n\nActually, this is unlikely. The TCP data should be available almost immediately after flush.\n\nWait, I just realized: the agent's BufReader is created fresh in EACH iteration of the for loop:\n```rust\nfor _round in 0..10 {\n    // Write api_chat\n    let _ = dsx_proto::write_frame(hp, &chat);\n    // Create NEW BufReader\n    let mut hp_reader = BufReader::new(&mut *hp);\n    // Streaming loop reads from hp_reader\n    loop {\n        let hp_resp: HpToAgent = dsx_proto::read_frame(&mut hp_reader);\n        ...\n    }\n    // hp_reader dropped here\n}\n```\n\nEach iteration creates a NEW BufReader. The BufReader wraps `&mut *hp` (the TcpStream). When created, the BufReader's internal buffer is empty. On the first `read_line`, the BufReader reads from the underlying TcpStream.\n\nBut here's the thing: after the previous iteration's streaming loop (which broke on api_response), the BufReader was dropped. The `hp` variable is still alive (it's `&mut TcpStream`). So the next iteration's BufReader starts fresh.\n\nIn the FIRST iteration, after writing api_chat, the BufReader reads the HP frames. The HP sends content_delta (for display), reasoning_delta, tool_progress (for tool call accumulation), and api_response (with tool_calls). The agent reads all of these.\n\nBut the debug shows the agent processes content_delta and tool_progress, but NOT api_response. This means:\n1. The api_response is NOT being sent by the HP (but we proved it IS)\n2. The api_response is sent but NOT READ by the BufReader because... the connection is closed before it arrives?\n\nWait — could there be a streaming issue where the HP sends the last frame (api_response) and then returns, closing the HTTP connection? No, the HP returns from `handle_api_chat_streaming` but the TCP connection stays open. The HP waits for the next frame from the agent.\n\nBut the agent is in the streaming loop, reading from the BufReader. The BufReader's `read_line` blocks on the TcpStream read. The api_response frame is in the TCP buffer. The BufReader reads it. But what if the BufReader reads more than one line, and the second line is incomplete?\n\nBufReader reads up to its buffer size (default 8KB). If the HP sends multiple frames, they might all be read at once into the BufReader's buffer. But `read_line` only returns ONE line. The rest stays in the buffer.\n\nThis should work correctly. Each `read_frame` call reads one line from the BufReader.\n\nOK I think I need a different approach. Let me add debug on the `_ => {}` arm and the error handlers to see what's happening.","signature":"8a0d6dd7-63ff-4511-a4c9-caa00d0b9015"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":330,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":442752,"output_tokens":1334,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"aec99cee-4149-4ce9-a10f-310dcdc73ca2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:25:11.086Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
