{"parentUuid":"20e75646-39cb-4e5d-b115-d3abb8fab9a9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"e92e1d22-d1b1-4c96-a9f5-743b2f1189ea","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"NOW I see the issue!\n\n```\ndsx-agent: api_response tool_calls=true has_tools=true   ← api_response correctly parsed!\ndsx-agent: parsed 1 tool calls from api_response          ← tool call parsed!\ndsx-agent: has_tools=true executing 1 tools               ← entering tool execution!\ndsx-agent: HP parse error: expected `,` or `}` at line 1 column 58  ← SECOND API CALL FAILS!\n```\n\nThe model's tool call was correctly received. The agent starts executing the tool. But then the agent sends the TOOL RESULT back to HP in a SECOND api_chat request, and THIS request gets a 400 error!\n\nThe issue is: the second api_chat includes the tool result message in the conversation. The OpenAI endpoint doesn't accept the tool result format correctly.\n\nThe tool result is stored as `Message` with `role: \"tool\"`. When serialized to JSON and sent back to HP, the JSON has:\n```json\n{\"role\":\"tool\",\"content\":\"hello world\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_xxx\"}\n```\n\nThe HP receives this, and the openai gateway converts it to `OpenAIMessage`. Let me check the openai.rs gateway conversion for `role == \"tool\"`:\n\n```rust\n} else if role == \"tool\" {\n    OpenAIMessage {\n        role: \"tool\".into(),\n        content: text,\n        tool_calls: None,\n        tool_call_id,\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis looks correct. The OpenAI API expects tool results with `role: \"tool\"`.\n\nBut the 400 error is at column 58 of the SECOND api_chat request. This might be a schema issue. Let me think about what the full second request looks like:\n\nThe messages would be:\n1. User: \"read /tmp/test.txt\"\n2. Assistant: tool_calls (read_file)\n3. Tool: content (result of read_file)\n\nThis is the correct format for OpenAI. But the 400 might be caused by:\n- The assistant message having empty content (since the model only generated tool_calls)\n- The API rejecting the schema\n\nActually, let me check: does the OpenAI API accept an assistant message with `tool_calls` but NO `content` field?\n\nLooking at the OpenAIMessage converter:\n```rust\nlet oai_msg = if role == \"assistant\" && tool_calls.is_some() {\n    // build with tool_calls\n    OpenAIMessage {\n        role: \"assistant\".into(),\n        content: text,  // might be None\n        tool_calls: ...,\n        tool_call_id: None,\n    }\n```\n\nThe `content` field is `Option<String>`. If `text` is None (empty content from model), it's skipped via `skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\"`. So the JSON would have `tool_calls` but no `content`. This should be valid for OpenAI API.\n\nBut wait — the issue might be with the HP's re-encoding. After the agent dispatches the tool, the agent gets the tool RESULT from the tools process. The agent then pushes the tool result to context and makes a SECOND api_chat request.\n\nBut the HP's streaming handler receives this second request, and the HP tries to parse it. The 400 is at \"column 58\" — let me check what's at column 58 of the request.\n\nActually, the error says \"HP parse error\" — this means the HP is returning an error frame, NOT that the API is returning 400. The HP returns an error frame because... something in the second request is malformed.\n\nWait, the HP parse error is when the HP fails to parse the RESPONSE from the API. Not the request from the agent. The second api_chat request from the agent is valid JSON. The HP sends it to the OpenAI API. The API returns a 400 error with details. The HP converts this to an error frame.\n\nBut actually, looking at the error: \"expected `,` or `}` at line 1 column 58\" — this is a JSON parse error. It could be either:\n1. The API returned a 400 with an error JSON that the HP can't parse\n2. The HP's streaming handler is confusing two requests\n\nLet me check what column 58 might be. The agent sends the second api_chat with tool result. The tool content might be long and contain special characters.\n\nActually wait — I bet the issue is that the tool RESULT contains newlines or special characters that break the JSON-LP format. The tool result is sent as: `{\"content\": \"...\", \"tool_call_id\": \"...\", ...}`. If the content contains `\\n`, `\"`, or `\\`, it could break the JSON on the HP side.\n\nLet me check the tool result content. For `read_file /tmp/test.txt` where the file contains \"hello world\", the result is \"hello world\". That shouldn't cause issues.\n\nHmm, but what about the SECOND streaming loop? After the agent makes the second api_chat, it creates a fresh BufReader. But the BufReader might have leftover data from the first streaming loop!\n\nWait — before the streaming loop exits on the first api_response, the BufReader has processed all frames (content_delta, tool_progress, api_response). The last frame read was the api_response. After reading it, the BufReader's buffer should be empty (no more data from HP).\n\nThe for loop iterates to the second round. A new BufReader is created. It reads from the HP connection. The API should respond with new data.\n\nBut what if the BufReader from the first iteration read MORE than just the api_response line? What if it buffered extra data (like a heartbeat or some other HP frame)?\n\nThe HP only sends frames in response to requests. The agent sends api_chat → HP streams response → agent reads all frames → agent sends next api_chat → HP streams next response. There's no interleaved data.\n\nI think the issue is simpler: the second API call is getting a 400 from DeepSeek because of the message format. The HP writes the error to the stream. The agent reads the error frame, which might be a parse error for the agent's HpToAgent deserialization.\n\nLet me focus on the error message. The agent's \"HP parse error\" means the agent couldn't parse the HP's response. The HP might have sent back an error frame that the agent can't parse. Or the HP sent back a malformed response.\n\nLet me check what the HP actually sends for an API 400 error.","signature":"e92e1d22-d1b1-4c96-a9f5-743b2f1189ea"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":315,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":445056,"output_tokens":1493,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"c632b413-ae9d-4ec0-bf93-a50cc702f251","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:26:05.764Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
