ou try again or check if the tool runtime is properly initialized? In the meantime, the file `/tmp/test.txt` may or may not exist — the system calls aren't reaching their handlers.\",\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":3915,\"completion_tokens\":160,\"total_tokens\":4075,\"prompt_cache_hit_tokens\":0,\"prompt_cache_miss_tokens\":0,\"completion_tokens_details\":null}}\n{\"type\":\"done\"}\ndsx-agent: shutdown complete (session f53d6e90, 1 turns, 15233 tokens)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b6ccb16d-ac27-435e-b589-5878473e3512","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/qaqtamsy/Desktop/dsx","sessionId":"40da231b-9053-442b-b857-d081c20c78fb","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"master","slug":"gentle-orbiting-karp"}
{"parentUuid":"f20f3ad9-3c9d-4d35-abf1-63dc4f863900","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"e4889e7b-9e60-40ab-a52a-e7f621e84091","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! The first API call worked (tool_calls received). The agent tried to execute the tool. But the tool execution FAILED with \"Unknown tool: toolname/toolname\" — which means the tools process couldn't find the tool.\n\nWait, the error is `Unknown tool: toolname/toolname`. The tool name should be `read_file` but the agent is calling it as `toolname`. Let me check the agent's tool dispatch.\n\nLooking at the agent code:\n```rust\nlet call = AgentToTools::CallReq {\n    id: id.clone(),\n    name: name.clone(),\n    action: name.clone(),  // action = name = \"read_file\"\n    args: args_val,\n    timeout_secs: Some(30),\n};\n```\n\n`name.clone()` — name should be from `tc.function.name` which is `\"read_file\"`. But the error says `toolname`. That doesn't match.\n\nWait, maybe the error happened AFTER the second API call failed (with 400), and the model was talking about fictional tool execution. Let me check more carefully.\n\nActually looking at the output:\n1. \"dsx-agent: tools → 27 definitions\" — tools initialized\n2. content_delta with reasoning\n3. tool_progress (read_file)\n4. api_response with content talking about \"Unknown tool\"\n5. done\n\nThe agent made ONE successful API call (the model returned tool_calls). But then instead of executing the tool, the agent made a SECOND API call that returned an error. Let me check if the tool was actually dispatched.\n\nWait — looking at the content of the api_response: \"It looks like all tool calls are failing with an internal error\" — this is the MODEL's text, not an agent error. The model generated this response because... the second API call with tool result FAILED.\n\nSo the sequence was:\n1. First api_chat → model returns tool_calls (read_file)\n2. Agent tries to dispatch to tools → BUT the tools process returns \"Unknown tool\"\n3. Agent records tool failure\n4. Agent makes SECOND api_chat with the error message\n5. Second api_chat SUCCEEDS (no 400) — model generates a response\n6. Done\n\nThe \"Unknown tool: toolname/toolname\" error from the tools process means the tool was NOT found. Let me check why.\n\nActually wait — looking more carefully, the model's text says \"Unknown tool: toolname/toolname\". But the tool dispatch should use the actual tool name (\"read_file\"). If the agent dispatched `read_file`, the tools process should find it (we know 27 tools including read_file are registered).\n\nUnless... the tool dispatch failed because of a NAME ISSUE. Let me check: the `name` in the agent's CallReq comes from `tc.function.name` from the parsed tool call. What if the tool call parsing is wrong?\n\nActually, looking at the HP's api_response tool_calls:\n```json\n{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"path\\\": \\\"/tmp/test.txt\\\"}\",\"id\":\"call_00_...\",\"name\":\"read_file\"}\n```\n\nAnd `parse_tool_calls` extracts:\n```rust\nlet name = tc.get(\"function\")\n    .and_then(|f| f.get(\"name\"))\n    .and_then(|v| v.as_str())\n    .unwrap_or(tc.get(\"name\").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or(\"\"));\n```\n\nThe flat format has `name` at the top level. So `tc.get(\"function\")` returns None, and the fallback `tc.get(\"name\")` returns \"read_file\". This works.\n\nBut wait — the HP's format is `{\"id\": \"call_xx\", \"name\": \"read_file\", \"arguments\": \"...\"}`. This IS flat. The agent's `parse_tool_calls` handles this correctly. So `name` should be \"read_file\".\n\nHmm, but then why does the tools process say \"Unknown tool: toolname/toolname\"? Let me check the AgentToTools::CallReq format.\n\nActually, looking at the tools process's Unknown tool message:\n```rust\nformat!(\"[ERROR] Unknown tool: {}/{}\", name, action)\n```\n\nSo both name AND action are \"toolname\". But we're using `name.clone()` for both name and action. The name comes from `tc.function.name` which should be \"read_file\".\n\nWait, I bet the issue is that the TOOL RESULT from the tools process was what was sent to the SECOND api_chat. The tools process DID execute the tool but returned an error. Let me check: does the tools process actually run the tool?\n\nActually, looking at the second api_chat: the agent makes the SECOND api_chat call AFTER the tool execution. If the tool execution returns an error, the agent sends the error as a tool result. The model then responds with \"It looks like all tool calls are failing...\"\n\nSo the FULL flow was:\n1. First api_chat → model returns tool_calls ✓\n2. Agent dispatches to tools → tools process returns UNKNOWN TOOL ERROR!\n3. Agent records tool failure → second api_chat with error\n4. Model responds about the failure\n\nThe bug is NOT the 400 (that's fixed by reasoning_content). The bug is that the tools process returns \"Unknown tool\".\n\nBut wait — the tools process HAD 27 definitions registered. Let me check: was the tool actually dispatched correctly?\n\nThe `AgentToTools::CallReq` has `name`, `action`, `args`. The tools process receives this and looks up the handler by `(name, action)`. If the handler is not found, it returns \"Unknown tool\".\n\nLet me check if the tool dispatch happens BEFORE or AFTER the API call fails.\n\nHmm, actually I need to look at the agent's code flow more carefully. After the streaming loop breaks (api_response received), the agent:\n1. Parses tool_calls from api_response\n2. For each tool_call: gates → dispatch to tools process\n3. Gets tool result from tools process\n4. Records result (push_tool_result)\n5. Continues the for loop with next api_chat\n\nSo the tool dispatch at step 2 should execute the tool in the tools process. If the tools process can't find the tool, it returns an error.\n\nBut the tools process has `read_file` registered. So why can't it find it?\n\nOh wait — I bet the issue is the `action` field. When the agent dispatches a tool:\n```rust\nlet call = AgentToTools::CallReq {\n    id: id.clone(),\n    name: name.clone(),\n    action: name.clone(),  // action = name = \"read_file\"\n    args: args_val,\n    timeout_secs: Some(30),\n};\n```\n\nThe tools process looks for `ToolKey { name: \"read_file\", action: \"read_file\" }`. But the handler was registered as `ToolKey { name: \"read_file\", action: \"\" }` — or some other action name!\n\nLet me check how tools are registered. In file.rs:\n```rust\npub fn register(mgr: &mut ToolManager) {\n    // Read file\n    let read_key = ToolKey::new(\"read_file\", \"\");\n    mgr.register(ToolHandler {\n        key: read_key,\n        ...\n    });\n```\n\nThe read_file handler is registered with key `(\"read_file\", \"\")`. But the agent dispatches with `action = name = \"read_file\"`. So the tools process looks for key `(\"read_file\", \"read_file\")` which doesn't match `(\"read_file\", \"\")`!\n\nTHIS IS THE BUG! The agent sets `action = name.clone()` for the tool dispatch, but the tools process registers tools with the default action being different.\n\nLooking at the tools process's lookup:\n```rust\nlet key = ToolKey::new(name, action);\nlet Some(handler) = self.handlers.get(&key) else {\n    // Try without action (legacy)\n    let fallback = self.handlers.iter()\n        .find(|(k, _)| k.name == name)\n        .map(|(_, h)| h);\n    return match fallback {\n        Some(h) => self.execute_handler(h, name, action, args),\n        None => format!(\"[ERROR] Unknown tool: {}/{}\", name, action),\n    };\n};\n```\n\nThere IS a fallback! If the exact key is not found, it tries to find any handler with the same name (ignoring action). `self.handlers.iter().find(|(k, _)| k.name == name)` should find the `read_file` handler regardless of action.\n\nBut wait — `self.handlers` is a `HashMap<ToolKey, ToolHandler>`. `iter()` iterates all entries and `find(|(k, _)| k.name == name)` checks the name. This should find `read_file`.\n\nUnless the fallback code i